// Generated by scripts/gen-l10n.mjs (gdetail n3, en) — 不要手改;改翻譯請改 scripts/out/l10n/en/ 再重 build。
window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N=window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N||{};window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]=Object.assign(window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]||{},{"n3-42":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜わけがない」means <b>\"there's no way ~\"</b> or <b>\"how could ~?\"</b>. Here 「わけ」 means \"reason\" or \"logic\", so 「わけがない」 literally says \"there's no logic to it.\" You're saying with total confidence that this is <b>simply impossible</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Declaring something impossible</span>Based on common sense, or on what you know about someone, you're sure this could never happen.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなに簡単に成功するわけがない。</span>(There's no way you'd succeed that easily.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こんな値段で本物が手に入るわけがない。</span>(There's no way you can get the real thing at this price.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんなに練習したんだから、負けるわけがない。</span>(After practicing that hard, there's no way we'll lose.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Pushing back on someone</span>In casual chat, it's like saying \"no way, come on.\" In speech, 「が」 is often dropped, giving 「わけない」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宝くじなんて、当たるわけないよ。</span>(A lottery? No way you'd win.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなわけないでしょ。</span>(No way, right?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly. Negative and past forms work too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>負ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>負けるわけがない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知らないわけがない</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いわけがない</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: replace 「だ」 with 「な」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好きだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きなわけがない</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: replace 「だ」 with 「の」. In more formal speech you can use 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本物だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本物のわけがない</span></span><br>To be polite, say 「<span class='gd-jp'>わけがありません</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With な-adjectives and nouns, you can't keep 「だ」 before it: <span class='gd-jp'>好きだわけがない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>本物だわけがない</span> are both wrong.<br>・「〜ないわけがない」 stacks two negatives, so it actually means <b>\"definitely will\"</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>彼が期限を守らないわけがない。</span> (There's no way he wouldn't keep the deadline = he'll definitely keep it.)<br>・English \"can't\" covers two different ideas, and Japanese separates them. \"I can't go out and play, I have an exam tomorrow\" means you <b>aren't in a position to / shouldn't</b>, so you need わけにはいかない: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は試験だから、遊びに行くわけにはいかない。</span> (I have an exam tomorrow, so I can't go out and play.) If you wrote <span class='gd-jp'>遊びに行くわけがない</span>, it would become \"there's no way I'd go out and play.\"<br>・The tone is very strong, with a \"what nonsense are you saying?\" feel. Answering a boss or an elder with <span class='gd-jp'>そんなわけないです</span> sounds harsh. Try <span class='gd-jp'>それはないと思います</span> instead.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Situation: you offer Tanaka some cake and he says no thanks. Someone nearby asks you, \"Does Tanaka hate cake?\"<br><b>わけがない</b> = <b>strong denial</b>, in a \"no way, come on!\" tone:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんがケーキが嫌いなわけがないよ。毎日食べてるもん。</span>(There's no way Tanaka hates cake. He eats it every day!)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>はずがない</b> = <b>reasoned conclusion</b> that it shouldn't be so, in a calmer tone:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは甘いものが大好きだから、ケーキが嫌いなはずがない。</span>(Tanaka loves sweets, so he can't possibly hate cake.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The two are often interchangeable. The difference is that はずがない leans toward \"going by the clues I have, it shouldn't be so,\" while わけがない leans toward \"it just makes no sense,\" and in speech it carries stronger emotion.<br><b>わけではない</b> = <b>\"it's not that ~\"</b>. It only corrects a misunderstanding and has no sense of \"impossible\" at all. If Tanaka explained himself, he'd say:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>甘いものが嫌いなわけではない。今はおなかがいっぱいなだけだ。</span>(It's not that I dislike sweets. I'm just full right now.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: for \"no way,\" use わけがない. For \"by all logic it shouldn't be,\" use はずがない. For \"it's not that ~,\" use わけではない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The 「わけ」 family also includes <span class='gd-jp'>わけではない</span> (it's not that ~) and <span class='gd-jp'>わけにはいかない</span> (can't ~). Add the closest in meaning, <span class='gd-jp'>はずがない</span>, and studying them together is the best way to avoid mixing them up.</div>","n3-43":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜わけではない」means <b>\"it's not that ~\"</b> or <b>\"that doesn't mean ~\"</b>. The other person may assume something, and you step in to clarify: \"It's not what you think.\" It's like English \"It's not that I dislike it, it's just…\", and the real reason usually follows.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Clearing up a misunderstanding</span>The other person seems to have misread you, so you set things straight.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>反対しているわけではありません。ちょっと心配なだけです。</span>(It's not that I'm against it. I'm just a little worried.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>別に怒っているわけじゃないよ。</span>(It's not like I'm angry.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not everything is like that</span>Used with 「<span class='gd-jp'>みんな</span>」, 「<span class='gd-jp'>いつも</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>全部</span>」, it means \"not necessarily always so.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本人がみんな納豆が好きなわけではない。</span>(Not every Japanese person likes natto.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>高い服がいつもいいわけではない。</span>(Expensive clothes aren't always better.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Softly declining</span>You don't want to shut things down outright, so you first deny a possible misunderstanding, then give your reason.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料理が嫌いなわけじゃないけど、毎日はちょっと…。</span>(It's not that I hate cooking, but every day is a bit much...)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the plain form before it, meaning the form without ます (dictionary, ない and た forms all work).<br><b>Verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くわけではない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったわけではない</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいわけではない</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add <b>な</b>　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>嫌い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>嫌いなわけではない</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add <b>な</b>, or use 「という」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生というわけではない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>In speech it often becomes <b>わけじゃない</b>. For a more polite tone, use <b>わけではありません</b> or <b>わけじゃありません</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After な-adjectives and nouns you <b>can't use だ</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>嫌いなわけではない</span>, not 「嫌いだわけではない」.<br>・The 「わけ」 here doesn't mean \"reason,\" so don't translate it as \"there's no reason.\"<br>・It's softer than a plain denial: <span class='gd-jp'>嫌いじゃない</span> means \"I don't dislike it,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>嫌いなわけじゃない</span> means \"it's not that I dislike it (there's another reason),\" so an explanation usually follows.<br>・With a negative before it, it's a double negative: <span class='gd-jp'>行きたくないわけじゃない。</span> (It's not that I don't want to go.) This means you actually kind of want to go.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All talking about whether a shop is expensive:<br><b>わけではない</b> = <b>it's not that</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>この店、高いわけではないけど、ちょっと遠い。</span> (It's not that this shop is expensive, it's just a bit far.)<br><b>わけがない</b> = <b>no way</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>100円ショップだから、高いわけがない。</span> (It's a 100-yen shop, so there's no way it's expensive.)<br><b>わけだ</b> = <b>no wonder</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>有名なシェフの店か。高いわけだ。</span> (Oh, it's a famous chef's restaurant. No wonder it's expensive.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use わけではない for \"it's not that ~,\" わけがない for \"no way ~,\" and わけだ for \"no wonder ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ないことはない</span>」 (it's not that I can't ~) is close in meaning to 「〜ないわけではない」, and both carry a \"well, I guess it's possible\" tone: <span class='gd-jp'>辛い料理も食べられないことはない。</span> (It's not that I can't eat spicy food either.)</div>","n3-8":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にとって」means <b>\"for ~\"</b> or <b>\"to ~\"</b> in the sense of \"from ~'s point of view.\" First you say whose perspective you're taking, then you say how <b>important, difficult, or good</b> something is for that person. The same thing can feel completely different depending on who you ask, and にとって marks \"whose feeling this is.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私にとって、家族が一番大切です。</span>(To me, family is the most important thing.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Just add にとって directly to a noun. It's most often a person, but animals, companies, and countries work too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>私</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>私にとって</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>外国人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>外国人にとって</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>外国人にとって、敬語は難しい。</span>(For foreigners, honorific language is difficult.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>猫にとって、この部屋は暑すぎるかもしれない。</span>(For a cat, this room might be too hot.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>にとって</span>can go at the start of a sentence or in the middle. It means \"from this person's standpoint, this is how things are.\" A comma after it is optional.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この経験は私にとって大きな財産だ。</span>(This experience is a great asset to me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>にとっては</span>adds は, which brings in a sense of contrast: \"for this person it's so, but maybe not for others.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大人にとっては短い時間でも、子供にとっては長い。</span>(Even a time that's short for adults is long for children.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>にとっての＋noun</span>packs \"the ... for ~\" into a single noun phrase, so you need to add <b>の</b> in between.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供にとっての幸せを考えてみましょう。</span>(Let's think about what happiness means for children.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows にとって is an <b>evaluation</b>: 大切, 難しい, 便利, 貴重, 意味がある, and so on. It can't be followed by an <b>attitude or action directed at a person</b>. To say \"The teacher is kind to the students,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>先生は学生に対して親切だ</span>. Saying 先生は学生にとって親切だ is wrong.<br>・English \"for\" and \"to\" cover a lot of ground, but Japanese splits those meanings into different patterns. Don't write にとって every time you'd say \"for\" or \"to.\"<br>・The matter of の: <span class='gd-jp'>私にとって、幸せは家族と過ごす時間です。</span> is completely correct, because にとって modifies the whole sentence. You only need <span class='gd-jp'>にとっての</span> when you turn \"the ... for ~\" into a single noun block, for example when it comes before を as an object.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these involve \"students,\" but each grammar point changes the meaning:<br><b>にとって</b> = an <b>evaluation</b> from the students' point of view: <span class='gd-jp'>学生にとって、この先生は怖い存在だ。</span> (To the students, this teacher is a scary presence.)<br><b>に対して</b> = an attitude or action <b>directed toward</b> the students: <span class='gd-jp'>この先生は学生に対して厳しい。</span> (This teacher is strict with the students.)<br><b>について</b> = the <b>topic</b> of speech or thought: <span class='gd-jp'>先生は学生について話した。</span> (The teacher talked about the students.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another one that's easy to mix up is <b>として</b>, which means \"in the capacity of ~\" or \"as ~\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>親として、子供を守りたい。</span> (As a parent, I want to protect my child.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>親にとって、子供は宝物だ。</span> (To a parent, a child is a treasure.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if what follows is a feeling or evaluation like \"important, difficult, precious,\" use にとって. If it's an attitude toward a person like \"strict, kind, opposed,\" use に対して. If it's \"talk, investigate, discuss,\" use について. If you want to say \"as a ~,\" use として.</div>","n3-28":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に関して」means <b>\"regarding ~\" or \"concerning ~\"</b>. It means about the same as 「〜について」, but it sounds more <b>formal and stiffer</b>. In casual chat you'd say \"about that,\" while a company notice or a news report would say \"regarding this matter.\" You'll see it a lot in news, company notices, school announcements, and customer service replies.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It always follows a <b>noun</b>. What comes next decides which form you use:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a clause</span>Noun + に関して, followed by a full sentence (what someone does, how things are).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>事故の原因に関して、今調べています。</span>(Regarding the cause of the accident, we are investigating now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この件に関して、何か質問はありますか。</span>(Regarding this matter, do you have any questions?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a noun</span>Noun + に関する + noun modifies the noun that follows, giving \"a ○○ related to ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>環境問題に関する本を探しています。</span>(I'm looking for a book about environmental issues.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化に関するレポートを書きました。</span>(I wrote a report on Japanese culture.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before a noun you can also say <span class='gd-jp'>に関しての</span>, as in <span class='gd-jp'>環境問題に関しての本</span>. The meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に関しては</span>Adding は gives the feel of \"as for the ○○ part,\" and it's often used when explaining something or answering a question.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料金に関しては、こちらをご覧ください。</span>(As for the fees, please see here.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に関しまして</span>A more polite version. You'll hear it often in business calls and formal explanations.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この件に関しまして、後ほどご連絡いたします。</span>(Regarding this matter, we will contact you later.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Before a noun, you need <b>に関する</b> or <b>に関しての</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>環境問題に関して本</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>環境問題に関する本</span>.<br>・It's very formal, so using it with friends sounds odd, as if you were in a meeting. When friends talk about something like yesterday's movie, <span class='gd-jp'>について</span> is enough.<br>・English \"to\" or \"toward\" doesn't always map onto に関して. In \"be polite to customers,\" the attitude is directed at someone, so you need <span class='gd-jp'>に対して</span>, not に関して.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>について</b> = \"about ~.\" It works in both everyday and formal settings, so it has the widest range. <b>に関して</b> means the same thing, just more formal.<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日の試合について話そうよ。</span> (Let's talk about yesterday's game: chatting with a friend.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日の試合に関して、監督がコメントを発表した。</span> (Regarding yesterday's game, the coach released a statement: news.)<br>One more thing to remember: before a noun, について becomes <span class='gd-jp'>についての</span>, as in <span class='gd-jp'>日本についての本</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に対して</b> = \"toward ~,\" about whom an attitude or action is directed.<br><span class='gd-jp'>お客様に対して、丁寧な言葉を使います。</span> (We use polite language toward customers.)<br>You can't swap in に関して here, because you're not talking about the topic of customers but speaking directly to them.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にとって</b> = \"for ~ / to ~,\" evaluating something from someone's standpoint.<br><span class='gd-jp'>私にとって、家族が一番大切です。</span> (To me, family is the most important thing.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if you can say \"regarding ~\" or \"related to ~\" in English, use に関して (formal) or について (everyday). Use に対して for \"toward someone,\" and にとって for \"for someone / from someone's point of view.\"</div>","n3-3":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>〜について</span> is the Japanese way of saying <b>“about ~” or “regarding ~”</b>. You put out a topic first, then follow it with a verb like “talk, ask, look up, think.” That tells the listener what the <b>content</b> of your conversation or research is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この映画についてどう思う？</span>(What do you think about this movie?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Followed by a verb</b>: noun + について<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化について調べる</span></span> (look into Japanese culture)<br><b>Followed by a noun</b>: noun + <b>についての</b> + noun<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化についての本</span></span> (a book about Japanese culture)<br><b>After a whole sentence</b>: add 「こと」 or 「か」 first to turn it into a topic.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留学する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留学することについて</span></span> (about studying abroad)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>どうする</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>どうするかについて話し合う</span></span> (discuss what to do)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Memory aid: <span class='gd-jp'>について</span> comes from 「に＋就いて」, which carries the feeling of “centering on this matter,” so it points to a subject you're discussing.</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>について＋verb</span>The basic pattern. The verb is usually something like “talk, write, ask, look up, think.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>将来について考えたことある？</span>(Have you ever thought about your future?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>旅行の予定について話しましょう。</span>(Let's talk about our travel plans.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>についての＋noun</span>Modifies the noun that follows, like English “a ~ about …”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本の歴史についての本を読んでいます。</span>(I'm reading a book about Japanese history.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>については</span>Pulls the topic out to talk about it on its own, with the feel of “as for ~” or “as far as ~ goes.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料金については、受付で聞いてください。</span>(As for the fees, please ask at the reception desk.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>につきまして</span>The very polite version. You'll hear it a lot from customer service and in company announcements.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この件につきましては、後ほどご連絡いたします。</span>(Regarding this matter, we will contact you later.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When a noun follows, you must add 「の」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化について本</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>日本の文化についての本</span>.<br>・When a verb comes before it, add こと first: <span class='gd-jp'>日本で働くについて</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>日本で働くことについて</span>.<br>・English “about” is very flexible, but <span class='gd-jp'>について</span> needs an action tied to the “content” (talk, ask, look up, think, discuss). Watch out: <span class='gd-jp'>先生について行く</span> is a different expression meaning “follow the teacher,” not “go about the teacher.”<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>私について</span> (about me) and <span class='gd-jp'>私にとって</span> (for me / to me) look almost the same, but the meanings are completely different.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even when you're talking about “children,” changing the phrase changes the meaning a lot:<br><b>について</b> = the <b>topic</b> being discussed: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもの教育について話す。</span> (talk about children's education)<br><b>に対して</b> = an attitude or action <b>aimed at someone</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもに対して優しい。</span> (kind to children)<br><b>にとって</b> = an evaluation from <b>someone's standpoint</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもにとって大切なことだ。</span> (It's something important for children.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>There's also <b>に関して</b>, which means almost the same thing and can be swapped in most of the time. It's just more formal and stiffer, and common in announcements, news and reports:<br><span class='gd-jp'>新しいルールについて説明します。</span> (I'll explain the new rules. Everyday speech)<br><span class='gd-jp'>新しいルールに関して説明します。</span> (Regarding the new rules, I will give an explanation. More formal)<br>When modifying a noun, the counterpart of <span class='gd-jp'>についての</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>に関する</span>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for “what we're talking about or looking up,” use について; for “how someone is toward someone,” use に対して; for “from someone's point of view,” use にとって; and in formal writing where you want “regarding,” you can switch to に関して.</div>","n3-2":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に対して」 has two meanings:<br>The first is <b>“toward ~” or “directed at ~”</b>. It works like an arrow, showing who or what an attitude, action or reaction is aimed at.<br>The second is <b>“in contrast to ~”</b>, which sets two sides next to each other: A is like this, but B is like that.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Target of an attitude</span>Being strict, gentle or polite toward someone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は学生に対して厳しい。</span>(The teacher is strict with the students.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は誰に対しても優しい。</span>(She's kind to everyone.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Target of a response</span>Answering or reacting to a question, opinion or proposal.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>質問に対して、丁寧に答えた。</span>(In response to the question, I answered carefully.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新しいルールに対して、反対の意見が多い。</span>(Many people oppose the new rules.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Contrast</span>Use it when two sides are clearly different.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>兄が静かなのに対して、弟はにぎやかだ。</span>(My older brother is quiet, whereas my younger brother is lively.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>東京は雨だったのに対して、大阪は晴れていた。</span>(It was raining in Tokyo, whereas it was sunny in Osaka.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The “toward ~” use</b>: attach に対して directly to a noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>お客様</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お客様に対して</span></span><br>When a noun follows, change it to <b>に対する</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>先生に対して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>先生に対する態度</span> (attitude toward the teacher)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The “in contrast to ~” use</b>: put a whole sentence before it, then add <b>のに対して</b>.<br>Verbs and い-adjectives connect directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>よく食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よく食べるのに対して</span></span><br>な-adjectives and nouns need 「な」 first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなのに対して</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生なのに対して</span></span><br>For past events, use the past form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったのに対して</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The particle before it is <b>に</b>, not を: 「先生を対して」 is wrong.<br>・Before a noun, use <b>に対する</b>: 「先生に対して態度」 is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>先生に対する態度</span>.<br>・The contrast use always needs 「の」 before it: 「静かに対して」 is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>静かなのに対して</span>.<br>・English “to,” “for” and “toward” are very broad, and not every one of them can become に対して. “Good for your health” is <span class='gd-jp'>体にいい</span>; “interested in Japanese culture” is <span class='gd-jp'>日本文化に興味がある</span>; “for me / to me” is <span class='gd-jp'>私にとって</span>.<br>・に対して sounds fairly formal. In casual chat, “nice to my friends” is perfectly natural as <span class='gd-jp'>友達に優しい</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even when the subject is the same “new rules,” a different phrase changes the meaning:<br><b>について</b> = what the topic is (about ~): <span class='gd-jp'>新しいルールについて説明した。</span> (I explained the new rules.)<br><b>に対して</b> = who or what an attitude or reaction is aimed at (toward ~): <span class='gd-jp'>新しいルールに対して、文句を言う人が多い。</span> (Many people are complaining about the new rules.)<br><b>にとって</b> = an evaluation from someone's standpoint (for ~ / to ~): <span class='gd-jp'>学生にとって、新しいルールは厳しすぎる。</span> (For students, the new rules are too strict.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>This pair is the easiest to mix up:<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生は学生に対して厳しい。</span> (The teacher is strict with the students: the teacher is the one being strict.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>この試験は学生にとって難しい。</span> (This exam is hard for students: the students are the ones who find it hard.)<br>Also, <b>に関して</b> means the same as について, just more formal.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for “what topic,” use について; for “how someone acts toward someone, or reacts to something,” use に対して; for “how it is from someone's standpoint,” use にとって.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, study <span class='gd-jp'>〜にとって</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に関して</span> so you can sort out all of these “toward / about” expressions at once.</div>","n3-41":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜わけだ」 is the Japanese for <b>“no wonder…” or “so that’s why…”</b>, and it can also mean <b>“in other words…”</b>. You already know a fact, then you learn the reason behind it (or you do the math on the information). When the two click together and you think “ahh, that makes sense,” you use わけだ.<br>(<span class='gd-jp'>わけ</span> is written 「訳」 in kanji, and it means reason or logic.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No wonder</span>Something has seemed odd for a while, and now you know why, so it all makes sense. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>道理で</span> (no wonder) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜のか</span> (oh, so it’s…).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道理で寒いわけだ。雪が降っている。</span> (No wonder it’s so cold. It’s snowing.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>3年も住んでいたのか。日本語が上手なわけだ。</span> (You lived there for three years? No wonder your Japanese is so good.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In other words</span>You restate the earlier information in a different way, or work out a result, as if saying “so the conclusion is…”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>8時に出て9時に着いたから、1時間かかったわけだ。</span> (I left at 8 and arrived at 9, so in other words it took an hour.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「明日は会社が休みです。」「じゃあ、ゆっくり寝られるわけですね。」</span> (“The office is closed tomorrow.” “Then that means you can get a good sleep.”)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます; past and negative both work)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>眠れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>眠れないわけだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>かかった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>かかったわけだ</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いわけだ</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: keep the 「な」<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>上手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>上手なわけだ</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「な」 (more formal writing uses 「である」)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みなわけだ</span></span><br>To be polite to someone, switch to <span class='gd-jp'>わけです</span>. In conversation it’s often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>わけか</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>わけね</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After な-adjectives and nouns, you <b>can’t attach わけ directly</b>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>上手なわけだ</span>, not 「上手わけだ」.<br>・わけだ isn’t a casual guess. There has to be a <b>fact or reason you already know</b>, and you’re just connecting it to the result.<br>・When it’s used about someone else’s shortcomings, as in <span class='gd-jp'>だから失敗したわけだ</span> (no wonder it failed), it can sound a bit like “well, of course,” so be careful with seniors and customers.<br>・Don’t mix it up with <span class='gd-jp'>わけがない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>わけではない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>わけにはいかない</span>. They differ by only a few characters but mean completely different things.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar-looking forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>はずだ</b> also means judging based on a reason. The key difference is <b>when the result isn’t confirmed yet</b>: if you’re only inferring what should be true, you can only use はずだ, not わけだ. Take the same situation: Mr. Tanaka lived in the US for ten years.<br><span class='gd-tag'>わけだ</span>You’ve already heard him speak English and found it fluent, and now you know why:<br><span class='gd-jp'>10年もアメリカにいたのか。英語がうまいわけだ。</span> (He was in the US for ten years? No wonder his English is so good.)<br><span class='gd-tag'>はずだ</span>You haven’t heard him speak English yet and are only inferring (わけだ doesn’t work here):<br><span class='gd-jp'>10年もアメリカにいたから、英語がうまいはずだ。</span> (He was in the US for ten years, so his English should be good.)<br>Another one: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は来ると言っていたから、来るはずだ。</span> (He said he’d come, so he should come.) The person hasn’t shown up and the outcome isn’t confirmed yet, so it’s はずだ.<br>That said, for the “no wonder” use, はずだ also works. <span class='gd-jp'>道理で寒いはずだ。</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>10年もアメリカにいたのか。英語がうまいはずだ。</span> are both perfectly natural and mean about the same as わけだ.<br>If you don’t even have a reason and are just guessing “probably,” that’s <span class='gd-jp'>だろう</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>でしょう</span>, not はずだ or わけだ.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick check: when the result isn’t confirmed and you’re only inferring, use はずだ only. When the result is already in front of you and you’re saying “no wonder / I see,” both わけだ and はずだ work.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn the rest of the わけ family: <span class='gd-jp'>わけがない</span> (there’s no way), <span class='gd-jp'>わけではない</span> (it’s not that…), <span class='gd-jp'>わけにはいかない</span> (can’t, for reasons of principle or circumstance). Also, when you finish explaining a reason and want to wrap up, you often say <span class='gd-jp'>というわけで</span> (so, that’s why…): <span class='gd-jp'>というわけで、明日は休みます。</span> (So that’s why I’m taking tomorrow off.)</div>","n3-44":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜わけにはいかない」 means <b>“I can't (just) ~”</b>. The point isn't that you're unable to do it. It's that, given what's reasonable, what people expect, or your position, <b>it wouldn't be right to do it</b>. Your boss is still working overtime and you think, “I can't just leave before him.” That's the feeling.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't do</span>Dictionary form + わけにはいかない<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰るわけにはいかない</span></span> (I can't just go home)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't not do</span>ない form + わけにはいかない means <b>you have to do it</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないわけにはいかない</span></span> (I can't not go = I have to go)<br>The ない form is the “don't ~” form of a verb, for example <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span></span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>With seniors or guests, be more polite by changing いかない to いきません: <span class='gd-jp'>休むわけにはいきません</span> (I can't take the day off)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't just do</span>You shouldn't do it, because of the reasons or your position.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>車で来たから、お酒を飲むわけにはいかない。</span> (I came by car, so I can't drink.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は大事な会議だから、休むわけにはいかない。</span> (I have an important meeting tomorrow, so I can't take the day off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't not do</span>You don't really want to, but social obligation and responsibility are there, so you do it anyway.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先輩に頼まれたから、手伝わないわけにはいかない。</span> (My senior asked me, so I can't not help.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束したから、行かないわけにはいかない。</span> (I promised, so I can't not go.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>わけにもいかない</span>Change は to も and it becomes “I can't ~ <b>either</b>.” It's often used when you're torn between two things.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く帰りたいけど、先に帰るわけにもいかない。</span> (I'd love to go home early, but it wouldn't feel right to leave before everyone else.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In fast speech, は is sometimes dropped, giving <span class='gd-jp'>わけにいかない</span>. The meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't use it for things you can't do because of skill or ability. “I can't swim” is <span class='gd-jp'>泳げない</span>, not 泳ぐわけにはいかない.<br>・It ends in <b>いかない</b>, not いけない. 「わけにはいけない」 is wrong.<br>・Whether the verb before it is affirmative or negative flips the meaning: <span class='gd-jp'>飲むわけにはいかない</span> = I can't drink; <span class='gd-jp'>飲まないわけにはいかない</span> = I can't not drink.<br>・Don't use it for simply “not wanting to.” That's <span class='gd-jp'>行きたくない</span>. Behind わけにはいかない there is always a “by rights” reason.<br>・It's mostly used for <b>your own</b> situation: the speaker feels “I can't very well ~.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Let's take the same topic, a company get-together, and see the difference:<br><b>Negative potential form</b> = you can't, because of circumstances: <span class='gd-jp'>熱があって、飲み会に行けない。</span> (I have a fever, so I can't go.)<br><b>わけにはいかない</b> = you can't very well, because of obligation or position: <span class='gd-jp'>部長も来るから、行かないわけにはいかない。</span> (The department head is coming too, so I can't not go.)<br><b>わけがない</b> = I judge it impossible: <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは忙しいから、来るわけがない。</span> (Tanaka is so busy, there's no way he'll come.)<br><b>わけではない</b> = it's not that…: <span class='gd-jp'>飲み会が嫌いなわけではない。</span> (It's not that I dislike get-togethers.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: “can't do it” takes the potential form, “I can't very well ~” is わけにはいかない, “no way” is わけがない, and “it's not that…” is わけではない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The N2 pattern 「〜ざるを得ない」 also means “have no choice but to ~,” but it sounds stiffer and more formal, like written language: <span class='gd-jp'>行かざるを得ない。</span> (I have no choice but to go.)</div>","n3-61":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜べきだ」 means <b>“should ~” or “ought to ~”</b>. It isn't a guess about what will happen. It's about <b>right and wrong</b>: you feel this is the right thing to do. Just like we say “you should pay back money you borrow,” in Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>借りたお金は返すべきだ。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verb dictionary form + べきだ</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>守る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>守るべきだ</span></span> (should follow/keep)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>謝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>謝るべきだ</span></span> (should apologize)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Saying “shouldn't”</b>: Still use the dictionary form, and change 「だ」 to 「ではない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言うべきではない</span></span> (shouldn't say)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>する is special</b>: both <span class='gd-jp'>するべき</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>すべき</span> are correct. You'll see <span class='gd-jp'>すべき</span> more in writing and the news, and you'll hear both in speech.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>確認する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>確認すべきだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: add 「であるべきだ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>公平</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>公平であるべきだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は公平であるべきだ。</span> (Teachers should be fair.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>べきだ／べきです</span>Say what you feel someone ought to do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束は守るべきだ。</span> (You should keep your promises.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>若いうちに、いろいろな経験をするべきです。</span> (You should try all kinds of experiences while you're young.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>べきではない</span>Things you feel one shouldn't do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人の悪口を言うべきではない。</span> (You shouldn't badmouth other people.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>べきだった</span>Looking back with regret: you should have done it, but you didn't. Conversely, <span class='gd-jp'>べきではなかった</span> means “shouldn't have done it.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あのとき、ちゃんと謝るべきだった。</span> (I should have apologized properly back then.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんなこと、言うべきではなかった。</span> (I really shouldn't have said that.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>べき＋名詞</span>“The … that one should ~,” placed directly before a noun.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今やるべきことは何だろう。</span> (I wonder what I should be doing now.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't say 「言わないべき」 for the negative. Say <span class='gd-jp'>言うべきではない</span>. Before べき, always use the dictionary form.<br>・する doesn't become 「しべき」. It's only <span class='gd-jp'>するべき</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>すべき</span>.<br>・The tone is strong, with a preachy “this is the right way” feel. Saying 「〜べきです」 straight to a boss or an elder can easily sound like you're lecturing them. To give advice, use <span class='gd-jp'>〜たほうがいいと思います</span> instead. Even when you do use べき, at least add <span class='gd-jp'>と思います</span> to soften it.<br>・べき is what <b>the speaker personally feels</b> one should do, not a rule. For things you absolutely must do, like laws and school rules, use <span class='gd-jp'>〜なければならない</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>日本では、車は左側を走らなければならない。</span> (In Japan, cars must drive on the left.) Swapping in べき here would sound like a personal opinion, which is odd.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>べき vs はず</b>: English “should” has two meanings, and Japanese splits them into two expressions.<br><b>べき</b> = <b>ought to</b> (a judgment of right and wrong)<br><b>はず</b> = <b>should be / is expected to</b> (an inference)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは約束を守るべきだ。</span> (Tanaka should keep his promises: I think he ought to.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは約束を守るはずだ。</span> (Tanaka should keep his promise: I expect he will.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>べきではない vs ものではない</b>: Both can be translated as “shouldn't.” <span class='gd-jp'>ものではない</span> is about social common sense, something everyone knows, much like an elder telling a child “people just don't do that.” <span class='gd-jp'>べきではない</span> is more the speaker's own judgment and opinion.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束を破るものではない。</span> (You just don't break promises: common sense.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束を破るべきではない。</span> (You shouldn't break promises: I think it's wrong.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use べき for “ought to / ought not to,” and はず for “should be / is expected to.” When you want the “people just don't do that” tone of common sense, use ものではない.</div>","n3-13":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The most common meaning of 「〜うちに」 is <b>\"while ~\"</b>, as in \"strike while the iron is hot.\" The current state <b>will change later</b>, so you do it quickly while there's still time. It has a second meaning too: <b>\"as you keep doing ~, before you know it…\"</b>. Both are very common.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>While ~ lasts</span>The current state (hot, young, still a student) won't last forever, so you take the chance.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>熱いうちに召し上がってください。</span>(Please eat it while it's hot.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>学生のうちに、たくさん本を読んでおきたい。</span>(I want to read lots of books while I'm still a student.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>両親が元気なうちに、一緒に旅行したい。</span>(I want to travel with my parents while they're still healthy.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before ~ happens</span>With the ない form + うちに, it means \"before something happens,\" with a bit of a sense of hurry.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>暗くならないうちに帰ろう。</span>(Let's go home before it gets dark.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忘れないうちに、メモしておこう。</span>(Let me jot it down before I forget.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before you know it…</span>You keep doing something, and as you do, a change shows up.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>話しているうちに、仲良くなった。</span>(We got close as we talked.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度も聞いているうちに、歌詞を覚えた。</span>(After listening to it many times, I ended up memorizing the lyrics.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>知らないうちに寝てしまった。</span>(I fell asleep without realizing it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb ている form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話している</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話しているうちに</span></span><br>Verb ない form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないうちに</span></span><br>い-adjectives: attach directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>熱い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>熱いうちに</span></span><br>な-adjectives: add な　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気なうちに</span></span><br>Nouns: add の　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生のうちに</span></span><br>The dictionary form works too, but it's mostly with verbs that describe a \"state,\" like <span class='gd-jp'>日本にいるうちに</span> (while you're still in Japan).</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't attach the た form: <b>×話したうちに</b>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>話しているうちに</span>.<br>・Don't mix up な-adjectives and nouns: <span class='gd-jp'>元気なうちに</span>, not 元気のうちに; <span class='gd-jp'>学生のうちに</span>, not 学生なうちに. い-adjectives take no の: <span class='gd-jp'>若いうちに</span>, not 若いのうちに.<br>・Don't be thrown by the negative in 「ないうちに」. It means \"<b>before it happens</b>,\" not \"while not doing ~.\"<br>・The \"while ~\" use is usually followed by something you want or plan to do: <span class='gd-jp'>〜ておこう</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜たい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜てください</span>.<br>・The \"before you know it\" use is followed by a change that happens naturally, often <span class='gd-jp'>〜なった</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜てしまった</span>, not something you do on purpose.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>間に</b> simply means \"during that period,\" with no special feeling: <span class='gd-jp'>日本にいる間に、富士山に登りたい。</span> (I want to climb Mt. Fuji during my time in Japan.)<br><b>うちに</b> carries the feeling of \"<b>while you still can, because the chance will be gone later</b>\": <span class='gd-jp'>日本にいるうちに、富士山に登りたい。</span> (I want to climb Mt. Fuji while I'm still in Japan.)<br>If the time span has a clear start and end, like \"between 3 and 5 o'clock,\" you can only use <span class='gd-jp'>3時から5時の間に</span>, not うちに.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>前に</b> and <b>ないうちに</b> look alike too:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降る前に帰ろう。</span> (Let's go home before it rains: simply the order of events.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降らないうちに帰ろう。</span> (Let's get home before it starts raining: more of a sense of hurry.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you mean \"hurry up while there's still time,\" use うちに; if you're just giving the time period, use 間に; if you're only stating the order of events, use 前に.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at 「〜間に」 and 「〜最中に」 (in the middle of ~ing). Compare all three together and you won't mix up the ways of talking about time.</div>","n3-20":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ば〜ほど」 means <b>\"the more ~, the more ~.\"</b> Think \"the more you practice, the better you get\" or \"the more I think about it, the more annoyed I am.\" The more you do the first thing, or the higher its degree, the more obvious the result becomes.<br>Japanese does this by <b>saying the same word twice</b>: the first time in the ば form (the \"if ~\" form), the second time in its plain form, and then adding ほど.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>練習すればするほど上手になる。</span>(The more you practice, the better you get.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs</span>First change the verb to the ば form, then add the dictionary form of the same verb, then ほど.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the え-row sound of the same row, then add ば.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → む becomes め → <span class='gd-jp'>読めば読むほど</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行けば行くほど</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop る and add れば.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べれば食べるほど</span></span><br><b>Group 3</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すればするほど</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来れば来るほど</span>（くればくるほど）</span><br>For \"noun + する\" verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span>, the second part usually keeps just する: <span class='gd-jp'>勉強すればするほど</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>い-adjectives</span>Drop い and add ければ, then follow with the original い-adjective + ほど.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安ければ安いほど</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>な-adjectives</span>Use なら the first time, then な + ほど the second time.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなら静かなほど</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語は勉強すればするほど面白くなる。</span>(The more you study Japanese, the more interesting it gets.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この本は読めば読むほど好きになる。</span>(The more I read this book, the more I like it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>返事は早ければ早いほどいいです。</span>(The sooner you reply, the better.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部屋は静かなら静かなほどいい。</span>(The quieter the room, the better.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>早ければ早いほどいい</span>」 and 「<span class='gd-jp'>多ければ多いほどいい</span>」 are almost set phrases. Memorize them whole and they'll come in handy.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Both parts must be the <b>same word</b>. English says \"the more A, the more B,\" but Japanese says A twice and puts B in the clause that follows.<br>・The second one uses the plain form, with no second ば: it's <span class='gd-jp'>考えれば考えるほど</span>, not 考えれば考えればほど.<br>・The ば form of <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>よければ</span>, not いければ: <span class='gd-jp'>よければよいほど</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来れば</span> is read くれば, not きれば.<br>・With な-adjectives, the first part is なら and the second is な. They're different, so don't write <span class='gd-jp'>静かなら静かならほど</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ば〜ほど</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>練習すればするほど上手になる。</span> (The more you practice, the better you get.)<br><b>〜ほど</b> (dropping the first ば): <span class='gd-jp'>練習するほど上手になる。</span> (Same meaning.)<br>The version without ば is shorter; the version with ば makes the \"the more… the more…\" feeling clearer and stronger.<br><b>〜とともに</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>年をとるとともに、体力が落ちる。</span> (As we get older, our stamina declines.)<br>Compare: <span class='gd-jp'>年をとればとるほど、体力が落ちる。</span> (The older you get, the more your stamina drops.)<br>とともに means \"along with / as ~\" and is more formal and bookish; ば〜ほど means \"the more ~, the more ~\" and is common in both speech and writing.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you want to say \"the more ~, the more ~,\" use ば〜ほど; use とともに only when you want the more formal \"as ~\" or \"along with ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>On its own, 「〜ほど」 also means \"to the extent of ~,\" for example <span class='gd-jp'>泣きたいほど痛い</span> (it hurts so much I want to cry). Check out the 「〜ほど」 card next.</div>","n3-33":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜っぽい」is a lot like English <b>\"-ish\"</b> or <b>\"-y\"</b>, or \"kind of like ~\". Think of calling someone \"childish\" or saying a shirt looks \"cheap\". After certain verbs, it means <b>\"tends to ~ / is prone to ~\"</b>, as in \"forgetful\" or \"quick to get angry\". It's very casual and you'll hear it all the time in everyday conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Seems like ~</span>Noun + っぽい: it isn't actually that thing, but it gives that impression.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はもう30歳なのに、子供っぽい。</span>(He's already 30, but he's so childish.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はちょっと風邪っぽい。</span>(I feel a bit like I'm coming down with a cold today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>~-ish in color, with a hint of ~</span>い-adjective + っぽい: often used for colors or textures.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>白っぽいシャツを買った。</span>(I bought a whitish shirt.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Tends to ~</span>Verb + っぽい: describes a person's character.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は年を取って、怒りっぽくなった。</span>(My dad has gotten older and become easily irritated.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は飽きっぽいから、何をしても続かない。</span>(I get bored easily, so I can't stick with anything.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: just add っぽい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供っぽい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>風邪</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>風邪っぽい</span>(seems like a slight cold)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final い, then add っぽい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → drop い → <span class='gd-jp'>安っぽい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>白い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>白っぽい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change to the ます form, drop ます, then add っぽい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れっぽい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>怒ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>怒りっぽい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once attached, 「〜っぽい」becomes a whole い-adjective and conjugates just like <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れっぽい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れっぽくなった</span>(became forgetful)</span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供っぽい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供っぽくない</span>(not childish)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't attach it to just any verb. The common ones are few: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れっぽい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>怒りっぽい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>飽きっぽい</span>. Don't invent words like <span class='gd-jp'>食べっぽい</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>安っぽい</span> doesn't mean \"inexpensive.\" It means \"looks cheap, tacky,\" and it's negative: <span class='gd-jp'>このバッグは安っぽい。</span>(This bag looks cheap.) To say something is low-priced, use <span class='gd-jp'>安い</span>.<br>・It often carries a slightly critical tone. Calling an adult <span class='gd-jp'>子供っぽい</span> isn't a compliment; on the other hand, calling a child or young person <span class='gd-jp'>大人っぽい</span> (mature) is usually praise.<br>・It's very casual, so avoid it in reports and formal writing.<br>・Young people also attach っぽい to a whole sentence to mean \"it seems like ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>あの店、もう閉まったっぽい。</span>(That shop seems to have closed already.) This is very casual. It's fine with friends, but don't use it with your seniors.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>らしい</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>子供らしい</span> is how a child <b>naturally should be</b>, usually praise for an actual child; <span class='gd-jp'>子供っぽい</span> means <b>not a child, yet acting like one</b>, usually said about adults.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>元気で子供らしい子だ。</span>(He's a lively, genuinely childlike kid.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は考え方が子供っぽい。</span>(His way of thinking is childish.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>がち</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れっぽい</span> describes <b>a person's character</b>; <span class='gd-jp'>忘れがち</span> describes <b>something that often happens</b>, and you can say clearly what gets forgotten.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は忘れっぽい。</span>(He's forgetful.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>私はメールの返事を忘れがちだ。</span>(I often forget to reply to emails.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>気味</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>風邪っぽい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味</span> both mean \"a bit of a cold.\" っぽい is more casual and fine with friends; when asking your company for time off or explaining to a doctor, <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味</span> is safer.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use らしい for \"truly is that way, as it should be\"; use っぽい for \"isn't, but resembles\" or \"tends to ~ by personality\"; use がち for \"something often happens.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Two other patterns also describe tendencies: <span class='gd-jp'>〜がち</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜気味</span>. Studying all three together makes them easier to tell apart.</div>","n3-34":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜がち」means <b>\"tends to ~\", \"is prone to ~\", \"often ends up ~ing\"</b>. It describes a tendency that <b>happens often and is usually undesirable</b>, such as catching colds easily, running late, or forgetting things.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冬は風邪を引きがちだ。</span>(In winter, people tend to catch colds.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs</span>Change to the ます form, drop ます, then add がち.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みがち</span></span> (often absent)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅れます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅れがち</span></span> (often late, often behind)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しがち</span></span> (tends to do)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Nouns</span>Attach directly, but only a fixed few common ones work. Not every noun can take it.<br><span class='gd-jp'>病気がち</span> (often sick), <span class='gd-jp'>留守がち</span> (often not at home), <span class='gd-jp'>曇りがち</span> (often cloudy)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜がちだ</span>At the end of a sentence, saying a person or thing often does this.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいと、食事を抜きがちだ。</span>(When I'm busy, I tend to skip meals.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は最近、学校を休みがちだ。</span>(He's been missing school a lot lately.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜がちな + noun</span>Before a noun, it works like 「静かな部屋」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは初心者にありがちなミスです。</span>(This is a mistake beginners often make.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It can't attach to the dictionary form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>休みがち</span>, not 「休むがち」.<br>・It carries an undesirable nuance, so it's generally not used for good things. To say \"I study often,\" just say <span class='gd-jp'>よく勉強する</span>. Saying 「勉強しがち」 would sound odd, as if studying were a bad habit.<br>・You can't use it for something that happened only once. がち means \"often, prone to,\" not \"this time.\"<br>・Don't attach it to nouns freely. For now, just remember common ones like <span class='gd-jp'>病気がち</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>留守がち</span>.<br>・The <span class='gd-jp'>ガチ</span> that young people say (\"serious, for real, super\") is slang and has nothing to do with 「〜がち」here.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>がち</b> = a tendency that <b>happens often</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>子どものころは病気がちだった。</span>(I was often sick as a child.)<br><b>気味</b> = you <b>feel a bit</b> that way <b>right now</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>今日はちょっと風邪気味だ。</span>(I feel a bit like I have a cold today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>がち</b> = something that <b>easily happens</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>人の名前は忘れがちだ。</span>(I often forget people's names.)<br><b>っぽい</b> = the person <b>has</b> that quality overall: <span class='gd-jp'>祖父は最近忘れっぽい。</span>(My grandfather has become forgetful lately.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use がち for \"often tends to ~,\" 気味 for \"a bit ~ right now,\" and っぽい for \"the person is generally ~-ish.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜気味</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜っぽい</span>. Remembering these three together is the best way to avoid mixing them up.</div>","n3-30":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にしては」 means <b>“for a ~…” or “considering that it's ~, it's surprisingly…”</b>. You have an expectation about someone's status or about a fact, and reality <b>turns out different from that expectation</b>. That's when you use it.<br>For example, you see a child's very neat handwriting and say “Nice handwriting for a kid.” That's <span class='gd-jp'>子どもにしては、字がきれいだ。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Better than expected</span>You didn't expect much, but the result is good, and there's a hint of praise.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>初めて作ったにしては、おいしいね。</span>(Pretty tasty for a first try!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新人にしては、よくやっている。</span>(He's doing well for a newcomer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Worse than expected</span>You expected a lot, but the result is a letdown, and there's a hint of complaint.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>有名な店にしては、あまりおいしくなかった。</span>(For a famous restaurant, it wasn't that good.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Weather, time</span>It doesn't match what you'd expect for the season or the time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>4月にしては寒いですね。</span>(It's cold for April, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly, with <b>no の and no だ</b> in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>プロ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>プロにしては</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>4月</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>4月にしては</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: use the plain form (dictionary form, た-form, and other forms without ます).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>初めて作った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>初めて作ったにしては</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>3年も住んでいる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>3年も住んでいるにしては</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a noun, attach にしては directly, <b>without の</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>子どもにしては</span>, not 子どものにしては. (The one with の is <span class='gd-jp'>子どものわりに</span>, so don't mix them up.)<br>・What comes before it should be a <b>concrete status, number, or fact</b>, like 「新人」「60歳」「千円」「初めて」. Words that just name a range or category, like “age,” “price,” or “height,” can't attach directly: <span class='gd-jp'>値段にしては</span> sounds unnatural, so switch to <span class='gd-jp'>値段のわりに</span>.<br>・い-adjectives generally don't attach directly to にしては. 「安いにしては」 sounds odd, so use <span class='gd-jp'>安いわりに</span> instead.<br>・The second half is your <b>evaluation or impression</b>. It doesn't take commands or decisions like “please ~” or “I'll ~.”<br>・You can use it about yourself too: <span class='gd-jp'>私にしては、よく頑張ったと思う。</span>(For me, I think I worked pretty hard.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>わりに</b> (n3-29) also means “different from what you'd expect,” and English often translates both as “for a ~.” The difference is <b>what comes before it</b>.<br>Same situation, an older man who looks very young:<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの人は60歳にしては若く見える。</span>(He looks young for 60.) → a concrete 「60歳」 comes before it<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの人は年のわりに若く見える。</span>(He looks young for his age.) → a range word like “age” comes before it<br>Same cheap, tasty restaurant:<br><span class='gd-jp'>千円にしてはおいしい。</span>(Tasty for a thousand yen.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>値段のわりにおいしい。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>安いわりにおいしい。</span>(Tasty for the price / Cheap, yet tasty.)<br>Also, にしては carries more of an in-the-moment “wait, this is supposed to be ~!” surprise, and the evaluation it carries is stronger.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>として</b> (n3-5) is also often translated as “as a ~,” but it means <b>speaking from the standpoint of that role</b>. It has no “different from expectation” meaning.<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生として、生徒を守りたい。</span>(As a teacher, I want to protect my students.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生にしては、字が汚いね。</span>(Pretty messy handwriting for a teacher, huh.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if a concrete status, number, or fact comes first and the result surprises you, use にしては. If a range word like “age” or “price,” or an adjective, comes first, use わりに. If you're just saying “in the role of ~” with no surprise, use として.</div>","n3-5":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜として」 means <b>“as a ~,” “in the capacity of ~,” or “as (a) ~”</b>. Put a noun before it to say <b>what role, status, or purpose</b> something is done in.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Status・position</span>Doing something in a certain capacity. This is the most common use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学生として日本に来ました。</span>(I came to Japan as an international student.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は医者として、また父として尊敬されている。</span>(He is respected both as a doctor and as a father.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>As・for the purpose of</span>Treating something as having a certain use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これはお土産として買いました。</span>(I bought this as a souvenir.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>As a category</span>Saying that something is known or regarded “as a ~.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>京都は観光地として有名です。</span>(Kyoto is famous as a tourist destination.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's simple: <b>noun + として</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>先生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>先生として</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>趣味</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>趣味として</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>としての＋noun</span>When a noun follows, add 「の」 in between.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親としての責任がある。</span>(I have a responsibility as a parent.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>私としては</span>“From my standpoint.” A polite way to give your own opinion, very common in meetings and at work.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私としては、この案に賛成です。</span>(Personally, I'm in favor of this proposal.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When a noun follows, use <b>としての</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>医者としての仕事</span>, not 「医者として仕事」 to mean “a doctor's job.”<br>・The “for” in “For students, studying is important” is not として. Say <span class='gd-jp'>学生にとって、勉強は大切だ。</span> Writing 「学生として、勉強は大切だ」 sounds very odd.<br>・As a rule, put a noun before として. Don't attach a verb or adjective directly.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>として</b> = the noun before it is <b>the subject's own status or role</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>父として、子どもを守りたい。</span>(As a father, I want to protect my child. The speaker <i>is</i> the father.)<br><b>にとって</b> = the noun before it is <b>whose point of view</b> the feeling or judgment comes from:<br><span class='gd-jp'>父にとって、子どもは一番大切だ。</span>(To a father, his child is the most important thing. It's seen from the father's perspective.)<br>One more pair: <span class='gd-jp'>京都は観光地として有名だ。</span>(Kyoto <i>is</i> a tourist destination, and is famous in that capacity.) / <span class='gd-jp'>私にとって、京都は特別な場所だ。</span>(From my point of view, Kyoto is a special place.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if the noun before it is the subject's own status, role, or category (he <i>is</i> a doctor, Kyoto <i>is</i> a tourist destination), use として. If it's “whose point of view the feeling or judgment comes from” (to a parent, to me), use にとって.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Review <span class='gd-jp'>〜にとって</span> from the same lesson alongside this one. You can also see として in <span class='gd-jp'>〜をはじめ（として）</span> (“starting with ~”).</div>","n3-21":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ないことはない</span>」 is the Japanese version of <b>\"I guess I could\" or \"It's not that I can't\"</b>. Two negatives stack up, so the meaning is really \"possible\", but it's a <b>discounted yes</b>. You can barely manage it, or it's technically possible, but you don't really want to or aren't keen. Say a friend invites you on a 4 a.m. hike and you answer \"Well, I guess I could go…\". Everyone can tell you'd rather not.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its ない form, then add <span class='gd-jp'>ことはない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>こともない</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行けない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行けないことはない</span></span> (I could go, I suppose)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べられない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べられないこともない</span></span> (I could eat it, I suppose)<br>It's most often used with \"can / can't\" expressions like <span class='gd-jp'>行ける</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span>, because this pattern is mostly about whether you're able to do something.</div><div class='gd-sec'>If the ない form still feels shaky, here's a quick review:<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last kana to its あ-column counterpart, then add ない. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → く becomes か → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span>. Verbs ending in う change to わ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会わない</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add ない. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span>（こない）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Barely doable</span>It's a bit of a stretch, but you could do it if you had to.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この量なら、一人で食べられないことはない。</span>(If it's this much, I could finish it by myself, I suppose.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a \"but\"</span>The most common shape adds 「<span class='gd-jp'>けど</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>が</span>」 and then states the real concern.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅まで歩けないことはないけど、ちょっと遠いね。</span>(I could walk to the station, I guess, but it's a bit far.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行けないこともないが、あまり気が進まない。</span>(I could go, I suppose, but I'm not really into the idea.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noncommittal answer</span>Use it when someone asks you something and you don't want to say yes outright, but you don't want to flatly refuse either.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日までにできる？</span>(Can you get it done by tomorrow?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>できないことはないですけど…。</span>(Well, it's not impossible, but…)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>What's the difference between ことはない and こともない?</b> The meaning is almost the same and you can swap them. <span class='gd-jp'>こともない</span> has an extra 「も」, which makes it softer and vaguer, like saying \"well… I guess it's kind of okay.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・This sentence is <b>affirmative</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>行けないことはない</span> means \"I can go\" (just reluctantly). Don't see two negatives and assume it means \"I can't go.\"<br>・It is <b>not an enthusiastic \"yes\"</b>. If a friend invites you and you reply <span class='gd-jp'>行けないことはない</span>, they'll sense you don't really want to come. If you truly want to go, say <span class='gd-jp'>行けるよ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>いいね、行こう</span>. Answering your boss or a client this way can also come across as uncooperative.<br>・The verb before it must be in the <b>ない form</b>. Without the ない, <span class='gd-jp'>行くことはない</span> becomes \"there's no need to go\", which is a completely different meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take karaoke as the topic. The only thing that changes is what comes before, and the meaning changes completely:<br><b>ない form + ことはない</b> = \"it's not that I don't…\": <span class='gd-jp'>カラオケは行かないことはないけど、年に一回くらいかな。</span> (It's not that I never go to karaoke, but maybe once a year.)<br><b>Dictionary form + ことはない</b> = \"no need to…\": <span class='gd-jp'>嫌なら、無理して行くことはないよ。</span> (If you don't like it, there's no need to force yourself to go.)<br><b>た form + ことはない</b> = \"have never…\": <span class='gd-jp'>カラオケには一度も行ったことはない。</span> (I've never once been to karaoke.)<br><b>ないわけではない</b> = \"it's not that…\": <span class='gd-jp'>カラオケが好きじゃないわけではない。</span> (It's not that I don't like karaoke.)<br>The last one is close to ないことはない. The difference is that <span class='gd-jp'>わけではない</span> focuses on correcting the other person's misunderstanding: \"it's not what you think.\" <span class='gd-jp'>ないことはない</span> focuses on \"it's possible, or barely okay.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: ない + <span class='gd-jp'>ことはない</span> = \"it's not that I can't…\"; dictionary form + <span class='gd-jp'>ことはない</span> = \"no need to…\"; た form + <span class='gd-jp'>ことはない</span> = \"have never…\", yet another meaning. To correct someone's misunderstanding, use <span class='gd-jp'>わけではない</span>.</div>","n3-29":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜わりに」 is the Japanese version of <b>\"for a …, it's quite …\" or \"you'd expect X, but actually …\"</b>. From the first part you imagine how things \"should\" turn out, and when reality differs from that expectation, you use わりに. It's like telling a friend \"For the price, this place gives you a lot of food.\" That's the feel of わりに.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Pleasant surprise</span>The result is better than you'd expect.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は、値段のわりに量が多い。</span>(For the price, this place serves a lot of food.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はよく食べるわりに、太らない。</span>(He eats so much, yet he doesn't gain weight.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Unpleasant surprise</span>The result is worse than you'd expect, with a slightly disappointed tone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>よく勉強したわりに、点数が悪かった。</span>(Considering how much I studied, my score was bad.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は体が大きいわりには、気が小さい。</span>(For someone so big, he's pretty timid.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>For extra emphasis you can say <b>わりには</b>. The meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly; use the た form for past events.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>よく食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よく食べるわりに</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強したわりに</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>近い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>近いわりに</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: change だ to な.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>丈夫だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>丈夫なわりに</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add の.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>年齢</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>年齢のわりに</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は駅から近いわりに、家賃が安い。</span>(This room is close to the station, yet the rent is cheap.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このかばんは丈夫なわりに軽い。</span>(This bag is sturdy, yet light.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は年齢のわりに元気だ。</span>(My dad is full of energy for his age.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use 「な」 after な-adjectives and add 「の」 after nouns: it's <span class='gd-jp'>丈夫なわりに</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>年齢のわりに</span>, not 丈夫だわりに or 年齢わりに.<br>・It only works when the result is \"different from what you'd expect.\" It's natural that expensive things taste good, so <span class='gd-jp'>値段が高いわりにおいしい</span> sounds odd. The right version is <span class='gd-jp'>値段が高いわりにおいしくない</span> (so expensive, yet not tasty).<br>・What comes before it should ideally be something with a scale, like high/low or more/less: age, price, height, amount of study.<br>・What follows should describe or evaluate something. You can't follow it with requests or invitations like 「Please ～」 or 「Let's ～」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>わりに</span> placed alone before an adjective (often also <span class='gd-jp'>わりと</span>) is a different usage, meaning \"more … than expected\": <span class='gd-jp'>今日のテストはわりに簡単だった。</span> (Today's test was easier than I expected.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にしては</b> also means \"for a …, unexpectedly\", and both translate into English almost the same way. The difference is what goes before it.<br><b>わりに</b>: what comes before can be a general degree like \"age\" or \"price\", and it can also follow adjectives and verbs.<br><b>にしては</b>: what comes before is a specific number, status or fact, like 「60歳」「1000円」「初めて」. It mainly follows nouns and verbs, and rarely adjectives.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は年齢のわりに若く見える。</span>(My dad looks young for his age.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は60歳にしては若く見える。</span>(My dad looks young for a 60-year-old.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この料理は値段のわりにおいしい。</span>(This dish is tasty for the price.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この料理は1000円にしてはおいしい。</span>(This dish is tasty for 1000 yen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Conversely, 「年齢にしては」 and 「値段にしては」 sound unnatural, because \"age\" and \"price\" are not specific figures.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before is a general degree like \"age\" or \"price\", or an adjective, use わりに. If it's a specific number or status like \"60 years old\", \"1000 yen\" or \"the first time\", use にしては.</div>","n3-17":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜さえ〜ば」means <b>\"as long as ~, then ~\"</b> or <b>\"all you need is ~\"</b>. You don't need anything else: <b>this one condition is enough</b>. When you'd say \"As long as I have internet, I can work anywhere,\" Japanese uses this pattern.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>時間さえあれば、もっと旅行したい。</span>（As long as I have time, I want to travel more.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First, a quick review of how the ば-form (the \"if ~\" form) is made, word by word:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to け → <span class='gd-jp'>書けば</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あれば</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よければ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>名詞＋さえ＋〜ば</span>The most common pattern. Put さえ right after the thing you want to emphasize, then use the ば-form of a verb or adjective.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気さえよければ、明日は富士山が見える。</span>（As long as the weather is good, you'll be able to see Mt. Fuji tomorrow.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>動詞＋さえすれば</span>Emphasizes \"as long as you just do this action.\" Put the verb in ます-form, drop ます, then add さえすれば.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みさえすれば</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この薬を飲みさえすれば、すぐ治りますよ。</span>（As long as you take this medicine, you'll get better soon.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ボタンを押しさえすれば、あとは機械がやってくれる。</span>（All you have to do is press the button, and the machine does the rest.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>て形＋さえいれば</span>Emphasizes \"as long as you just stay in this state.\"<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待ってさえいれば</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここで待ってさえいれば、大丈夫です。</span>（As long as you wait here, you'll be fine.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>形容詞＋さえあれば</span>For い-adjectives, change い to く. For な-adjectives, add で. Then attach さえあれば.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安くさえあれば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かでさえあれば</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>静かでさえあれば、狭い部屋でもかまわない。</span>（As long as it's quiet, I don't mind even a small room.）</div><div class='gd-h'>In speech, it often becomes たら or なら</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you swap ば for 「たら」 or 「なら」, the meaning is still \"as long as ~\". You'll hear this a lot in conversation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>時間さえあったら、手伝うよ。</span>（As long as I have time, I'll help.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あなたさえいいなら、私はそれでいい。</span>（As long as you're happy with it, I'm fine with it too.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With verbs, use the <b>ます-form minus ます</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>飲みさえすれば</span>, not 「飲むさえすれば」.<br>・さえ replaces 「が」 and 「を」 after a noun: it's <span class='gd-jp'>お金さえあれば</span>, not 「お金がさえあれば」.<br>・It means \"as long as\", not \"only if\". <span class='gd-jp'>練習さえすれば</span> means \"just practice and that's enough.\" The point is that the condition is easy, not that \"nothing else will do.\"<br>・さえ must be followed by a conditional form (ば, たら, なら) to mean \"as long as\". Without one, さえ means \"even ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's the same さえ, but what follows decides the meaning:<br><b>さえ〜ば／たら／なら</b> = as long as ~: <span class='gd-jp'>ひらがなさえ読めれば、このゲームはできる。</span>（As long as you can read hiragana, you can play this game.）<br><b>さえ on its own</b> = even ~: <span class='gd-jp'>ひらがなさえ読めない。</span>（I can't even read hiragana.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: check whether さえ is followed by a conditional form (〜ば, 〜たら, 〜なら). If it is, it means \"as long as\". If not, it means \"even ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The stand-alone 〜さえ (\"even ~\") is worth studying alongside the similar <span class='gd-jp'>〜すら</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜まで</span>.</div>","n3-24":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜代わりに」means <b>\"B in place of A\"</b>. It can be swapping things, people or methods. It can also be a trade like \"you help me, I'll treat you,\" or a gain and a loss like \"the pay is high, but in return it's exhausting.\" The core is always the same: <b>one side for the other, evenly balanced</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Swapping things or methods</span>You would normally use or do A, but you use or do B instead.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>砂糖の代わりに、はちみつを使った。</span>（I used honey instead of sugar.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は外食する代わりに、家で作ろう。</span>（Instead of eating out today, let's cook at home.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Swapping people</span>A was supposed to do it, but B does it instead.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長の代わりに、部長が会議に出た。</span>（The department head attended the meeting in place of the president.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私の代わりに電話に出てくれる？</span>（Can you answer the phone for me?）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Trading favors</span>I do A, and you do B.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>英語を教える代わりに、日本語を教えてもらっている。</span>（I teach them English, and in exchange they teach me Japanese.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題を手伝ってもらった代わりに、昼ご飯をおごった。</span>（They helped me with my homework, so in return I treated them to lunch.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Good and bad</span>One good point comes with a matching bad point, or the other way around.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>給料が高い代わりに、仕事がきつい。</span>（The pay is high, but in return the work is tough.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は駅から遠い代わりに、家賃が安い。</span>（This room is far from the station, but in return the rent is cheap.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」 in between. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>砂糖</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>砂糖の代わりに</span></span><br><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form. Affirmative, negative and past all work.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行く代わりに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない代わりに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>手伝ってもらった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>手伝ってもらった代わりに</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高い代わりに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>効かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>効かない代わりに</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: keep the 「な」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利な代わりに</span></span><br>When you mean \"instead of doing A, do B,\" the verb is usually in dictionary form, e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>行く代わりに</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この薬はあまり効かない代わりに、副作用もない。</span>（This medicine isn't very effective, but in return it has no side effects either.）</div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>その代わり</span>Placed alone at the start of a sentence, it means \"but in return ~\". It's very common when you're setting terms with a friend.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>車、貸してあげる。その代わり、ガソリン入れてきてね。</span>（I'll lend you my car. In return, fill up the tank for me, okay?）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The word order is the reverse of English</b>: English says \"honey instead of sugar,\" with the new thing first. Japanese is <span class='gd-jp'>砂糖の代わりにはちみつ</span>, with the thing being replaced before 「代わりに」. If you follow the English order and write <span class='gd-jp'>はちみつの代わりに砂糖</span>, the meaning is completely flipped.<br>・Don't drop the 「の」 after a noun or the 「な」 after a な-adjective.<br>・For the \"good and bad\" use, the two sides must offset each other. For a plain \"but,\" use 「けど」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語は難しいけど、毎日勉強している。</span> You can't replace it with 代わりに here.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>代わりに</b> = one good, one bad, <b>evenly balanced</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>この店は高い代わりに、すごくおいしい。</span>（This place is expensive, but in return it's really delicious.）<br><b>わりに</b> = considering A, the result is <b>not what you'd expect</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>この店は高いわりに、あまりおいしくない。</span>（For how expensive this place is, it's not that good.）<br>Also, <b>反面</b> can express a good and a bad side too (<span class='gd-jp'>給料が高い反面、仕事がきつい。</span>). The meaning is very close to 代わりに, but it's more formal and leans toward describing two sides of the same thing.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if it feels like \"one for the other, it evens out,\" use 代わりに. If it feels like \"considering ~, how come it's like this?\", use わりに.</div>","n3-7":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜せいで」means <b>\"it's because of ~\" or \"~ is to blame\"</b>. When something turns out badly and you want to say what caused it, this is the phrase.<br>Switch to 「〜せいか」 and the tone steps back: it becomes <b>\"maybe because of ~\"</b>, meaning you're not quite sure of the cause yourself.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>せいで</span>The result is bad, and you're sure who or what caused it. There's a hint of blame or complaint.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨のせいで、試合が中止になった。</span>(Because of the rain, the match was cancelled.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私が寝坊したせいで、みんなを待たせてしまった。</span>(It's my fault for oversleeping; I made everyone wait.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>せいか</span>You're only guessing at the cause, and it may not really be so. In English it's often \"maybe it's because of ~\" or \"perhaps due to ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年のせいか、最近疲れやすい。</span>(Maybe it's my age, but I get tired easily these days.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝不足のせいか、頭がぼんやりする。</span>(Maybe it's from lack of sleep, but my head feels foggy.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the end of a sentence</span>Put the cause last, and it becomes \"~ is because of ~\" or \"~ is to blame\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>失敗したのは準備不足のせいだ。</span>(The failure was due to a lack of preparation.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私のせいです。すみません。</span>(It's my fault. I'm sorry.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>「せい」is itself a noun, so what comes before it works just like before a noun.<br><b>Noun</b>: add 「の」<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>風邪</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>風邪のせいで</span></span><br><b>な-adjective</b>: add 「な」<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>下手なせいで</span></span><br><b>い-adjective</b>: attach directly<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>狭い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>狭いせいで</span></span><br><b>Verb</b>: use the plain form (the form without ます); the past tense is fine too<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べすぎた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べすぎたせいで</span></span><br>せいか attaches in exactly the same way; just swap で for か.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns must take 「の」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>雪のせいで</span>, not 「雪せいで」.<br>・な-adjectives take 「な」, not 「だ」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>暇なせいか</span>, not 「暇だせいか」.<br>・<b>Don't use せいで for good results.</b> For \"Thanks to my teacher, I passed,\" use おかげで. With せいで it would sound like you're blaming the teacher for your passing, which is odd.<br>・Saying <span class='gd-jp'>あなたのせいで</span> to someone's face is a direct accusation. It's harsh, so be careful.<br>・The very common <span class='gd-jp'>気のせいだよ</span> is a set phrase meaning \"you're just imagining it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even with the same cause, \"because of the rain,\" a different result means a different word:<br><b>せいで</b> = bad result, you blame it: <span class='gd-jp'>雨のせいで、花火大会が中止になった。</span>(Because of the rain, the fireworks festival was cancelled.)<br><b>おかげで</b> = good result, you're thankful for it: <span class='gd-jp'>雨のおかげで、涼しくなった。</span>(Thanks to the rain, it got cooler.)<br>Conversely, using <span class='gd-jp'>おかげで</span> for a bad result is sarcasm: <span class='gd-jp'>君のおかげで、また怒られたよ。</span>(Thanks to you, I got scolded again.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"it's ~'s fault,\" use せいで; to say \"thanks to ~,\" use おかげで; if the cause is uncertain and you're just guessing, use せいか.</div>","n3-9":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「とともに」can be written with kanji as 「と共に」, and it's close to English <b>\"together with ~\" or \"along with ~ / as ~\"</b>. It's a fairly <b>formal, written</b> expression, common in news, speeches, essays and greetings. It feels like saying \"together with my family\" or \"as times change\"; people don't use it much in casual chat.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Together</span>Doing something with someone. It's the formal version of 「と一緒に」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族とともに新居で新年を迎えた。</span>(I welcomed the new year in our new home together with my family.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>チームの仲間とともに優勝を喜んだ。</span>(I celebrated the championship together with my teammates.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Along with / as</span>When the first thing changes, the second changes with it. This is the usage most tested at N3.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢とともに体力が落ちてきた。</span>(As I've gotten older, my stamina has declined.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人口が減るとともに、町の店も少なくなった。</span>(As the population shrank, the shops in town became fewer too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the same time</span>The moment A happens, B happens too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ベルが鳴るとともに、子どもたちは教室を飛び出した。</span>(The moment the bell rang, the children dashed out of the classroom.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Noun + とともに</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家族</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家族とともに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>技術の発展</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>技術の発展とともに</span></span><br><b>Verb dictionary form (the base form you'd look up in a dictionary) + とともに</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>増える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増えるとともに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>鳴る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>鳴るとともに</span></span><br>A step further: <b>Noun + であるとともに</b> = \"is ~, and at the same time is also ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は医者であるとともに、作家でもある。</span>(He is a doctor and also a writer.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's very formal. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>友達とともにラーメンを食べた</span> to a friend sounds like reading out an official document. In speech, use <span class='gd-jp'>友達と一緒に</span>.<br>・In the \"along with\" usage, the second half must describe <b>what changes</b>, not just a fixed habit: ×<span class='gd-jp'>年をとるとともに、毎日運動している</span>　○<span class='gd-jp'>年をとるとともに、運動する時間が増えた</span>.<br>・Verbs always take the dictionary form, even for past events; the past tense goes at the end of the sentence: ×<span class='gd-jp'>増えたとともに</span>　○<span class='gd-jp'>人口が増えるとともに、町がにぎやかになった</span>.<br>・The particle before it is 「と」, not 「に」: <span class='gd-jp'>家族とともに</span>, not 家族にともに.<br>・In books it's often written with kanji as <span class='gd-jp'>と共に</span>. Both spellings are correct.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>と一緒に</b> = everyday, spoken \"together\": <span class='gd-jp'>家族と一緒に旅行した。</span>(I traveled with my family.)<br><b>とともに</b> = formal \"together\": <span class='gd-jp'>家族とともに旅行した。</span>(Same meaning, but it reads like an essay or the news.)<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>と一緒に</span> can't mean \"along with a change,\" so you can't say ×<span class='gd-jp'>年齢と一緒に体力が落ちた</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>につれて</b> = only means \"as (something changes)\": <span class='gd-jp'>年をとるにつれて、体力が落ちてきた。</span>(As I've gotten older, my stamina has declined.)<br><b>とともに</b> = can also mean \"as,\" in a more formal tone: <span class='gd-jp'>年をとるとともに、体力が落ちてきた。</span><br>The difference: <span class='gd-jp'>につれて</span> can't be used for \"together\" or \"at the same time,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>とともに</span> covers all three.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for everyday \"together,\" use と一緒に. For \"as (something changes),\" both につれて and とともに work, and the more formal it is, the more it leans toward とともに. For \"together\" or \"at the same time\" in a formal register, choose とともに.</div>","n3-47":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ものだ」is like saying <b>\"that's just how it is\" or \"that's what you're supposed to do\"</b>. It states a truth or common sense that everyone accepts, not your personal opinion. Its opposite, 「〜ものではない」, is the voice of an elder saying <b>\"you shouldn't ~\" or \"that's no way to behave\"</b>, with a bit of a lecture in it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>That's just how it is</span>Describes the nature of people or things, or how the world works. It often carries a sigh of \"well, that's life.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人は誰でも間違えるものだ。</span>（Everyone makes mistakes. That's just human.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>時間が経つのは早いものだ。</span>（Time sure flies.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>That's what you should do</span>Says what one \"should do\" according to social common sense.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お年寄りには親切にするものだ。</span>（You should be kind to the elderly.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人の話は最後まで聞くものだ。</span>（You should listen to people until they finish.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>You shouldn't</span>Use 「〜ものではない」 to advise or scold someone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べ物を粗末にするものではない。</span>（You shouldn't waste food.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Nostalgia for the past</span>With 「た form + ものだ」 you talk about things you <b>used to do often</b>, with a feeling of \"ah, those days...\". It often appears with 「よく」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供の頃は、よくこの川で遊んだものだ。</span>（As a kid, I used to play by this river a lot.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>間違える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>間違えるものだ</span></span><br>Verb ない form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>変わらない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>変わらないものだ</span></span><br>い-adjectives attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>早い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>早いものだ</span></span><br>な-adjectives need な: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気なものだ</span></span><br>Nostalgia uses the た form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んだものだ</span></span><br>\"Shouldn't\" uses the dictionary form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言うものではない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>In casual speech it is often shortened to <b>もんだ</b> and <b>もんじゃない</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>そんなこと言うもんじゃないよ。</span>（You shouldn't say things like that, you know.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・ものだ states a <b>widely accepted truth</b>. You can't use it for your own plan on one particular occasion. For \"I'm going to school tomorrow,\" just say <span class='gd-jp'>明日学校へ行きます</span>, with no ものだ.<br>・「ないものだ」 and 「ものではない」 mean different things: <span class='gd-jp'>人は簡単には変わらないものだ</span> means \"people just don't change easily\" (human nature), while <span class='gd-jp'>人の悪口を言うものではない</span> means \"you shouldn't speak ill of others\" (advice).<br>・The nostalgic use is for things you <b>did often</b> in the past. It doesn't work for a one-time event: if you had ramen yesterday, say <span class='gd-jp'>昨日ラーメンを食べた</span>, with no ものだ.<br>・ものではない is a scolding tone, so it sounds rude toward a boss or teacher. It's normally used by elders to younger people, or parents to children.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these mean \"should,\" so look at whose viewpoint it is:<br><b>ものだ</b> = <b>social common sense</b> says it should be this way, a general principle: <span class='gd-jp'>約束は守るものだ。</span>（You're supposed to keep promises.）<br><b>べきだ</b> = <b>the speaker's own judgment</b> that it should be done. It's more direct and stronger: <span class='gd-jp'>あなたはこの約束を守るべきだ。</span>（You should keep this promise.）<br><b>ことだ</b> = <b>giving advice</b> to the person in front of you, \"you'd better ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>約束を守りたいなら、メモしておくことだ。</span>（If you want to keep your promise, you'd better write it down.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for \"people are supposed to...\" use ものだ; for \"I think you should...\" use べきだ; for advice like \"you'd better...\" use ことだ. Also, <span class='gd-jp'>ものだから</span> gives a reason (\"because...\"), and it is a different thing from the ものだ here.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Two more patterns also state principles and advise people: 「〜べきだ・〜べきではない」 and 「〜ことだ」. Look at them next. Comparing all three side by side will make it clearer which one to use when.</div>","n3-1":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜において」is the <b>formal \"at / in ~\"</b>. English does the same thing: in everyday speech you say \"we had a meeting in Taipei,\" but in an official document it becomes \"the meeting was <b>held</b> in Taipei.\" Japanese works alike: in daily speech you say 「<span class='gd-jp'>台北で</span>」, but in formal writing, news, or speeches it becomes 「<span class='gd-jp'>台北において</span>」. The meaning is still \"at/in ~\", just <b>much more formal</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Where</span>Where an event or ceremony takes place. This is most common in news.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>開会式は台北において行われた。</span>（The opening ceremony was held in Taipei.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>On what occasion</span>A \"setting\" such as a meeting, interview, or match.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>面接において、一番大切なのは第一印象だ。</span>（In an interview, the most important thing is the first impression.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In which field</span>A particular field or area.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医療の分野において、AIの活用が進んでいる。</span>（In the medical field, the use of AI is advancing.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It always follows a <b>noun</b>. What matters is <b>what comes after</b>:<br><b>An action or a full sentence follows</b> → use <b>において</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>京都で開催された</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>京都において開催された</span></span>（held in Kyoto）<br><b>A noun follows directly</b> → use <b>における</b>. It works like \"in\" or \"of\" before a noun, the role 「<span class='gd-jp'>での</span>」 plays in casual speech.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本での問題</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本における問題</span></span>（problems in Japan）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本における外国人労働者の数は年々増えている。</span>（The number of foreign workers in Japan is increasing every year.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>You can also add 「は」 or 「も」 after it for emphasis:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この点においては、私も同じ意見です。</span>（On this point, I share the same opinion.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't use it for small everyday things</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>コンビニにおいてパンを買った</span> sounds like an official document and feels odd. For your own daily life, just say <span class='gd-jp'>コンビニでパンを買った</span>.<br>・<b>Switch to における when a noun follows</b>: you can't say 「日本において経済」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>日本における経済</span>.<br>・It only replaces the で meaning <b>\"where / on what occasion\"</b>. The で of means or tools can't be swapped: <span class='gd-jp'>はしで食べる</span> (eat with chopsticks) can't become 「はしにおいて」.<br>・For \"something <b>exists</b> somewhere\" you use に, not で, so it can't become において either. Say <span class='gd-jp'>公園に子どもがいる</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>で</b> = works in speech and everyday situations: <span class='gd-jp'>会議で決まりました。</span>（It was decided at the meeting.）<br><b>において</b> = the formal way to say the same thing: <span class='gd-jp'>会議において決定されました。</span>（It was decided at the meeting.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>について</b> looks similar but means something completely different: <b>\"about ~\"</b>, giving the content or topic. Look at this sentence, where both appear together:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議において、環境問題について話し合った。</span>（At the meeting, we discussed environmental issues.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>「会議」 is <b>where</b> the discussion happened, and 「環境問題」 is <b>what</b> was discussed.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you'd say \"at / in ~\" in English, it's において. If you'd say \"about / regarding ~\", it's について.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The pattern of 「〜て」 before an action and 「〜る」 before a noun shows up in many grammar points, such as <span class='gd-jp'>〜に関して／〜に関する</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜によって／〜による</span>. Once you've got this one, those will be easy.</div>","n3-12":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜最中に」means <b>\"right in the middle of ~, when suddenly …\"</b>. You're partway through something, busy with it, and then something else interrupts you or an accident happens. Think \"in the middle of a presentation, the power went out\" or \"in the middle of an interview, my phone rang.\" What follows is usually <b>something sudden that catches you off guard</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Interrupted midway</span>This is the most typical use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議の最中に、電話が鳴った。</span>(In the middle of the meeting, a phone rang.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>シャワーを浴びている最中に、お湯が出なくなった。</span>(Right in the middle of my shower, the hot water suddenly stopped.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>最中だ</span>At the end of a sentence, it means \"I'm in the middle of ~ right now.\" It's very common for answering the phone or politely turning someone down.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、食事の最中なので、あとでかけ直します。</span>(I'm in the middle of a meal right now, so I'll call you back later.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: first change to the 「〜ている」 form, then add 最中に.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べている最中に</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話している最中に</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: noun + の + 最中に. The noun has to be <b>an activity that is in progress</b>, like <span class='gd-jp'>会議</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>試合</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>授業</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>面接</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>試合</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>試合の最中に</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With verbs you must use the 「ている」 form: say <span class='gd-jp'>食べている最中に</span>, not 食べる最中に.<br>・Don't drop the 「の」 after a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>授業の最中に</span>, not 授業最中に.<br>・Here 「最中」 is read <b>さいちゅう</b>.<br>・It needs an action that is <i>ongoing over a stretch of time</i>. Actions that finish in an instant, like <span class='gd-jp'>着く</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>始まる</span>, can't take 最中に. Verbs that just describe a state, like <span class='gd-jp'>いる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span>, don't work either.<br>・What follows is usually <b>something sudden that interrupts</b>. If you just want to say \"during that time I did something,\" 最中に sounds odd.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>最中に</b> = while you're in the middle of doing something, <b>you're suddenly interrupted or something unexpected happens</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>寝ている最中に、地震が起きた。</span> (While I was sleeping, an earthquake hit.)<br><b>間に</b> = taking advantage of that period to <b>get something done</b> (it can be deliberate): <span class='gd-jp'>子どもが寝ている間に、部屋を片付けた。</span> (While the kids were asleep, I tidied up the room.)<br>If you changed the second sentence to 「寝ている最中に、部屋を片付けた」, it would sound off, because tidying a room isn't something that \"suddenly pops up.\"</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜中</b> (for example <span class='gd-jp'>会議中</span>) simply says \"in progress\" in a neutral way, and you often see it on door signs and notices. <b>〜の最中</b> is stronger and stresses \"right in the thick of it, at the busiest point.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for \"in the middle of ~, suddenly …\" use 最中に. For \"taking advantage of the time while ~, I did …\" use 間に.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To make it even stronger, you can say 「真っ最中」: <span class='gd-jp'>今、テストの真っ最中だ。</span> (I'm right in the middle of a test at this very moment.)</div>","n3-14":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「～間に」means <b>\"while ~ (something happened)\"</b> or <b>\"while ~ (I got something done)\"</b>. Picture a period of time as a line. 「間に」 <b>plants a flag</b> on that line: at some moment within that period, something happened, or you finished something.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Happened while you weren't looking</span>Something happened while you were away or not paying attention.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留守の間に宅配便が届いた。</span>(A package was delivered while I was out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>知らない間に、もう夜の12時になっていた。</span>(Before I knew it, it was already midnight.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Getting something done in that time</span>You use a stretch of time to finish something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもが寝ている間に、部屋を掃除した。</span>(I cleaned the room while the kids were asleep.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本にいる間に、富士山に登りたい。</span>(I want to climb Mt. Fuji while I'm in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>What comes before it must be <b>a period of time</b> or <b>a continuing state</b>.<br><b>Verbs</b>: for actions like \"sleep, eat, watch,\" you usually use the ている form (～ている), which turns them into a \"be in the middle of ~\" stretch of time. <span class='gd-jp'>いる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> are already states, so you can use them as they are. The ない form (～ない) also works.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝ている間に</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本にいる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本にいる間に</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知らない間に</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: put 「の」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留守</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留守の間に</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>夏休み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>夏休みの間に</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You must add 「の」 after a noun: say <span class='gd-jp'>留守の間に</span>, not 「留守間に」.<br>・Here 「間」 is read <b>あいだ</b>. The 「かん」 in <span class='gd-jp'>一週間</span> is a different reading, so don't mix them up.<br>・English just says \"while ~\" for both, but in Japanese it depends on what comes next. If the second part <b>keeps going the whole time</b>, you don't add に. So don't follow 「間に」 with 「ずっと～」: <span class='gd-jp'>寝ている間に、ずっと雨が降っていた</span> is wrong, and you need to drop the に.<br>・Even when the whole sentence is about the past, the first part is usually still 「ている間に」. Put the past 「た」 only at the end: <span class='gd-jp'>寝ている間に地震があった。</span> (There was an earthquake while I was sleeping.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these are about summer vacation:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏休みの間、ずっとアルバイトをしていた。</span>(I worked part-time the whole summer vacation.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏休みの間に、運転免許を取った。</span>(Sometime during summer vacation, I got my driver's license.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏休みのうちに、宿題を終わらせたい。</span>(I want to finish my homework before summer vacation ends.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>間</b> (without に) = you do something <b>continuously</b> through that whole period, often with 「ずっと」 after it.<br><b>間に</b> = at <b>some moment</b> within that period, something happened or got done.<br><b>うちに</b> = adds a feeling of <b>\"while there's still time\"</b>, implying that once the period is over, it won't be so easy.<br>There's also <b>最中に</b> = you're right in the middle of something and <b>of all times</b> you get interrupted: <span class='gd-jp'>シャワーを浴びている最中に、電話が鳴った。</span> (I was in the middle of my shower when, of all times, the phone rang.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what follows is \"keeps doing ~ the whole time,\" use <b>間</b>. If it's \"something happened / got done,\" use <b>間に</b>. If you mean \"hurry up while you still can,\" use <b>うちに</b>.</div>","n3-4":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「によって」 works a lot like English <b>“by”, “through” or “depending on”</b>. Put a noun in front of it to say that something was done by it, caused by it, or depends on it. In English you'll translate it as “through”, “because of”, “depending on” or “by”, depending on context. It's especially common in news, explanatory writing and formal situations.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It always attaches directly to a <b>noun</b>, with no 「の」. What comes after decides which form you use:<br>A verb or a full clause follows → <b>によって</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>台風によって電車が止まった。</span> (The trains stopped because of the typhoon.)<br>A noun follows directly → <b>による</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>台風による停電</span> (a power outage caused by the typhoon)<br>In writing you'll also see 「<span class='gd-jp'>により</span>」. It means the same as によって, just more formal.</div><div class='gd-h'>The patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Varies by ～</span>“It depends on ～; it's different for each ～.” It's almost always followed by words meaning “different”, such as <span class='gd-jp'>違う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>異なる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>変わる</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>地域によって食文化が異なる。</span> (Food culture differs by region.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人によって考え方が違う。</span> (Everyone thinks differently.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Cause</span>“Because of ～; caused by ～.” The tone is objective, like a news report.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台風による停電が続いている。</span> (The power outage caused by the typhoon is still continuing.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>事故によって電車が遅れています。</span> (The trains are delayed because of an accident.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Means / method</span>“Through ～.” This is the more formal way to say it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この問題は話し合いによって解決した。</span> (This problem was resolved through discussion.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The “by whom” in passive sentences</span>Used to say who made a work, an invention or a building.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この小説は有名な作家によって書かれた。</span> (This novel was written by a famous author.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電話はベルによって発明された。</span> (The telephone was invented by Bell.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>によっては</span>Add 「は」 and the meaning becomes <b>“some ～ will…”</b>. You're picking out just some of them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>店によって値段が違う。</span> (Prices differ from shop to shop.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>店によっては、カードが使えない。</span> (Some shops don't accept cards.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Passives don't always use によって</b>. In everyday “scolded by someone” or “told by someone”, use 「に」: <span class='gd-jp'>母に叱られた。</span> (I was scolded by my mom.) Saying 「母によって叱られた」 sounds odd. によって is reserved for “who made something”.<br>・<b>For everyday means, just use 「で」</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>バスで行く</span>. You don't say 「バスによって行く」. The “through” of によって is formal and fits bigger things like methods and systems.<br>・<b>による must be followed by a noun</b>. If a clause follows, go back to によって. Don't write 「台風による電車が止まった」.<br>・“Varies by ～” and “some ～ will…” differ only by 「は」, but the meaning changes. Check whether it's there.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these can mean “because of the typhoon”, but the tone is very different:<br><b>によって</b> = states the cause objectively, with no emotion: <span class='gd-jp'>台風によって電車が止まった。</span> (The trains stopped because of the typhoon.)<br><b>せいで</b> = blames it, with a complaining tone: <span class='gd-jp'>台風のせいで旅行に行けなかった。</span> (It's the typhoon's fault that I couldn't go on the trip.)<br><b>おかげで</b> = thanks to it, with a tone of relief or gratitude: <span class='gd-jp'>台風のおかげで学校が休みになった。</span> (Thanks to the typhoon, school was cancelled.)<br>With a noun, せいで and おかげで need 「の」 before them (<span class='gd-jp'>雨のせいで</span>), while によって attaches directly with no 「の」 (<span class='gd-jp'>雨によって</span>).</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use によって to state a cause flatly, like a news report; use せいで to complain; use おかげで when you feel grateful or relieved.</div>","n3-6":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜おかげで」 means <b>“thanks to ～” or “luckily there was ～”</b>. Put the cause that brought a good result in front, then state that good result. There's a touch of gratitude in the tone.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Thanking someone</span>Things went well because someone helped.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生のおかげで、合格できました。</span> (Thanks to my teacher, I passed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達が手伝ってくれたおかげで、早く終わった。</span> (Thanks to my friend's help, it was finished quickly.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A good cause</span>It doesn't have to be a person. Weather, surroundings or things all work, as long as they brought a good result.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この薬を飲んだおかげで、熱が下がった。</span> (Thanks to taking this medicine, my fever went down.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅が近いおかげで、通勤が楽です。</span> (Luckily the station is close, so commuting is easy.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>先輩</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>先輩のおかげで</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the dictionary form, the た-form and so on, without ます). The good result has usually already happened, so the た-form is the most common.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>頑張った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>頑張ったおかげで</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>近い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>近いおかげで</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>よかった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よかったおかげで</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: use 「な」 in the present and 「だった」 in the past.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなおかげで</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気だったおかげで</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>The patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>おかげさまで</span>When someone asks how you are, you politely answer “thanks to you”. You can say it even if the other person didn't actually do anything for you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お元気ですか。</span>——<span class='gd-jp'>おかげさまで、元気です。</span> (How are you? — Thanks to you, I'm well.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜のおかげです</span>Placed at the end of a sentence to give someone the credit.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合格できたのは、先生のおかげです。</span> (I passed all thanks to my teacher.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Sarcasm</span>Just like English “thanks a lot”, you can use it sarcastically to needle someone when the result was actually bad.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>君のおかげで、全部やり直しだよ。</span> (Thanks a lot to you, we have to redo everything.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A noun must be followed by 「の」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>先生のおかげで</span>, not 先生おかげで.<br>・A な-adjective in the present can't take 「だ」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>静かなおかげで</span>, not 静かだおかげで.<br>・What follows must be a <b>result that has already happened or is certain to happen</b>. It can't be an invitation or a request, like “let's ～” or “please ～”.<br>・English “because” is very broad, but not every “because” can become おかげで. To simply explain a cause (for example, the match was cancelled because it rained), use <span class='gd-jp'>から</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ので</span>. Using おかげで would sound as if you were happy the match was cancelled.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>おかげで</b> = thanks to ～, and the result is <b>good</b>, with gratitude:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったおかげで、涼しくなった。</span> (Thanks to the rain, it got cooler.)<br><b>せいで</b> = it's ～'s fault, and the result is <b>bad</b>, with complaint or blame:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったせいで、試合が中止になった。</span> (Because of the rain, the match was cancelled.)<br>The two connect in exactly the same way. The only difference is whether you feel grateful or want to complain about the result.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use おかげで for “thanks to / luckily there was”; use せいで for “it's ～'s fault”; use から or ので when you're just stating a cause with no good or bad feeling.</div>","n3-10":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>ところ</span> originally means \"place\", but here it means <b>\"a point in time\"</b>. Think of an action as having three stages: <b>not started yet, halfway through, just finished</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>ところだ</span> tells the listener which stage you're in right now, much like English \"about to…\", \"in the middle of…\" and \"just finished…\".</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it: the form before it picks the stage</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>About to do</span>Dictionary form (the base form you find in a dictionary) + ところだ. The action has <b>not started yet</b>, but it's about to.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今から出かけるところです。</span>(I'm just about to head out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：もうご飯食べた？　B：ううん、これから食べるところ。</span>(A: Have you eaten yet? B: Not yet, I'm just about to.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In the middle of</span>て form + いる + ところだ. The action is <b>halfway through</b> and not finished yet.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今シャワーを浴びているところだから、あとでかけ直すね。</span>(I'm in the shower right now, so I'll call you back later.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今調べているところなので、少々お待ちください。</span>(I'm looking into it right now, so please wait a moment.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just finished</span>た form (past tense) + ところだ. The action ended <b>just a moment ago</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>たった今、家に帰ってきたところです。</span>(I just got home this very second.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議がちょうど終わったところです。</span>(The meeting has just ended.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's clearest to see all three with the same verb:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べるところ</span></span> (about to eat)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べているところ</span></span> (in the middle of eating)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたところ</span></span> (just finished eating)<br>With seniors and customers, say <span class='gd-jp'>ところです</span>; with friends you can just say <span class='gd-jp'>ところ</span>. In casual speech, <span class='gd-jp'>ているところ</span> often shrinks to <span class='gd-jp'>てるところ</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It shows up most when someone asks \"what are you doing right now?\", on phone calls, and when replying to messages, to explain <b>your situation at this moment</b>. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>これから</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>今から</span> (about to), <span class='gd-jp'>今</span> (in the middle of), and <span class='gd-jp'>たった今</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ちょうど</span> (just). When you see these words, you know which stage is meant.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Dictionary form + ところ means <b>not started yet</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>今食べるところ</span> means \"I'm about to eat\", not \"I'm eating\". For \"I'm eating\", say <span class='gd-jp'>食べているところ</span>.<br>・ているところ only works with <b>actions that are really in motion</b>. Verbs that describe a state, like <span class='gd-jp'>住んでいる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span>, can't be used this way: you can't say 「住んでいるところだ」.<br>・たところ means <b>really just a moment ago</b>. You can't say 「先月日本に来たところだ」, because last month doesn't count as \"just now\". Use たばかり instead (see below).<br>・Only <span class='gd-jp'>ところだ</span> at the end of a sentence has this meaning. 「〜たところ、…」 with a comma, continuing the sentence, is a different grammar point (\"I did ~, and then found that…\"). Don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たところだ</b> and <b>たばかりだ</b> both translate as \"just did ~\". The difference is how recent \"just\" really is.<br><b>たところだ</b>: the moment is right now, a few minutes ago at most.<br><span class='gd-jp'>今、駅に着いたところです。</span> (I just arrived at the station.)<br><b>たばかりだ</b>: the speaker <b>feels</b> it hasn't been long, which could be days or even months.<br><span class='gd-jp'>先月日本に来たばかりです。</span> (I only came to Japan last month.)<br>Also, たばかり often carries a feeling of \"I only just ~, so / and yet…\": <span class='gd-jp'>買ったばかりなのに、もう壊れた。</span> (I only just bought it and it's already broken.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can swap in \"right this very moment\", use たところ. If you mean \"not long ago, still feels fresh\", use たばかり. When unsure, if it was before today, use たばかり.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Dictionary form + <span class='gd-jp'>ところだった</span> = almost did ~ (luckily it didn't happen): <span class='gd-jp'>もう少しで電車に乗り遅れるところだった。</span> (I almost missed the train.)</div>","n3-11":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たばかり」 means <b>\"only just ~\" or \"it hasn't been long since ~\"</b>. The key is that you <b>feel</b> the time has been short, so it often carries a mood like \"I'm not used to it yet\" or \"again already?\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たばかりで、まだ慣れない。</span>(I've only just come to Japan, so I'm not used to it yet.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Always attach it to the verb's <b>た form</b> (past tense, like <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span>), then add ばかり.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたばかり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買ったばかり</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たばかり</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At sentence end</span>〜たばかりだ / 〜たばかりです</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先月結婚したばかりです。</span>(I only got married last month.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>More to say</span>〜たばかりで gives a reason: \"since I only just ~, …\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>起きたばかりで、頭がぼんやりしている。</span>(I only just woke up, so my head is still foggy.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Modifying a noun</span>〜たばかりの + noun</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは買ったばかりの靴です。</span>(These are shoes I just bought.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Complaint, surprise</span>〜たばかりなのに means \"I only just ~, and yet already…\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さっき食べたばかりなのに、もうお腹がすいた。</span>(I only just ate, and I'm already hungry again.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The form before it must be the た form. Writing <span class='gd-jp'>来るばかり</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>食べるばかり</span> gives a different meaning (explained below).<br>・To modify a noun, you need <b>の</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>買ったばかりの靴</span>, not 買ったばかり靴.<br>・How long \"just\" lasts depends on your feeling: <span class='gd-jp'>去年入社したばかりです</span> (I only joined the company last year) is perfectly fine, even though it was a year ago.<br>・Among friends it's often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>たばっか</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>今起きたばっか。</span> (I just woke up.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜たところだ</b> (n3-10 ところだ) also means \"just ~\", but it points to <b>the exact moment the action ended</b>, really seconds or minutes ago.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、駅に着いたところです。</span> (I've just arrived at the station.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、駅に着いたばかりです。</span> (I only just arrived at the station.)<br>Both work for this kind of \"just now\" situation. But once the time stretches out, only たばかり works:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>先月引っ越したばかりです。</span> (I only moved last month.) ○<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>先月引っ越したところです。</span> ×</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>If the form before ばかり changes, the meaning changes</b>:<br><b>Dictionary form + ばかり</b> mostly means \"keeps changing in one direction (more and more ~)\", for example <span class='gd-jp'>物価は上がるばかりだ。</span> (Prices just keep going up.) It can also mean \"does nothing but ~\", for example <span class='gd-jp'>彼女は泣くばかりで何も言わない。</span> (She just keeps crying and says nothing.)<br><b>て form + ばかりいる</b> = always doing only one thing: <span class='gd-jp'>弟はゲームをしてばかりいる。</span> (My little brother does nothing but play video games.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: look at what comes before ばかり. The た form means \"only just ~\", the dictionary form means \"keeps ~ (more and more ~ / nothing but ~)\", and the て form means \"always ~\". If the sentence mentions a longer time, like \"last month\" or \"last year\", use たばかり for \"just ~\", not たところ.</div>","n3-18":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜としたら」means <b>\"if it really were the case that…\"</b>. You take something uncertain, or even unlikely, and <b>suppose it's true</b>, then say \"what would happen\" or \"what I'd want to do.\" It's like a friend saying, \"Okay, hypothetically, say you won the lottery…\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Pure hypothesis</span>Use it for things that are unlikely or haven't happened. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>もし</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>仮に</span> (\"suppose\").</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もし宝くじが当たったとしたら、何に使う？</span>(If you won the lottery, what would you spend it on?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一週間休めるとしたら、どこに行きたい？</span>(If you could take a week off, where would you want to go?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reasoning from what you heard</span>You take what someone said, or news you heard, as true for now, and draw a conclusion from it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の話が本当だとすれば、大変なことだ。</span>(If what he says is true, this is serious.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the start of a sentence</span>Add <span class='gd-jp'>だ</span> in front to get <span class='gd-jp'>だとしたら</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>だとすると</span>. It means \"in that case,\" and you continue from what the other person just said.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：明日は台風が来るらしいよ。</span>(I hear a typhoon is coming tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>B：だとすると、旅行は無理だね。</span>(In that case, the trip is probably off.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs and い-adjectives attach directly:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くとしたら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>当たった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>当たったとしたら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いとしたら</span></span><br>Nouns and な-adjectives need <b>だ</b> first:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だとしたら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暇だとしたら</span></span><br>All three forms, <span class='gd-jp'>としたら</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>とすれば</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>とすると</span>, connect the same way.</div><div class='gd-h'>Choosing among the three</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>としたら</b>: the most conversational and the easiest to use. It's the most natural choice when what follows is your own wish or plan, like \"I want to…\" or \"I'll…\".<br><b>とすれば・とすると</b>: these have more of a \"reasoning\" feel, and are often followed by a judgment like <span class='gd-jp'>〜だろう</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜はずだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜必要がある</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日雨だとすれば、運動会は延期だろう。</span>(If it rains tomorrow, the sports day will probably be postponed.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't forget <b>だ</b> after nouns and な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>雨だとしたら</span>, not 「雨としたら」.<br>・If you attach <span class='gd-jp'>としたら</span> directly to a noun <b>without だ</b>, it takes a different meaning, \"from the standpoint of…\": <span class='gd-jp'>親としたら、心配だろう。</span> (From a parent's standpoint, they'd probably be worried.)<br>・<b>Don't use it for things that will definitely happen.</b> \"Call me when you get home\" is <span class='gd-jp'>家に着いたら電話して</span>. If you said 「着いたとしたら」, it would sound like you doubt the person will make it home.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>とすると</span> doesn't combine well with \"I want to…\" or \"please…\". In those cases, switch to <span class='gd-jp'>としたら</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たら</b> = the ordinary \"if / after…\", where the thing may really happen:<br><span class='gd-jp'>宝くじが当たったら、車を買う。</span> (If I win the lottery, I'll buy a car.)<br><b>としたら</b> = specifically stresses \"this is only a hypothesis\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>宝くじが当たったとしたら、車を買う。</span> (Suppose I really did win, I'd buy a car.)<br>For things that are sure to happen, only たら works: <span class='gd-jp'>家に着いたら連絡するね。</span> (I'll get in touch once I'm home.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you could say \"suppose that…\" or \"in the event that…\" in English, use としたら. If it's just \"once/after…, then…\", use たら.</div>","n3-19":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たところ」means <b>\"I did X, and as a result I found out…\"</b>. You first do something (call and ask, look something up, try something out), and only then learn a result. For example: \"I called the shop to ask, and it turned out they were sold out.\"<br>The <span class='gd-jp'>ところ</span> here has nothing to do with \"place.\" Just think of it as a switch that introduces the result.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb た-form + ところ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞いた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞いたところ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>調べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>調べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>調べたところ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>電話する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>電話した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>電話したところ</span></span><br>The sentence has two halves: the first is <b>what you did</b>, and the second is <b>what you found out or what happened after doing it</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Found out by asking</span>Information you get after asking someone or calling to check.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>店に電話したところ、もう売り切れだった。</span>(I called the shop, and it turned out they were already sold out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生に相談したところ、いいアドバイスをもらえた。</span>(I talked it over with my teacher and got some good advice.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Found out by trying</span>The result after looking into something or trying it out.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>詳しく調べたところ、原因がわかった。</span>(I looked into it carefully and found the cause.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この薬を飲んだところ、熱が下がった。</span>(I took this medicine, and my fever went down.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The tone is fairly formal. You'll see it a lot in reports, business emails and news. When chatting with friends, people mostly just use 「〜たら」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The second half is <b>something that has already happened or become known</b>, so it's mostly in the past tense. It can't be followed by what you're about to do, or by a request or command:<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>調べたところ、先生に聞きます。</span><br>○<span class='gd-jp'>調べてから、先生に聞きます。</span> (I'll look it up first, then ask my teacher.)<br>・The first half is usually <b>something you did on purpose</b>. Things that happen naturally, like rain, don't work well there:<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったところ、涼しくなった。</span><br>○<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったら、涼しくなった。</span> (After it rained, it got cooler.)<br>・The point is \"the result you discovered after doing something,\" so the pattern itself doesn't mean \"because X, Y.\" Sometimes the two halves happen to be cause and effect (medicine → fever down), and sometimes they aren't (calling the shop didn't cause the sell-out; it just let you find out). Both are fine.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たところ</b> = you did something, and <b>a result follows in the same sentence</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>駅員に聞いたところ、電車は遅れているそうだ。</span> (I asked a station attendant, and he said the train is delayed.)<br><b>たところだ</b> = you <b>just now</b> finished doing it, and the sentence ends there:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今、駅員に聞いたところだ。</span> (I just asked a station attendant.)<br><b>たら</b> = can also mean \"I did X and found that…\", but it's more casual and has a wider range of uses:<br><span class='gd-jp'>駅員に聞いたら、電車が遅れてるって。</span> (I asked a station attendant, and he said the train is delayed.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if 「ところ」 is followed by a comma and another clause giving a result, it's たところ. If the sentence stops at 「ところだ」, it means \"just finished.\" Use たところ for results in formal situations, and たら when chatting with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Go back and compare the three time points of <span class='gd-jp'>〜ところだ</span> (about to do, in the middle of doing, just did). That way you won't mix it up with たところ.</div>","n3-15":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ついでに」 means <b>\"while I'm at it\"</b> or <b>\"on the way\"</b>. A is something you were going to do anyway, and since you're already doing it, you take the chance to do B too. For example, \"I'm going downstairs to take out the trash, so I'll grab the mail while I'm at it\": taking out the trash is A, and getting the mail is B.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>There are three patterns. What comes before ついでに is always the thing you were going to do anyway:<br>Noun + の + ついでに　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買い物</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買い物のついでに</span></span><br>Verb dictionary form + ついでに　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くついでに</span></span><br>Verb た-form + ついでに　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たついでに</span></span><br>The noun has to stand for an activity, such as <span class='gd-jp'>買い物</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>散歩</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>出張</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>旅行</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Dictionary form or た-form?</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Dictionary form</span>A hasn't happened yet, and you're about to head out to do it. You do B on the side. It's often used to ask someone a favor.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コンビニに行くついでに、お茶を買ってきてくれる？</span>(When you go to the convenience store, could you pick up some tea for me too?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>出かけるついでに、ゴミを出してくるね。</span>(I'll take out the trash on my way out, okay?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>た-form</span>You're already there, or A has already happened, and you use the chance to do B.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>京都に来たついでに、奈良にも行ってみた。</span>(Since I was already in Kyoto, I went over to Nara too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本屋に寄ったついでに、ノートも買った。</span>(While I was at the bookstore, I bought a notebook too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun の</span>The simplest pattern, and very handy in daily life.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>買い物のついでに、銀行に寄った。</span>(I went shopping and stopped by the bank while I was out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>出張のついでに、大阪の友達に会った。</span>(While I was on a business trip, I met up with a friend in Osaka.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most often</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>ついでに</span> can also stand alone at the start of a sentence, simply meaning \"while you're at it.\" A is already clear from context, so you don't need to repeat it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、ついでに牛乳も買ってきて。</span>(Oh, and grab some milk while you're at it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>The main task goes first and the side task goes second</b>. If you swap them, the meaning changes: the other task becomes the real purpose.<br>・What comes before it must be an <b>action</b>, not a bare place. You can't say 「駅のついでに」; say <span class='gd-jp'>駅に行くついでに</span>.<br>・Both A and B must be things you <b>choose to do yourself</b>. Things that happen naturally, like rain, don't fit. You can't say 「雨が降ったついでに」.<br>・English \"by the way\" adds extra information. For that, Japanese usually uses <span class='gd-jp'>ちなみに</span>, not ついでに.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>\"While I'm at it\" and \"while doing…\" can blur together in English, but Japanese keeps them clearly apart. Take the same train station scene:<br><b>ついでに</b> = you use the chance of doing A to <b>do one more thing</b>. The two things don't have to happen at the same time: <span class='gd-jp'>駅に行くついでに、手紙を出した。</span>(I mailed a letter on my way to the station.)<br><b>ながら</b> = two things <b>happen at the same time</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>音楽を聞きながら、駅まで歩いた。</span>(I walked to the station while listening to music.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you're \"doing one more thing while you're at it,\" use ついでに. If you're doing two things at once, use ながら.</div>","n3-16":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てからでないと」 means <b>\"only after ~ can you ~\"</b> or <b>\"unless you ~ first, you can't ~\"</b>. Think \"you need a ticket before you can enter\" or \"wash your hands before you eat.\" The point is that <b>the order can't be reversed, and the first thing must be finished first</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Break it down and it makes sense</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's really three pieces put together:<br><span class='gd-jp'>て</span>-form (having done ~) + <span class='gd-jp'>から</span> (after) + <span class='gd-jp'>でないと</span> (if it's not the case)<br>Together it means \"if it's not after having done ~,\" in other words, \"without doing ~ first.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in the て-form, then add 「からでないと」 directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べてからでないと</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>してからでないと</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞いてからでないと</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>What comes after it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is almost always a <b>negative: \"not allowed,\" \"can't,\" \"no way\"</b>. Typical endings are <span class='gd-jp'>できない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>わからない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>だめだ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>無理だ</span>, and the negative potential form of a verb.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>手を洗ってからでないと、おやつはだめだよ。</span>(No snacks unless you wash your hands first.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>実際に見てからでないと、買うかどうか決められない。</span>(I can't decide whether to buy it until I've seen it in person.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お金を払ってからでないと、商品はお渡しできません。</span>(We can't hand over the product until payment has been made.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most often</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>からでないと</span>The standard form, fine in both speech and writing.<br><span class='gd-tag'>からでなければ</span>More formal and stiff. You often see it in notices and instructions.<br><span class='gd-tag'>からじゃないと</span>The casual spoken version between friends.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題やってからじゃないと、ゲームはできないよ。</span>(You can't play video games until you've done your homework.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The second half <b>can't be a positive \"you can\" sentence</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>予約してからでないと、入れます</span> is wrong. To say \"make a reservation, then go in,\" just use the plain <span class='gd-jp'>予約してから入ります</span>.<br>・Don't drop the 「<b>で</b>」 in the middle. 「てからないと」 is wrong.<br>・Get the て-form right: <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span>. One mistake here breaks the whole sentence.<br>・It carries a sense of \"rule\" or \"condition.\" When speaking to customers, use polite endings such as <span class='gd-jp'>できません</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>お渡しできません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>One situation: you need to read the contract before signing.<br><b>てから</b> = simply states the <b>order</b>, A then B: <span class='gd-jp'>内容を確認してから、サインします。</span>(I'll check the contents, then sign.)<br><b>てからでないと</b> = stresses that <b>you must do A first, or B is impossible</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>内容を確認してからでないと、サインできません。</span>(I can't sign until I've checked the contents.)<br><b>た上で</b> = you finish A and then do B <b>based on it</b>. More formal and careful: <span class='gd-jp'>内容を確認した上で、サインします。</span>(I'll sign after confirming the contents.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what follows is \"can do / will do,\" use <b>てから</b> or <b>た上で</b>. If what follows is \"not allowed / can't,\" and you want to stress \"you have to ~ first,\" use <b>てからでないと</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Move on to 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜上で</span>」 (n3-23). It's very common in formal settings for saying \"first ~, then ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>よく考えた上で、決めてください。</span>(Please decide after thinking it over carefully.)</div>","n3-22":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜以上（は）」means <b>\"since…, then…\"</b> or <b>\"now that…, then…\"</b>. You first state something that is already settled, then follow with \"so I definitely have to…\" or \"so I should…\". It's like saying, \"You already said yes, so you have to follow through.\" It carries a sense of <b>\"given this, I'm taking responsibility.\"</b></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>約束した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>約束した以上</span></span> (now that we've promised)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>やる以上</span></span> (since I'm going to do it)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ない以上</span></span> (since they're not coming)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: you need to add 「である」 in between. You can't attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生である以上</span></span> (since you're a student)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>プロ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>プロである以上</span></span> (since you're a professional)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The following 「は」 is optional. Adding it makes the tone a bit stronger, but the meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>What comes after it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The key is the second half: it must express the speaker's <b>resolve, obligation, or judgment</b>, not simply describe something that happened.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I will… (resolve)</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた以上は、最後までやります。</span> (Since I've taken this on, I'll see it through to the end.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やる以上は、一番を目指したい。</span> (If I'm going to do it, I want to aim for first place.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Should… / must… (obligation)</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に住んでいる以上、日本の法律を守らなければならない。</span> (As long as you live in Japan, you must obey Japanese law.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>プロである以上、言い訳はできない。</span> (Since you're a professional, you can't make excuses.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Then all we can do is… (judgment)</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本人が来ない以上、この話は進められない。</span> (If the person themselves isn't coming, we can't move this discussion forward.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>With the <span class='gd-jp'>た</span> form, it means \"it has already happened, so…\". With the dictionary form, it means \"since I'm going to do this / since this is my role, so…\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns need である: it's <span class='gd-jp'>学生である以上</span>, not 「学生な以上」. Writing <span class='gd-jp'>学生以上</span> directly would be read as a different meaning (student level or above).<br>・The second half can't be just a plain fact. You can't say 「雨が降った以上、試合が中止になった」, because \"the game was cancelled\" is just something that happened, not a resolve or a judgment.<br>・Don't mix it up with the other meanings of <span class='gd-jp'>以上</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>三人以上</span> means \"three people or more\", and <span class='gd-jp'>以上です</span> said at the end of a report means \"That's all.\"<br>・The tone is fairly serious and resolute. It's often used for topics like responsibility, promises, and roles. Using it for small matters sounds a bit exaggerated.</div><div class='gd-h'>How it differs from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>The one most easily confused with it is <b>から</b>, which simply gives a reason.<br><span class='gd-jp'>約束したから、行きます。</span> (Because we made a promise, I'll go.): just explains the reason.<br><span class='gd-jp'>約束した以上、行かなければならない。</span> (Now that we've promised, I have to go.): stresses \"this is settled, and I have a responsibility.\"<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったから、試合が中止になった。</span> (Because it rained, the game was cancelled.) works with から, but not with 以上, because what follows is just an event.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what follows is a resolve or judgment like \"I must / I should / all we can do is…\", use 以上. If you're just stating a cause and its result, use から.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears together with <span class='gd-jp'>べきだ</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>約束した以上、守るべきだ。</span> (Since we promised, we should keep it.) Later you'll also meet <span class='gd-jp'>からには</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>上は</span>, which mean almost the same thing. <span class='gd-jp'>上は</span> is more formal and more written in style.</div>","n3-23":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜上で」has two meanings, and <b>the form before it tells you which one</b>:<br>After the <b>た form</b>: \"do ～ properly first, <b>and then</b>…\", as in \"decide <b>after</b> talking it over.\"<br>After the <b>dictionary form</b> (the base form you find in a dictionary): \"<b>in the process of</b> doing ～\", as in \"when living in Japan, keigo is important.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>① Do it first, then…</span>た form + 上で / noun + の + 上で<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>相談する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>相談した上で</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>確認</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>確認の上で</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>よく考えた上で、返事します。</span> (I'll give you my answer after thinking it over carefully.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族とよく相談した上で、転職を決めた。</span> (I decided to change jobs after talking it over thoroughly with my family.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The point is that the first action is a <b>necessary preparation</b>, and only once it's done do you move on to the second. The second part often uses actions like <span class='gd-jp'>決める</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>申し込む</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>返事する</span>, which involve making decisions or handling procedures.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>② In the matter of ～</span>dictionary form + 上で / noun + の + 上で<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>生活する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>生活する上で</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>仕事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>仕事の上で</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本で生活する上で、敬語は大切だ。</span> (When living in Japan, keigo is important.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この本は日本語を勉強する上で役に立つ。</span> (This book is useful when studying Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事の上での付き合いは大切です。</span> (Relationships in the workplace are important.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>What follows is mostly an evaluation: <span class='gd-jp'>大切だ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>必要だ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>欠かせない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>役に立つ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>注意が必要だ</span>. When a noun follows, change the で to <b>での</b>, as in <span class='gd-jp'>仕事の上での付き合い</span> above.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often see it</div><div class='gd-sec'>On notices, forms, and store instructions you'll often see 「<b>ご + noun + の上</b>」, with the で frequently dropped. It's a very polite, written-style expression.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>内容をご確認の上、サインしてください。</span> (Please sign after confirming the contents.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use the wrong form and the meaning changes: <span class='gd-jp'>相談した上で</span> means \"after talking it over\", while <span class='gd-jp'>相談する上で</span> means \"in the matter of talking it over\" (e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>相談する上で大切なこと</span> = what's important when consulting someone). To say \"after doing ～, then…\", always use the た form.<br>・① isn't a casual \"and then\". <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べた上で、テレビを見た</span> sounds odd. For everyday sequences, <span class='gd-jp'>〜てから</span> is enough.<br>・Here 「上」 is read <b>うえ</b>. There's also <span class='gd-jp'>仕事上</span>, attached directly to a noun and read しごと<b>じょう</b>. It means about the same as <span class='gd-jp'>仕事の上で</span>, but the spelling and reading differ, so don't mix them up.<br>・Don't write it as 「上に」. <span class='gd-jp'>〜上に</span> means \"not only ～ but also ～\", which is a different grammar point.</div><div class='gd-h'>How it differs from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>た上で vs てから</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>家族と相談してから決めます。</span> (I'll talk with my family first, then decide: simply states the order.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>家族と相談した上で決めます。</span> (I'll talk it over properly with my family and decide based on that: stresses that you've seriously done this step.)<br>てから works in any situation. た上で is more formal and used for important decisions.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>る上で vs ために</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>日本で働くために、日本語を勉強しています。</span> (I'm studying Japanese in order to work in Japan.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本で働く上で、日本語は欠かせません。</span> (When working in Japan, Japanese is indispensable.)<br>Both can come out as \"to…\" in English (\"To work in Japan, you need Japanese\"), but ために is followed by <b>what you do for that purpose</b>, while る上で is followed by <b>what's needed or important for that activity</b>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say \"do ～ properly first, then…\", use <b>た上で</b>. To say \"when doing ～, what matters is…\", use <b>る上で</b>. If what follows is \"I do something (for that goal)\", use <b>ために</b>.</div>","n3-27":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をはじめ」means <b>\"starting with ~\" or \"~ and others, all of which…\"</b>. You name the most representative, best-known item first, then bring in the rest of the same group. It's like introducing Taiwanese street food: you start with \"bubble tea,\" then add \"beef noodle soup, fried chicken cutlet, and so on.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Only a <b>noun</b> can come before it. <span class='gd-jp'>をはじめ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>をはじめとして</span> mean the same thing; adding として makes it more formal and complete.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>東京</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>東京をはじめ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>社長</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>社長をはじめとして</span></span><br>If you want to attach a noun directly afterward, use <b>をはじめとする＋noun</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>寿司をはじめとする日本料理</span> (Japanese food, with sushi as the leading example).</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving a representative</span>Name the most famous one first, then say there are many more.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本には、東京をはじめ、大きな都市がたくさんある。</span> (Japan has many big cities, starting with Tokyo.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The top-ranking person leads</span>It goes from the person at the very top to everyone else. You hear it a lot at companies and ceremonies.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長をはじめ、社員全員が会議に出た。</span> (Everyone attended the meeting, from the company president to all the employees.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>をはじめとする</span>Used as a modifier, with a noun right after it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寿司をはじめとする日本料理は、海外でも人気だ。</span> (Japanese food, sushi first among it, is popular overseas too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Put the item that is <b>most representative, most famous, or highest in rank</b> first. <span class='gd-jp'>社員をはじめ、社長も参加した</span> sounds odd because the order is backwards. Say <span class='gd-jp'>社長をはじめ、社員も参加した</span>.<br>・You must follow it with \"and others.\" It often pairs with <span class='gd-jp'>や</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>など</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>多くの</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>全員</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>も</span>. If you're only talking about one thing, you can't use it.<br>・The はじめ here does not mean \"beginning\" in time. \"Starting in September\" is <span class='gd-jp'>9月から</span>, not 9月をはじめ.<br>・The tone is fairly formal, common in news, guided tours, and speeches. When chatting with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>京都とか奈良とか</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を中心に</b> also puts one thing first, but the meaning is different. Look at the same rain:<br><span class='gd-jp'>東京をはじめ、関東の各地で雨が降った。</span> (It rained across the Kanto area, Tokyo included.)<br>→ Everywhere got rain; Tokyo is just named first.<br><span class='gd-jp'>東京を中心に雨が降った。</span> (It rained centered on Tokyo.)<br>→ The rain was concentrated around Tokyo, which is the core, and it thins out the farther you go.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"~ and all these others…,\" use をはじめ; to say \"the focus or core is ~,\" use を中心に.</div>","n3-31":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ほど」means <b>\"to the extent that…\" or \"so much that…\"</b>. Instead of just saying \"very,\" you throw out a picture and let the listener feel how extreme it is. It's like English \"tired to death\" or \"laughed until I cried\"; in Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>死ぬほど疲れた</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>涙が出るほど笑った</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>First, get the sentence order</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English we often say \"I was exhausted\" first, then \"to death.\" Japanese tends to go the <b>other way around</b>: first the picture, then what it describes.<br>【picture】＋ほど＋【what it's like】</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>涙が出るほど感動した。</span> (I was moved to tears.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>眠れないほど楽しみにしていた。</span> (I was looking forward to it so much I couldn't sleep.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: dictionary form or ない form, then just add ほど.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>涙が出る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>涙が出るほど</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>眠れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>眠れないほど</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: just add ほど.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>痛い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>痛いほど</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add な first, then ほど.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不思議</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不思議なほど</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: just add ほど.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>山</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>山ほど</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>死ぬほど</span>Very common in speech. It means \"like crazy,\" not that someone is actually dying.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメン、死ぬほどおいしい。</span> (This ramen is insanely good.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>山ほど</span>So many it piles up like a mountain, meaning \"a ton.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題が山ほどある。</span> (I have a mountain of homework.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>痛いほどわかる</span>You understand so well it hurts, meaning \"I totally get it.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その気持ち、痛いほどわかるよ。</span> (I totally understand how you feel.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ほどだ</span>You can also put it at the end of a sentence: state the situation first, then add the exaggerated degree last.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒くて、手が動かないほどだった。</span> (It was so cold that my hands wouldn't move.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't follow English word order: say <span class='gd-jp'>涙が出るほど感動した</span>, not \"感動したほど涙が出る.\"<br>・Don't forget the な with な-adjectives: it's <span class='gd-jp'>不思議なほど</span>, not 不思議ほど.<br>・When ほど follows a <b>quantity</b>, the meaning changes: <span class='gd-jp'>3時間ほど待った</span> means \"I waited about 3 hours.\" Here it means \"about,\" not exaggeration.<br>・With a negative, it's a different usage too: <span class='gd-jp'>今年の夏は去年ほど暑くない</span> means \"This summer isn't as hot as last year.\" It's a comparison, not \"hot to the point of…\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ほど vs. くらい</b>: for \"extreme degree,\" they're mostly interchangeable.<br><span class='gd-jp'>涙が出るほど笑った。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>涙が出るくらい笑った。</span> (Both mean \"I laughed until I cried.\")<br>くらい is more casual, and ほど is a little more formal.<br>But for <b>a low degree, \"just a small thing,\"</b> only くらい works:<br><span class='gd-jp'>名前くらい自分で書いて。</span> (At least write your own name.)<br>You can't swap in ほど here, because ほど pushes toward \"extreme.\"</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ほど vs. ば〜ほど</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>練習すればするほど上手になる</span> means \"the more you practice, the better you get.\" It describes two things changing together, which is different from the \"to the extent that\" meaning here.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"so extreme that…,\" both ほど and くらい work; to say \"a small thing like this, at least…,\" only くらい works; when you see 「～ば～ほど」, it means \"the more ~, the more ~.\"</div>","n3-25":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The 限 in <span class='gd-jp'>に限り</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>に限って</span> means “limit,” so the idea is <b>narrowing the scope down to just this one thing</b>. It has two very different uses. One is the notice-board <b>“limited to ～”</b>. The other is the everyday complaint <b>“of all ～, it had to be ～”</b>, like saying in English, “Of course the light turns red just when I'm in a hurry.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It goes after a <b>noun</b>.<br>A noun attaches directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本日に限り</span></span><br>To say “at times when ～” or “on days when ～,” first finish the phrase with a verb plus とき or 日, then attach it:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急いでいる</span> → add とき → <span class='gd-jp'>急いでいるときに限って</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>傘を持っていない</span> → add 日 → <span class='gd-jp'>傘を持っていない日に限って</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Limited to ～</span>You'll often see this on store promotions, tickets, and rules. It sounds formal and is mostly written as <span class='gd-jp'>に限り</span>. It means “only within this range.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先着100名様に限り、特典をプレゼントします。</span>(A special gift for the first 100 customers only.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このチケットは当日に限り有効です。</span>(This ticket is valid on the day only.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Of all ～, it had to be ～</span>Things are usually fine, but trouble strikes at exactly the worst moment. This use takes <span class='gd-jp'>に限って</span>. What follows is usually something unlucky or unwelcome, and the tone carries a bit of a complaint.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>傘を持っていない日に限って、急に雨が降る。</span>(It suddenly rains on the very day I don't have an umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>急いでいるときに限って、信号に引っかかる。</span>(I always get stuck at a red light just when I'm in a hurry.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>休みの日に限って、朝早く目が覚める。</span>(I wake up early on exactly the days I'm off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not them, of all people</span>Put a person you trust before it, and follow with “it can't be that ～.” It means “others might, but they absolutely wouldn't.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの子に限って、うそをつくはずがない。</span>(My child, of all people, would never lie.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの真面目な田中さんに限って、約束を忘れるなんてありえない。</span>(Someone as serious as Mr. Tanaka could never forget a promise.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mix-ups</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The “limited to” meaning is very formal, the voice of notices and ads. When chatting with a friend and saying “just today,” <span class='gd-jp'>今日だけ</span> is enough. <span class='gd-jp'>今日に限り</span> would sound like you're reading out a public notice.<br>・The “of all times” <span class='gd-jp'>に限って</span> is followed by something bad almost every time. Don't use it for good things.<br>・The “not them, of all people” use must be followed by a negative, such as <span class='gd-jp'>はずがない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ありえない</span>. Even if you stop at <span class='gd-jp'>うちの子に限って</span>, a Japanese listener will understand you're about to say “impossible.”<br>・Don't confuse it with <b><span class='gd-jp'>に限る</span></b>. That one means “～ is the best / nothing beats ～”: <span class='gd-jp'>暑い日は冷たいビールに限る。</span> (On a hot day, nothing beats a cold beer.) The spelling differs only slightly, but the meaning is completely different.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of these are about “today,” but the usage changes the meaning a lot:<br><span class='gd-jp'>本日に限り、入場無料です。</span>(Free admission today only.) → a notice, “limited to”<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日に限って、財布を忘れた。</span>(Of all days, I forgot my wallet today.) → a complaint, “of all times”<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの人に限って、そんなことはしない。</span>(They, of all people, wouldn't do such a thing.) → trust, “not them”</div><div class='gd-warn'>A quick test: if you see it on a notice or in the rules, it's “limited to.” If you're talking about your own bad luck, it's “of all times.” If a person comes before it and “impossible” comes after, it's “not them, of all people.”</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Make it negative as <span class='gd-jp'>に限らず</span> and it means “not only ～”: <span class='gd-jp'>この店は週末に限らず、いつも混んでいる。</span> (This shop is crowded not only on weekends but all the time.)</div>","n3-32":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜くらい」 works like English <b>\"so ~ that ~\" or \"to the point of ~\"</b>. In English you say \"laughed so hard my stomach hurt\" or \"so tired I didn't want to move.\" You paint a concrete picture to show how extreme something was. Japanese does this with くらい: <span class='gd-jp'>お腹が痛くなるくらい笑った。</span> (I laughed so hard my stomach hurt.) <span class='gd-jp'>くらい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ぐらい</span> mean exactly the same thing, so you can use either.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Exaggerated description</span>Put a vivid picture before くらい. What follows is the state you actually want to describe.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>手が震えるくらい緊張した。</span> (I was so nervous my hands were shaking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>泣きたいくらい悔しかった。</span> (I was so frustrated I wanted to cry.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝の電車は動けないくらい混んでいる。</span> (The morning train is so crowded you can't even move.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just a little thing</span>The opposite direction: you play the degree down, with a \"no big deal\" feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これくらい、自分でできるよ。</span> (I can do something like this myself.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At least ~</span>Used for \"the bare minimum you should do,\" often with a hint of complaint.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しくても、電話くらいしてよ。</span> (Even if you're busy, you could at least call.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No one/nothing is more ~ than</span>「Noun＋くらい～はない」 is for strong praise or emphasis.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんくらい優しい人はいない。</span> (There's no one as kind as Mr. Tanaka.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the dictionary form or ない form. For things you want to do, you can also use 「たい」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>震える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>震えるくらい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>動けない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>動けないくらい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泣きたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣きたいくらい</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>痛い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>痛いくらい</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「な」 first. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不思議</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不思議なくらい</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly, with no 「の」 in between. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>これ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>これくらい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>田中さん</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんくらい</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Number (quantity) ＋くらい means \"about,\" as in <span class='gd-jp'>三時間くらい</span> (about three hours). With verbs, adjectives, or non-quantity nouns like <span class='gd-jp'>これ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>田中さん</span>, くらい means \"to the extent of ~.\"<br>・Don't flip the order. What comes before くらい is the picture you're using as a comparison, and what comes after is the real state: <span class='gd-jp'>手が震えるくらい緊張した</span>, not 「緊張したくらい手が震えた」.<br>・The verb before くらい is usually just the dictionary form or ない form. For past events, only the end of the sentence changes to past tense: <span class='gd-jp'>声が出ないくらい驚いた。</span> (I was so shocked I couldn't speak.)<br>・Don't forget 「な」 with な-adjectives, and don't add an extra 「の」 after nouns: say <span class='gd-jp'>これくらい</span>, not 「これのくらい」.<br>・The \"just a little thing\" use sounds dismissive. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>これくらいできるでしょう</span> (surely you can do this much) to someone senior to you sounds very rude.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ほど</b> also means \"to the extent of ~.\" For exaggerated descriptions you can use either one, but <b>くらい is more conversational, while ほど is more formal and written</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>手が震えるくらい緊張した。</span> (chatting with a friend)<br><span class='gd-jp'>手が震えるほど緊張した。</span> (writing an essay, giving a speech)</div><div class='gd-sec'>But for the <b>\"just a little thing\" and \"at least\"</b> meanings, only くらい works. <span class='gd-jp'>これほど</span> instead means \"this much ~,\" meaning you feel the degree is large:<br><span class='gd-jp'>これくらい簡単だよ。</span> (It's easy, just this much.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>これほど難しいとは思わなかった。</span> (I never thought it would be this difficult.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, comparisons like \"not as ~ as ~\" need ほど: <span class='gd-jp'>今年は去年ほど寒くない。</span> (This year isn't as cold as last year.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"just a little thing\" or \"at least ~,\" use くらい. For \"not as ~ as ~\" or formal writing, use ほど. For simply exaggerating a degree, either works, though you hear くらい more in speech.</div>","n3-35":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜気味」 means <b>\"a bit ~\" or \"a slight touch of ~\"</b>. Say your throat feels off and you tell someone, \"I think I'm coming down with a bit of a cold.\" In Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味です</span>. It's mostly used when your body or situation shows a <b>small, not-so-good sign</b> that isn't serious yet.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Physical condition</span>The most common use: a bit of a cold, a bit tired, a bit overweight, a bit short on sleep.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味なので、今日は早く寝ます。</span> (I'm coming down with a cold, so I'll go to bed early today.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、ちょっと太り気味なんだ。</span> (I've been putting on a little weight lately.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝不足気味で、授業中に眠くなった。</span> (I'm a bit sleep-deprived, so I got sleepy during class.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Feelings, progress of things</span>You can also use it for being a bit nervous or things running a bit behind.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>初めての発表なので、少し緊張気味です。</span> (It's my first presentation, so I'm a little nervous.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>工事が予定より遅れ気味だ。</span> (The construction is running a bit behind schedule.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: take the ます form, drop ます, and attach 気味 directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>疲れます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>疲れ気味</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>太ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>太り気味</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅れます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅れ気味</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>風邪</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>緊張</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>緊張気味</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>運動不足</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>運動不足気味</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜気味」 itself behaves like a noun. Add <span class='gd-jp'>だ／です</span> at the end of a sentence, use <span class='gd-jp'>の</span> before a noun, and use <span class='gd-jp'>で</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>なので</span> to connect to the next clause.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味の人は、マスクをしてください。</span> (If you're coming down with a cold, please wear a mask.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When attached to another word, it's read <b>ぎみ</b>, not きみ: <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味</span> is read かぜぎみ.<br>・Verbs must be in the ます-stem form (ます removed), not the dictionary form: say <span class='gd-jp'>太り気味</span>, not 太る気味.<br>・Adjectives don't attach directly to 気味. For \"a bit hot,\" you don't say 暑い気味. You say <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと暑い</span>.<br>・It's used almost only for unfavorable situations. You wouldn't use 気味 for \"a bit happy\" or \"a bit energetic.\"<br>・Before a noun, use <span class='gd-jp'>の</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味の人</span>, not 風邪気味な人.<br>・Even though 気味 already means \"a bit,\" Japanese speakers very often add <span class='gd-jp'>少し</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと</span> before it. It sounds natural, not redundant.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>気味が悪い</span> (read きみがわるい) means \"creepy, unsettling.\" It has nothing to do with this grammar point, so don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜がち</b> = <b>tends to</b> be that way, is prone to it (frequency, habit).<br><b>〜気味</b> = there's a sign of being that way <b>right now</b> (current state).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はちょっと風邪気味です。</span> (I'm a bit under the weather today, like a cold coming on.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冬は風邪をひきがちです。</span> (I tend to catch colds in winter.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜っぽい</b> can also be used as <span class='gd-jp'>風邪っぽい</span> (seems like a cold). The meaning is very close to <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味</span>, but it's more casual, suited to chatting with friends. With your boss or a doctor, <span class='gd-jp'>風邪気味です</span> is more appropriate. っぽい also has a much wider range: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもっぽい</span> (childish) and <span class='gd-jp'>白っぽい</span> (whitish) both use っぽい, and these can't be replaced with 気味.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"lately I often ~,\" use がち. For \"right now I'm a bit ~,\" use 気味. For a casual \"seems like ~\" with friends, you can use っぽい.</div>","n3-36":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜っぱなし」 means <b>\"left that way, never dealt with\"</b>, as in \"left on and never turned off.\" Think of a parent nagging: \"You left the light on again!\" \"Your clothes are just dumped on the floor!\" In Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>つけっぱなし</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>出しっぱなし</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>脱ぎっぱなし</span>. It usually carries a tone of <b>complaint or annoyance</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the ます form, drop ます, and add 「っぱなし」 directly. It works the same for all three verb groups, so there's no need to split them up.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>つけます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>つけっぱなし</span></span> (left on)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出しっぱなし</span></span> (taken out and not put away)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>脱ぎます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>脱ぎっぱなし</span></span> (taken off and left lying around)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>開けます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>開けっぱなし</span></span> (opened and left open)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Should have been undone, but wasn't</span>You did something, and afterwards you were supposed to turn it off, put it away, or tidy up, but you just left it. This is by far the most common use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テレビをつけっぱなしで寝てしまった。</span> (I fell asleep with the TV still on.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>水が出しっぱなしだよ。</span> (The water's still running!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>服を脱ぎっぱなしにしないで。</span> (Don't just leave your clothes lying where you took them off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Staying in the same state nonstop</span>One thing goes on and on without a break, usually with a feeling of \"for too long, I'm exhausted.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は一日中立ちっぱなしで、足が痛い。</span> (I've been on my feet all day, and my legs hurt.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝から働きっぱなしで疲れた。</span> (I've been working nonstop since morning and I'm tired.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>・<b>〜っぱなしだ</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開けっぱなしだ。</span> (The window has been left open.)<br>・<b>〜っぱなしで、〜</b>: what follows is the result, like \"fell asleep with the TV on\" above.<br>・<b>〜っぱなしにする</b>: to leave something as it is. To tell someone not to, say <span class='gd-jp'>〜っぱなしにしないで</span>.<br>・<b>〜っぱなしの＋noun</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>出しっぱなしの本</span> (a book that was taken out and never put back)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the <b>ます form minus ます</b>, not the dictionary form. Say <span class='gd-jp'>つけっぱなし</span>, not つけるっぱなし.<br>・There's a <b>small っ</b> in the middle. Pause for a beat: 「つけ・っ・ぱなし」, not つけぱなし.<br>・It often carries a complaint about something that should have been dealt with and wasn't (especially the \"should have undone it\" use). If you're just stating that something was done in a certain state, with no complaint, 「〜たまま」 is more natural. But the \"on my feet all day / working nonstop\" use can't be swapped for たまま: <span class='gd-jp'>立ったまま</span> only means \"standing (while doing something)\"; it doesn't mean \"standing for a long time.\"<br>・The point of っぱなし is \"leaving it in that state,\" and it isn't always an accident. You can also use it for something kept that way on purpose, like <span class='gd-jp'>換気のために窓を開けっぱなしにしている。</span> (I'm keeping the window open all the time for ventilation.) If you want to stress \"set it up in advance for a purpose,\" use 〜ておく.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take \"went out with the window open\":<br><b>〜たまま</b> = simply states the condition, with no particular emotion: <span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けたまま出かけた。</span> (I went out with the window open.)<br><b>〜っぱなし</b> = carries the annoyance of \"should have closed it, didn't\": <span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けっぱなしで出かけた。</span> (I went out leaving the window wide open.)<br><b>〜ておく</b> = keeping it that way <b>on purpose</b>, with a goal: <span class='gd-jp'>暑いから、窓を開けておいた。</span> (It was hot, so I left the window open.)<br>The attachment differs too: たまま takes the た form, ておく takes the て form, and っぱなし takes the ます form minus ます.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to complain \"why isn't this ever put away,\" use っぱなし. To just say \"in that state,\" use たまま. For \"deliberately set it up beforehand,\" use ておく.</div>","n3-37":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both express \"a good fit for a certain kind of person.\" The difference is <b>whether it was made specifically for them</b>.<br><b>向き（むき）</b> = \"well suited to ~.\" It looks at the nature of the thing itself, regardless of who it was originally made for.<br><b>向け（むけ）</b> = \"designed specifically for ~.\" It was aimed at that group from the start, like \"kids' edition\" or \"for beginners\" in English.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both attach directly to a <b>noun</b>, to show \"for whom\" or \"for what situation.\"<br>At the end of a sentence: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供向きだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供向けだ</span></span><br>Before a noun: you can add 「の」 (<span class='gd-jp'>初心者向けの講座</span>), or join them directly into one compound (<span class='gd-jp'>初心者向け講座</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>子供向け番組</span>). Both are correct. The joined style is especially common in titles, ads, and product descriptions. When making your own sentences, the version with 「の」 is the safest.<br>Before a verb: <span class='gd-jp'>向けに</span> + verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>作る</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> means \"made for ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>向き</span>The thing's own qualities happen to suit someone. It's often used to comment on houses, jobs, personalities, and clothes.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は一人暮らし向きだ。</span> (This room is well suited to living alone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は営業向きの性格だ。</span> (He has a personality well suited to sales.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>For \"not suited,\" say <b>不向き</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この靴は長く歩くのには不向きだ。</span> (These shoes aren't suited to walking long distances.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>向け</span>A product, program, or service was aimed at a certain group when it was designed.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>外国人向けのパンフレットをください。</span> (Could I have a brochure for foreign visitors, please?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このアプリは高齢者向けに作られている。</span> (This app is designed for older people.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Pronunciation: 向き is <b>むき</b> and 向け is <b>むけ</b>. They differ only in the last sound, so listen carefully.<br>・For \"made for ~,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>向けに作る</span>. You can't say 向きに作る, because 向き isn't about the purpose of making something.<br>・\"Not suited\" exists only as <span class='gd-jp'>不向き</span>. There's no 「不向け」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>南向きの部屋</span> means \"a south-facing room.\" Here 向き means <b>direction</b>, a different meaning from \"suited to,\" so don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same book and its relation to children:<br><span class='gd-jp'>これは子供向けの本だ。</span> (This is a children's book, written for kids from the start.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>大人向けに書かれた本だが、やさしいので子供向きでもある。</span> (This book was written for adults, but it's easy, so it also suits children.)<br>The second sentence shows the difference clearly: who it was made for is <b>向け</b>, and who it turns out to suit is <b>向き</b>. One book can be both.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if you can swap in \"made specifically for ~,\" use 向け. If you just feel \"it suits ~ well,\" use 向き.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>向き comes from the verb <span class='gd-jp'>向く</span> (to be suited), so you can also say <span class='gd-jp'>この仕事は私に向いている。</span> (This job suits me.) and <span class='gd-jp'>私は細かい作業に向いていない。</span> (I'm not suited to detailed work.).</div>","n3-40":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようになる」means <b>\"come to ~\"</b> or <b>\"get to the point where I can ~ / start ~ing.\"</b> The key is <b>it wasn't like this before, but now it is</b>, like \"I can finally travel to Japan on my own now\" or \"my kid has started putting on shoes by himself lately.\" For a change that has already happened, you usually use the past form 「<span class='gd-jp'>ようになった</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Ability changed</span>You couldn't before, but now you can: put the <b>potential form</b> (the form that means \"can ~\") in front.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読めるようになった</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きられる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きられるようになった</span></span><br><b>Exception</b>: verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>見える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>聞こえる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> already carry the meaning of \"can,\" so you use the dictionary form directly: <span class='gd-jp'>わかるようになった</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>息子がひらがなを読めるようになった。</span>(My son can read hiragana now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>練習して、泳げるようになった。</span>(After practicing, I learned how to swim.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Habit changed</span>You weren't doing it before, but now you've started: dictionary form + ようになる.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>結婚してから、毎日料理するようになった。</span>(Since getting married, I've started cooking every day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度も通ううちに、店員と雑談するようになった。</span>(After going there so many times, I gradually started chatting with the staff.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Quick review of the potential form</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「え」-row sound of the same row, then add る.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → change む to め → <span class='gd-jp'>読める</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop る and add られる.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span>（こられる）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most often</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようになりたい</span>You want to become able to do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語がペラペラ話せるようになりたい。</span>(I want to get to where I can speak fluent Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようになる</span>The present form says \"you'll come to ~ later.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日練習すれば、すぐ泳げるようになりますよ。</span>(If you practice every day, you'll be able to swim in no time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようになってきた</span>The change is happening little by little.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語が少しわかるようになってきた。</span>(I'm gradually starting to understand a bit of Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・To say you \"learned to do\" something, use the potential form: <span class='gd-jp'>泳げるようになった</span> means \"I learned to swim,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐようになった</span> means \"I've started swimming\" (it became a habit). They're different. But with <span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>見える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>聞こえる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span>, use the dictionary form directly; you can't say わかれる or 見えられる.<br>・To say \"no longer ~,\" 「なくなる」 is more common in everyday speech: <span class='gd-jp'>辛いものが食べられなくなった</span> (I can't eat spicy food anymore), <span class='gd-jp'>タバコを吸わなくなった</span> (I don't smoke anymore).<br>・When you want to say \"become some state,\" <b>adjectives and nouns can't connect directly to ようになる</b>: \"became busy\" is <span class='gd-jp'>忙しくなった</span>, \"became pretty\" is <span class='gd-jp'>きれいになった</span>, and \"became a teacher\" is <span class='gd-jp'>先生になった</span>. Extra note: noun + <span class='gd-jp'>のようになる</span> means \"become like ~,\" for example <span class='gd-jp'>先生のようになりたい</span> (I want to become like a teacher). It does not mean \"become a teacher,\" so they're different.<br>・You can also use it for results you worked hard to achieve, not only for things that happen \"naturally.\" What matters is that <b>the result has changed</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take \"getting up early\" as an example:<br><b>ようにする</b> = deliberately making an effort: <span class='gd-jp'>毎朝6時に起きるようにしている。</span> (I try to get up at 6 every morning.)<br><b>ようになる</b> = the result has changed: <span class='gd-jp'>朝6時に起きられるようになった。</span> (I can get up at 6 now.)<br><b>ことになる</b> = it was decided or arranged: <span class='gd-jp'>来月から、6時に出勤することになった。</span> (It's been decided that I'll start work at 6 from next month.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: \"try to ~\" is ようにする, \"can ~ now\" is ようになる, and \"it's been decided that ~\" is ことになる.</div>","n3-45":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜はずだ」means <b>\"should be ~\" or \"is supposed to be ~\"</b> (based on reasoning). You have grounds (something you saw, something you know, something arranged in advance), and from them you draw a conclusion you're quite confident about. For example, if you see the lights on at a friend's house, you think \"he should be home,\" which in Japanese is <span class='gd-jp'>家にいるはずだ</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます or です).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るはずだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たはずだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いはずだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: replace だ with な.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利なはずだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: replace だ with の.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休みだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みのはずだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>To be more polite, change だ to です: <span class='gd-jp'>来るはずです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reasoned conclusion</span>You draw a conclusion from known facts.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は医者だから、詳しいはずだ。</span>(He's a doctor, so he should know a lot about it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電気がついているから、家にいるはずだ。</span>(The lights are on, so he should be home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>According to plan or schedule</span>What should happen based on a timetable or something you agreed on.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは3時に来るはずです。</span>(Mr. Tanaka should arrive at three.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>荷物は明日届くはずです。</span>(The package should arrive tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I was sure ~, but…</span>Use 「はずなのに」 when you're certain, yet things turned out differently, with a \"that's strange\" feeling.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここに置いたはずなのに、ない。</span>(I'm sure I put it here, but it's gone.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No wonder</span>You first notice the result, then discover the reason and it clicks.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒いはずだ。雪が降っている。</span>(No wonder it's so cold. It's snowing.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English \"should\" has two meanings: \"it's probably the case\" and \"you ought to.\" <b>はず has only the first one</b>. \"You should go to bed earlier\" is advice, so you can't use はず.<br>・Nouns need の: it's <span class='gd-jp'>医者のはずだ</span>, not 「医者だはずだ」; with な-adjectives, say <span class='gd-jp'>便利なはずだ</span>.<br>・Don't use はず when you have no grounds and are just guessing. はず sounds very confident, so a wrong guess comes across as presumptuous.<br>・Don't use はず for things you yourself want to do. \"I plan to go tomorrow\" is <span class='gd-jp'>明日行くつもりだ</span>. はず is about what should happen by logic or by plan, not about your intention.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>はずだ</b> = it should be so (reasoned conclusion): <span class='gd-jp'>会議は3時に始まるはずだ。</span> (According to the schedule, the meeting should start at three.)<br><b>べきだ</b> = you ought to do it (obligation, opinion): <span class='gd-jp'>会議は時間どおりに始めるべきだ。</span> (Meetings should start on time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ないはずだ</b> = it shouldn't be so (calm conclusion): <span class='gd-jp'>彼は今日来ないはずだ。</span> (He shouldn't be coming today.)<br><b>はずがない</b> = there's no way (strong denial): <span class='gd-jp'>彼が来るはずがない。</span> (There's no way he'd come.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use はず for \"it should be so,\" べき for \"you ought to do ~\"; use ないはず for \"probably not\" and はずがない for \"impossible.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The past form 「はずだった」 = it was supposed to ~, but didn't: <span class='gd-jp'>3時に着くはずだったのに、電車が遅れた。</span> (I was supposed to arrive at three, but the train was delayed.)<br>Next, you can learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜はずがない</span>, which expresses \"impossible.\"</div>","n3-46":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜はずがない」means <b>\"there's no way ～\" / \"it can't possibly be ～\"</b>. You have a reason in mind: she's careful, the door was locked, he practiced for so long. Following that reasoning through, you conclude \"this can't possibly be true.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reasoning it out</span>You have grounds for it, so you say \"impossible\" with confidence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女があんなミスをするはずがない。</span>(There's no way she'd make a mistake like that.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんなに練習したんだから、負けるはずがない。</span>(He practiced that much, so there's no way he'll lose.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not believing what's in front of you</span>When something unexpected happens, you blurt out \"No way...\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>え、財布がない？そんなはずない。さっきまであったのに。</span>(Huh, my wallet's gone? That can't be. It was right here a moment ago.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>負ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>負けるはずがない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暑い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暑いはずがない</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「な」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>下手なはずがない</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>犯人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>犯人のはずがない</span></span><br>To say \"there's no way they did it\" or \"there's no way they don't ～\", put the past or negative form before はず:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったはずがない</span></span> (there's no way they've been there)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知らないはずがない</span></span> (there's no way they don't know)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まだ4月だから、そんなに暑いはずがない。</span>(It's only April, so it can't be that hot.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10年も日本に住んでいるんだから、日本語が下手なはずがない。</span>(He's lived in Japan for 10 years, so there's no way his Japanese is bad.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんなに優しい人が犯人のはずがない。</span>(Someone that kind can't possibly be the culprit.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'>「が」often becomes 「は」 for stronger emphasis, and in casual speech the particle is often dropped altogether. The version you'll hear most is <span class='gd-jp'>そんなはずはない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>そんなはずない</span> (\"That can't be,\" \"How could that be?\"). Memorize the whole phrase.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives can't attach directly: say <span class='gd-jp'>犯人のはずがない</span>, not 「犯人だはずがない」.<br>・English \"can't\" covers several meanings, but Japanese keeps them apart. はずがない only means \"impossible / can't be true.\" You can't use it for \"not allowed\" or \"not able to (ability).\" \"You can't smoke here\" is <span class='gd-jp'>ここでたばこを吸ってはいけません</span>, and \"I can't swim\" is <span class='gd-jp'>泳げません</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>知らないはずがない</span> means \"there's no way he doesn't know,\" which amounts to \"he must know.\" Don't translate it as \"there's no way he knows.\"<br>・The tone is strong. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>あなたにできるはずがない</span> (\"There's no way you can do it\") to someone's face is hurtful, so be careful.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same question, \"Will he come?\", and note how the tone changes:<br><b>はずだ</b> = he should come (confident): <span class='gd-jp'>彼は来るはずだ。</span> (He should be coming.)<br><b>ないはずだ</b> = he shouldn't come (leaning negative, but not final): <span class='gd-jp'>彼は来ないはずだ。</span> (He shouldn't be coming.)<br><b>はずがない</b> = absolutely impossible (final): <span class='gd-jp'>彼が来るはずがない。</span> (There's no way he's coming.)<br><b>わけがない</b> = nearly the same meaning as はずがない, and interchangeable in most cases. You hear it more in everyday chat, and it sounds blunter and more emotional: <span class='gd-jp'>彼が来るわけがない。</span> (How could he possibly come?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use ないはずだ for \"probably not\" and はずがない for \"absolutely impossible.\" When talking with friends or when you're more worked up, people often switch to わけがない.</div>","n3-48":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことだ」is what an elder or senior says when giving you advice: <b>\"Take my word for it, just ～\" / \"What you need to do is ～.\"</b> For example, a senior tells you, \"Want to pass N3? Just memorize vocabulary every day.\" In Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>毎日単語を覚えることだ。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Telling someone to do something: dictionary form + ことだ<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休むことだ</span></span> (just get some rest)<br>Telling someone not to do something: ない form + ことだ<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>無理をしない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>無理をしないことだ</span></span> (don't push yourself)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving advice</span>You often first say \"if you want to ～\" or \"in order to ～,\" then give the answer with ことだ.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風邪のときは、ゆっくり休むことだ。</span>(When you have a cold, just rest up properly.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やせたいなら、甘いものを減らすことだ。</span>(If you want to lose weight, cut down on sweets.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Advising against something</span>With the ない form, the tone is like \"just don't ～.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試験の前の日は、夜遅くまで勉強しないことだ。</span>(The day before the exam, don't stay up late studying.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Besides face-to-face advice, you'll also see this pattern often in self-help books, newspaper columns, and seniors' words of experience.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・This is a <b>top-down</b> tone, with a bit of an \"I know better than you\" feel. Don't use it with teachers, bosses, or customers. Even changing it to <span class='gd-jp'>〜ことです</span> is still impolite. With someone senior, use <span class='gd-jp'>〜たほうがいいと思います</span> instead.<br>・Only the dictionary form or ない form can come before it, not the た form: <span class='gd-jp'>休んだことだ</span> does not mean \"you should rest.\"<br>・If an adjective comes before it, as in <span class='gd-jp'>うらやましいことだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>残念なことだ</span>, it expresses a feeling (how enviable, how regrettable), so it's <b>not advice</b>. Don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say a friend has a cold but insists on going to work:<br><b>ことだ</b> = advice aimed at you: <span class='gd-jp'>今日は休むことだ。</span> (Take the day off today.)<br><b>べきだ</b> = reasoning about what one should or shouldn't do: <span class='gd-jp'>病気のときは休むべきだ。</span> (When you're sick, you should rest.) べきだ can also look back critically: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日休むべきだった。</span> (You should have taken yesterday off.) ことだ has no such use.<br><b>ものだ</b> = common sense in society: <span class='gd-jp'>体調が悪いときは無理をしないものだ。</span> (When you're feeling unwell, you shouldn't push yourself.)<br><b>ことはない</b> = \"there's no need to ～,\" used to reassure someone: <span class='gd-jp'>そんなに心配することはない。</span> (There's no need to worry that much.) This differs from <span class='gd-jp'>心配しないことだ。</span> (Just don't worry.): the former says \"no need,\" while the latter urges you \"not to.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use ことだ for \"just do ～\" to the person in front of you; べきだ for reasoning about what one should or shouldn't do; ものだ for common sense everyone knows; and ことはない for \"no need to ～.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜ことはない</span> (no need to ～) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜べきだ</span> (should ～). Memorize the three together and you'll be least likely to mix them up.</div>","n3-38":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜一方だ」 means <b>“just keeps getting more and more ~,” “only gets worse,” “goes from bad to worse”</b>. Something keeps changing in the same direction, with no sign of stopping or turning back, and <b>it's usually a change for the worse</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>物価は上がる一方だ。</span>(Prices only go up, never down.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Dictionary form of the verb (the base form you find in a dictionary) + 一方だ</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>増える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増える一方だ</span></span> (keeps increasing)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>悪化する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悪化する一方だ</span></span> (keeps getting worse)<br>The verb in front has to be a <b>“change” verb</b>, like <span class='gd-jp'>増える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>減る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>上がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>下がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>広がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>進む</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>形容詞</span>Adjectives can't attach directly. First turn them into 「〜くなる」 or 「〜になる」 (become ~), then add 一方だ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → change い to く and add なる → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなる一方だ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便</span> → add になる → <span class='gd-jp'>不便になる一方だ</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Complaining that things are getting worse</span>Very common in the news and when you talk with people about how things are going.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、仕事が増える一方だ。</span>(Lately my workload just keeps growing.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父の病気は悪くなる一方だ。</span>(My father's illness keeps getting worse.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町は人が減って、寂しくなる一方だ。</span>(This town keeps losing people and getting lonelier and lonelier.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Neutral trends</span>It doesn't have to be bad news. You can also use it for something that's simply shooting upward.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このアプリの利用者は増える一方だ。</span>(This app's users just keep increasing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>For past events, use <b>一方だった</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>その後、二人の関係は悪くなる一方だった。</span>(After that, the two of them kept growing further apart.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English “keeps ~ing” can tempt you to use this for “keeps doing an action.” That's wrong. <b>一方だ is only for change</b>:<br>　×<span class='gd-jp'>弟は毎日ゲームをする一方だ。</span><br>　○<span class='gd-jp'>弟は毎日ゲームばかりしている。</span>(My brother plays video games every day.)<br>・What comes before must be the <b>dictionary form</b>. Don't attach ている or ます: ×<span class='gd-jp'>増えている一方だ</span>, ×<span class='gd-jp'>増えます一方だ</span>. To be more polite, just change the ending: <span class='gd-jp'>増える一方です</span>.<br>・Don't attach i-adjectives directly: ×<span class='gd-jp'>寒い一方だ</span>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなる一方だ</span>.<br>・The tone carries a helpless feeling of “no improvement in sight,” so it's rarely used for good news.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜一方だ</b> (at the end of a sentence) = keeps changing in one direction: <span class='gd-jp'>家賃は高くなる一方だ。</span>(Rent just keeps getting more expensive.)<br><b>〜ばかりだ</b> (after the dictionary form) = almost the same meaning, more colloquial, and used almost only for bad things: <span class='gd-jp'>家賃は高くなるばかりだ。</span>(Rent just keeps getting more expensive.)<br><b>〜一方で</b> (in the middle of a sentence) = “on the other hand,” used to contrast two things, unrelated to change: <span class='gd-jp'>この町は便利な一方で、家賃が高い。</span>(This town is convenient, but on the other hand the rent is high.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: <b>一方だ at the end of a sentence</b> means “only keeps getting more and more ~”; <b>一方で in the middle of a sentence</b> means “on the other hand.” When you see 一方, check where it is first.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To talk about the process of “gradually becoming ~,” learn 「〜てくる・〜ていく」 next: <span class='gd-jp'>だんだん寒くなってきた。</span>(It's gradually gotten colder.)</div>","n3-39":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようにする」 means <b>“I try to ~” or “I make an effort to ~”</b>. It's like saying “I've been trying to drink fewer sodas lately.” The point is that you're making a deliberate effort, with no guarantee you'll manage it every time.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Things you do: dictionary form (the base form you find in a dictionary) + ようにする<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>早く寝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>早く寝るようにする</span> (try to go to bed early)</span><br>Things you avoid: ない form + ようにする<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないようにする</span> (try not to forget)</span><br>To make it polite, just change the very end: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ようにする</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ようにします</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>The three forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようにしている</span>A habit you've been keeping up lately. The most common one in daily life.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎晩10時までに寝るようにしている。</span>(I try to be in bed by 10 every night.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>エレベーターより階段を使うようにしている。</span>(I try to take the stairs instead of the elevator.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようにする</span>You'll start paying attention from now on.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからは遅刻しないようにします。</span>(From now on, I'll make sure not to be late.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようにしてください</span>Asking the other person to try to do something. Doctors and teachers use it a lot.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>水をたくさん飲むようにしてください。</span>(Please try to drink plenty of water.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Only the dictionary form or ない form can come before it, not the ます form or た form. You can't say 寝ますようにする or 寝たようにする. For politeness, change the ending: <span class='gd-jp'>寝るようにします</span>.<br>・For “I usually try to ~,” use <b>ようにしている</b>. With <b>ようにする／ようにします</b>, it sounds like “from now on I'll pay attention,” as if you haven't started yet.<br>・It only works for <b>things you can control yourself</b>. For something like “I hope it doesn't rain,” you can't use ようにする, because whether it rains isn't up to you.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ようにしてください</span> sounds like a reminder or instruction. It's natural from a doctor to a patient or a teacher to a student, but with a boss or a customer it can sound like you're telling them how to do things, so be careful.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three are about “speaking Japanese,” but change the ending and the meaning changes:<br><b>ようになる</b> = gradually <b>come to be</b> that way (a result): <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるようになった。</span>(I've become able to speak Japanese.)<br><b>ようにする</b> = <b>deliberately make an effort</b> yourself: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日日本語を話すようにしている。</span>(I try to speak Japanese every day.)<br><b>ことにする</b> = <b>make a decision</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日日本語を話すことにした。</span>(I decided to speak Japanese every day.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: “have come to ~” is ようになる, “try to ~” is ようにする, and “decide to ~” is ことにする.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようになる」 (come to ~) often shows up alongside this lesson, so learn it next. There's also another 「〜ように」 meaning “so that ~” or “in order to ~,” for example: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように、メモしておく。</span>(I'll write it down so I don't forget.) It looks a lot like ようにする, so compare the two once you get to it.</div>","n3-49":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことはない」 means <b>“there's no need to ~”</b> or <b>“no reason to ~”</b>. When someone is worried, apologizing, or rushing, you use it to say “you don't have to.” It usually sounds a bit reassuring, as if you're telling them to relax.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the dictionary form of the verb (the base form you'd look up) and add ことはない.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>心配する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>心配することはない</span></span> (no need to worry)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>謝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>謝ることはない</span></span> (no need to apologize)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急ぐことはない</span></span> (no need to rush)<br>For a more polite version: <span class='gd-jp'>ことはありません</span>. With friends, people often drop 「は」 to get <span class='gd-jp'>ことない</span>, then add <span class='gd-jp'>よ</span> at the end.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reassuring</span>When the other person is worrying or blaming themselves.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大したことじゃないから、気にすることないよ。</span> (It's no big deal, so don't worry about it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Telling someone not to go to the trouble</span>Often appears together with 「わざわざ」 (going out of one's way).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>わざわざ来ることはないよ。電話でいいから。</span> (You don't need to come all the way here. A phone call is fine.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Why bother ~</span>Put 「何も」 in front and it becomes a slightly exasperated “why on earth would you ~?”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何もそんなに怒ることはないでしょう。</span> (There's no need to get that angry.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before it is always the <b>dictionary form</b>. Switch to the た-form and the meaning changes completely to “have never ~” (compared below).<br>・It means “<b>there's no need</b>,” not “<b>you must not</b>.” <span class='gd-jp'>行くことはない</span> means “you don't have to go,” so you're still free to go if you want. To say “you're not allowed to go,” use <span class='gd-jp'>行ってはいけない</span>.<br>・This phrase carries the tone of “I've judged it for you: it's not necessary,” which is natural with friends and juniors. With a boss or a customer, saying 「〜ことはありません」 directly can sound a bit like you're instructing them. <span class='gd-jp'>大丈夫です</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ご心配なく</span> is safer.<br>・Sometimes it means “<b>won't happen</b>”: <span class='gd-jp'>もう二度と会うことはないだろう。</span> (We probably won't ever meet again.) When you're comforting or advising someone it means “no need,” and when you're talking about possibility it means “won't.” Let the context tell you which.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take “going to Japan.” Change the verb form in front and the meaning shifts a lot:<br><b>Dictionary form + ことはない</b> = no need to: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くことはない。</span> (There's no need to go to Japan.)<br><b>た-form + ことはない</b> = have never done: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行ったことはない。</span> (I've never been to Japan.)<br><b>ない-form + ことはない</b> = it's not that I never: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行かないことはない。</span> (It's not that I never go to Japan. I do go once in a while.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: look at the word right before 「こと」. Dictionary form means “no need,” た means “have never,” and ない means “it's not that I don't.”</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Its opposite is 「〜ことだ」, meaning “you'd better do it”: <span class='gd-jp'>心配なら、医者に相談することだ。</span> (If you're worried, you should talk to a doctor.) One urges you to do something, the other says you don't have to.<br>A very close phrase is <span class='gd-jp'>心配する必要はない</span> (there's no need to worry). It sounds more objective and stiffer, and you'll often see it in written text.</div>","n3-50":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both express a “decision.” The difference is <b>who made the call</b>.<br><b>ことにする</b> = I decided it myself: “I've decided to quit smoking.”<br><b>ことになる</b> = it wasn't my call. The company arranged it, a group discussed it, or things simply turned out that way: “The company is transferring me.”<br>English “I've decided to ~” matches ことにする, while “it's been decided that I'll ~” or “I'm being made to ~” matches ことになる.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Things you will do: dictionary form + ことにする／ことになる　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やめる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>やめることにした</span></span><br>Things you won't do: ない-form + ことにする／ことになる　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らないことにした</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことにした</span>I made the decision myself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>タバコをやめることにした。</span> (I've decided to quit smoking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今年の夏は実家に帰らないことにした。</span> (I've decided not to go back to my parents' home this summer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことになった</span>The company, school, or someone else decided, and I'm just accepting the result.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来月から大阪に転勤することになった。</span> (I'm being transferred to Osaka starting next month.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Announcing your own news</span>Even when it was your own decision, Japanese speakers often use <span class='gd-jp'>ことになりました</span> when announcing things like a marriage or leaving a job. It sounds humbler, like “things have come to this point,” and doesn't stress “this is what I wanted.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>実は、結婚することになりました。</span> (Actually, I'm getting married.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことにしている</span>A rule you set for yourself and keep following.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝る前はスマホを見ないことにしている。</span> (I make it a rule not to look at my phone before bed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことになっている</span>A rule, a custom, or something already scheduled.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋では靴を脱ぐことになっています。</span> (In this room, you're supposed to take off your shoes.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、田中さんと会うことになっている。</span> (I'm meeting Mr. Tanaka tomorrow. It's already arranged.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・In English you might say “I've decided” or “I'm going to.” Japanese normally uses the past tense <span class='gd-jp'>ことにした</span> to show the decision has already been made.<br>・Nouns like 旅行 or 勉強 that can take する must become verbs first: <span class='gd-jp'>旅行することにした</span>, not 旅行ことにした. (Ordinary nouns attach directly to にする, as in <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーにする</span>, which means “I'll go with ~.” That's a different usage from this one.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ことになる</span> doesn't always involve someone making a decision. It can also mean “if things go on like this, you'll end up ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>今やめたら、後で後悔することになるよ。</span> (If you quit now, you'll regret it later.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ことにしている</span> is a rule <b>you</b> set yourself, while <span class='gd-jp'>ことになっている</span> is a rule set <b>from outside</b>. Don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take “getting up early”:<br><b>ことにする</b> = deciding it yourself: <span class='gd-jp'>明日から6時に起きることにした。</span> (I've decided to get up at six starting tomorrow.)<br><b>ことになる</b> = being arranged or circumstances turning out that way: <span class='gd-jp'>仕事の関係で、毎朝6時に起きることになった。</span> (Because of work, I end up having to get up at six every morning.)<br><b>ようにする</b> = trying to do it: <span class='gd-jp'>毎朝6時に起きるようにしている。</span> (I try to get up at six every morning.)<br><b>ようになる</b> = gradually becoming able to: <span class='gd-jp'>6時に起きられるようになった。</span> (I've become able to get up at six.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: if it feels like a “decision,” use こと. If it's “try to” or “become,” use よう. Then ask who decided: if you made the call, use する. If someone else arranged it or it just turned out that way, use なる.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When <span class='gd-jp'>ことにする</span> follows a past-tense verb, it can also mean “I'll treat it as if ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>今の話は聞かなかったことにする。</span> (I'll pretend I didn't hear what was just said.)</div>","n3-53":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ために」has two meanings. The most common is <b>“in order to ~”</b>, which states a purpose. The other, more formal one is <b>“because of ~”</b>, which states a reason. English uses different words for these, but Japanese uses the same ために for both, so you tell them apart from context.</div><div class='gd-h'>Usage 1: in order to ~ (purpose)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In order to do something</span>Use the <b>dictionary form</b> of the verb + ために. With a noun, add 「の」 first: noun + のために.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に留学するために、アルバイトでお金をためている。</span>(I'm saving money from my part-time job in order to study abroad in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>健康のために、毎朝歩いている。</span>(I walk every morning for my health.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>For someone's sake</span>Put a person before it and it means “for someone.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族のために、毎日がんばって働いている。</span>(I work hard every day for my family.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Usage 2: because of ~ (reason)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Formal “because”</span>It can follow the た form or dictionary form of a verb, an い-adjective, a な-adjective + な, or a noun + の. It's very common in news, station announcements, and notices, and the に is often dropped, leaving just 「ため」. What follows is usually a result that has already happened and is somewhat negative, but not always.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大雪のために、電車が止まった。</span>(The trains stopped because of the heavy snow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>事故があったために、道が混んでいる。</span>(The roads are congested because there was an accident.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台風のため、今日の授業は休みです。</span>(Because of the typhoon, today's class is cancelled.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to tell which one it is</div><div class='gd-sec'>If the first part is <b>something you want to achieve that hasn't happened yet</b>, and the second part is the effort you make for it, it means “in order to.”<br>If the first part is <b>something that already happened, or a state</b>, and the second part is the result it caused, it means “because of.”<br>If you see a た form (<span class='gd-jp'>あった</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>降った</span>), it is definitely a reason. Noun + のために can be either, so look at what follows: <span class='gd-jp'>試験のために勉強する</span> (study for the exam), <span class='gd-jp'>雪のために遅れた</span> (was late because of the snow).</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A noun must be followed by の: you can't say 健康ために; say <span class='gd-jp'>健康のために</span>.<br>・It can't follow the ます form: you can't say 合格しますために; say <span class='gd-jp'>合格するために</span>.<br>・With the “in order to” ために, the subject before and after is usually <b>the same person</b>, and the first part must be <b>an action you can decide to do yourself</b>. For abilities or states like “can speak,” “can hear,” or “can understand,” use ように instead (compared below).<br>・The “because of” ために is formal, and what follows can't be a request, invitation, or command. You can't say 雨のために傘を持っていこう; say <span class='gd-jp'>雨だから、傘を持っていこう。</span> (It's raining, so let's take an umbrella.) In everyday conversation, から or ので is enough for giving a reason.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>The “in order to” ために and <b>ように</b> can both be translated as “in order to,” but what goes before them is different.<br>Some things can be said either way; it just depends on how you look at it:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>N2に合格するために、毎日勉強している。</span> (I study every day in order to pass N2.): 合格する is something you set out to achieve.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>N2に合格できるように、毎日勉強している。</span> (I study every day so that I can pass N2.): 合格できる is a state you hope to reach.<br>The following can only use ように:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>みんなに聞こえるように、大きな声で話した。</span> (I spoke loudly so that everyone could hear.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもにも読めるように、ひらがなで書いた。</span> (I wrote it in hiragana so that children could read it too.)<br>聞こえる and 読める are abilities or states, and in the second sentence the subjects differ (I write, the children read), so ために can't be used.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if the first part is “an action you actively do yourself,” use ために. If it's an ability or state like「できる、わかる、聞こえる、見える」, or the subjects differ, use ように.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>For the purpose meaning, look at 「〜ように」 next (n3-54). For the reason meaning, compare 「〜おかげで」 (thanks to ~, a good result) and 「〜せいで」 (because of ~, a bad result); they also express the speaker's gratitude or complaint.</div>","n3-54":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ように」means <b>“so that ~”</b> or <b>“so as not to ~.”</b> The first half states the result you hope for, and the second half says what you did to bring it about: <span class='gd-jp'>後ろの人にも聞こえるように、マイクを使った。</span> (I used a microphone so that the people in the back could hear too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you hope you “can ~ / will be able to ~”: dictionary form + ように<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>聞こえる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>聞こえるように</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → first change it to “can speak” <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せるように</span></span><br>When you hope “not to ~”: ない form + ように<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>What usually goes before it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The key point: what comes before ように is <b>a result you can't directly decide</b>; you can only work toward making it happen. Common types:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can ~</span>The first part is a “can ~ / be able to ~” expression (potential form).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>字幕なしでドラマが見られるように、毎日日本語を聞いています。</span>(I listen to Japanese every day so that I can watch dramas without subtitles.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語で話せるように、毎日練習している。</span>(I practice every day so that I can talk in Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Things that happen on their own</span>These are not actions you can do just by wanting to, such as <span class='gd-jp'>聞こえる</span> (can be heard), <span class='gd-jp'>間に合う</span> (make it in time), and <span class='gd-jp'>治る</span> (get better, recover).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風邪が早く治るように、薬を飲んで寝た。</span>(I took medicine and went to bed so that my cold would get better quickly.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最終便に間に合うように、早めに空港へ向かった。</span>(I headed to the airport early so that I'd make the last flight.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not to ~</span>ない form + ように = “so as not to ~.” Here it can be used even with actions you can control yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように、メモしておこう。</span>(Let me write it down so I don't forget.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅れないように、早めに家を出た。</span>(I left home early so as not to be late.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Extra: the two subjects can differ</div><div class='gd-sec'>This isn't a separate usage, just a feature of ように: the person in the first half can be different from the one in the second half. In the sentence below, the one who “understands” is the child, and the one who “explains” is me.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもにも分かるように、簡単な言葉で説明した。</span>(I explained it in simple words so that even a child could understand.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・For an action you decide to do yourself, the <b>affirmative form</b> can't be followed directly by ように:<br>× 日本語を勉強するように、日本へ来た。<br>○ <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を勉強するために、日本へ来た。</span> (I came to Japan in order to study Japanese.)<br>・Noun + 「のように」means “like ~,” which has nothing to do with the “so that” meaning here: <span class='gd-jp'>子どものように笑う</span> (laugh like a child).<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ように</span> is followed by a new action, while <span class='gd-jp'>ようにする</span> means “I'll try to ~.” Don't mix them up: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように、メモする</span> (write it down so as not to forget) vs <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないようにする</span> (I'll try not to forget).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take saving money to study abroad:<br><span class='gd-jp'>留学するために、お金をためている。</span> (I'm saving money in order to study abroad.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>留学できるように、お金をためている。</span> (I'm saving money so that I can study abroad.)<br>Before <b>ために</b> comes <b>something you decide to do yourself</b>, and “studying abroad” is the goal itself. Before <b>ように</b> comes <b>whether you can or will be able to</b>; “being able to study abroad” depends on whether you have enough money.<br>Also, with ために the subject before and after must be the same person, while ように allows different people. And ために can follow a noun directly (<span class='gd-jp'>健康のために</span>), but ように can't.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if you have full say over the first action, use ために. If the first part is “can ~” or “not to ~,” or depends on luck or other people, use ように.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようにと」introduces the content of a prayer, wish, or instruction, for example <span class='gd-jp'>合格できるようにと祈った</span> (I prayed that I would pass). You can also go back and compare 「〜ようにする」 and 「〜ようになる」.</div>","n3-55":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようにと」 is used to <b>report what someone told you to do</b>, like English “The teacher told us to be on time” or “The doctor told me to cut down on salt.” You're not repeating their exact words. You're passing on the gist of what they want done. The と works like closing quotation marks: it marks everything before it as the content of the instruction.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Something the person should do: dictionary form + ようにと　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>守る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>守るようにと</span></span><br>Something the person should not do: ない form + ようにと　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅れないようにと</span></span><br>It's followed by verbs of passing on a message: <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>伝える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>注意する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>頼む</span>, and also the verbs of wishing and praying, <span class='gd-jp'>祈る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>願う</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>In speech, the 「と」 is often dropped, giving 「〜ように言われた」 with the same meaning. When 「と」 is there, it puts more weight on “this is what they told me.”</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Being told</span>「〜ようにと言われた」: someone told me to do something. This is the most common one in daily life.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母に早く帰るようにと言われた。</span>（My mom told me to come home early）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医者にお酒を飲まないようにと言われた。</span>（The doctor told me not to drink alcohol）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Passing on a message</span>「〜ようにと伝えてください」: you're asking someone to relay a message to a third person.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんに、明日は8時に来るようにと伝えてください。</span>（Please tell Mr. Tanaka to come at 8 tomorrow）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Praying or wishing</span>「〜ようにと祈る」: you hope that something will come true.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試験に合格できるようにと祈った。</span>（I prayed that I'd pass the exam）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The action before ように is done by <b>the person who was told</b>, not by the person giving the instruction. In <span class='gd-jp'>母に早く帰るように言われた</span>, the one who should come home early is me, not my mom.<br>・Use the dictionary form or the ない form for the instruction, never the た form: say <span class='gd-jp'>帰るように言われた</span>, not 「帰ったように言われた」. With verbs like 「言う」 and 「注意する」, don't put the ます form before ように either.<br>・「ように言う」 has a flavor of <b>giving instructions</b>, so it suits talking down to someone or to a peer. If you're asking a teacher or boss to do something, 「ように言った」 sounds like you ordered them around. Say 「〜てくださいとお願いした」 instead, which is more polite.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>「」と言った</b> = a direct quote of the exact words: <span class='gd-jp'>先生は「時間を守りなさい」と言った。</span>（The teacher said, “Be on time.”）<br><b>ようにと言った</b> = a report of the gist: <span class='gd-jp'>先生は時間を守るようにと言った。</span>（The teacher told us to be on time）<br>The 「〜なさい」 or 「〜てください」 of the original words becomes 「〜ように」 when you report them.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ように (so that)</b> = you do something yourself for a purpose: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないようにメモした。</span>（I wrote a note so I wouldn't forget）<br><b>ようにと (instruction)</b> = someone told me to do something: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないようにと言われた。</span>（I was told not to forget）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if ように is followed by a message verb like 言う, 伝える, 注意する or 頼む, it's an instruction. If it's followed by an action you do yourself, it means “so that.”</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When something you were told to do becomes your own habit, you get 「〜ようにしている」: <span class='gd-jp'>医者に言われてから、塩分を控えるようにしている。</span>（Ever since the doctor told me to, I try to cut down on salt）</div>","n3-56":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことから」 means <b>“from the fact that ~, ...”</b>. Think of a tour guide saying, “This mountain is called Pot Mountain because it looks like a pot,” or a detective reasoning, “Judging from the broken window, someone broke in.” First you put a <b>visible fact</b>, then you give the <b>name</b> or <b>conclusion</b> that comes from that fact.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Origin of a name</span>It explains why something has a certain name or why it's called that. It's often followed by 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と呼ばれる</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜という名前がついた</span>」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>山の形が鍋に似ていることから、「鍋山」という名前がついた。</span>（Because the mountain's shape resembles a pot, it was named “Nabeyama”）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつも笑顔でいることから、彼は「太陽」と呼ばれている。</span>（Because he's always smiling, he's called “the Sun”）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Basis for a judgment</span>You draw a conclusion from a clue. It's often followed by 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と分かる</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と考えられる</span>」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓が割れていることから、泥棒に入られたと分かった。</span>（From the broken window, we could tell a burglar had broken in）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道がぬれていることから、夜の間に雨が降ったと考えられる。</span>（Judging from the wet road, it probably rained during the night）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving background</span>In introductory or explanatory writing, it tells how something came about.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この地域は温泉が湧いていることから、観光地として知られている。</span>（Because hot springs well up in this area, it's known as a tourist destination）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach the plain form directly (the form without ます or です). Present, past and negative all work.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>似ている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>似ていることから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>割れた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>割れたことから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いことから</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: keep 「な」, or change it to 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなことから</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かであることから</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>港町</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>港町であることから</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a noun, use <b>である</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>港町であることから</span>, not 「港町だことから」 and not 「港町のことから」.<br>・This is <b>written, formal</b> language, common in news, travel guides and explanatory texts. Using 「〜ことから」 when chatting with a friend sounds stiff. In everyday speech, 「から」 or 「ので」 is enough.<br>・What follows can only be a <b>fact, a name or a judgment</b>. It can't be an invitation, request or command such as “Let's ~,” “Please ~” or “Give me ~.”<br>・The reason in front should be a <b>fact everyone can see and verify</b>. It's not the place for your own feelings or excuses. To say “I'm tired, so I'm heading home,” use 「から」 or 「ので」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same fact, “the road is wet”:<br><b>から・ので</b> = the everyday “because.” What follows can be anything: a warning, an invitation, a request.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道がぬれているから、気をつけてね。</span>（The road is wet, so be careful）</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ことから</b> = “drawing a conclusion from this fact.” What follows can only be a judgment or a name.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道がぬれていることから、雨が降ったと分かる。</span>（From the fact that the road is wet, we can tell it rained）</div><div class='gd-sec'>Saying 「道がぬれていることから、気をつけてね」 is wrong, because what follows has turned into a warning to the listener.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can replace it in English with “judging from the fact that ~ (you can tell / that's why it's called ~),” use ことから. If it's just a plain “because,” use から or ので.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When explaining where a name comes from, the passive 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と呼ばれている</span>」 (is called ~) almost always follows, so it's a good chance to review 「～（ら）れる（受身）」.</div>","n3-51":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようだ」「〜みたいだ」 mean <b>\"it seems like ~\" or \"it looks like ~\"</b>. You use them when you've seen, heard, or sensed some clues and reached your own judgment, but you're not 100% sure. The two mean the same thing: <b>みたい is the casual, spoken version</b>, while ようだ is more formal and can be used in writing too.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing from clues</span>The clues are things <b>you yourself</b> saw, heard, or sensed.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>隣の部屋、電気が消えている。留守のようだ。</span>(The lights are off next door. It seems nobody's home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あれ、鍵がない。どこかで落としたみたい。</span>(Huh, my keys are gone. I must have dropped them somewhere.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's also very common for describing how you feel physically. It means \"that's how it feels to me, but I can't say for sure.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと熱があるみたい。</span>(I think I have a bit of a fever.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Like ~</span>It isn't actually that thing, but it's very similar. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>まるで</span> (just like, as if).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まるで夢みたい。</span>(It's just like a dream.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は春のように暖かい。</span>(It's warm today, like spring.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます or です). Both patterns work the same way.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降っている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降っているようだ</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>降っているみたいだ</span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いようだ</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>寒いみたいだ</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: here the two differ, so make a note of it.<br>Noun + ようだ needs 「の」 first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留守</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留守のようだ</span></span><br>Noun + みたいだ attaches directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留守</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留守みたいだ</span></span><br>な-adjective + ようだ needs 「な」 first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなようだ</span></span><br>な-adjective + みたいだ attaches directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かみたいだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>For past events, nouns and な-adjectives become 「だった」 first, and then the two patterns are identical: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留守だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留守だったようだ</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>留守だったみたいだ</span><br>To be polite, swap だ for です: <span class='gd-jp'>ようです</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>みたいです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before a noun it becomes <b>ような／みたいな</b>; before an action or adjective it becomes <b>ように／みたいに</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お寿司みたいな日本料理が好きです。</span>(I like Japanese food such as sushi.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本人みたいに日本語を話したい。</span>(I want to speak Japanese like a Japanese person.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a noun: ようだ needs の, みたいだ doesn't. <span class='gd-jp'>学生のようだ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>学生みたいだ</span> are both correct; 「学生のみたい」 and 「学生ようだ」 are both wrong.<br>・Many people think the plain form should keep 「だ」 and write 「留守だみたい」. That's wrong. For a present-tense noun, attach directly: <span class='gd-jp'>留守みたい</span>.<br>・みたい sounds the same as 「見たい」 (want to see), so check the context: <span class='gd-jp'>子供みたい</span> means \"like a child,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>映画が見たい</span> means \"I want to see a movie.\"<br>・みたい is colloquial. For reports, essays, and formal letters, use ようです. みたいです is fine when chatting with someone older, but ようです is more appropriate in formal situations.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same \"it's raining.\" What matters is where your information comes from:<br><b>ようだ／みたいだ</b> = you inferred it from clues you saw yourself: <span class='gd-jp'>みんな傘をさしている。雨が降っているみたいだ。</span>(Everyone has an umbrella up. It seems to be raining.)<br><b>らしい</b> = you heard it from someone else and haven't confirmed it: <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんの話では、外は雨が降っているらしい。</span>(According to Tanaka, it's apparently raining outside.)<br><b>そうだ</b> (appearance) = it looks like it's about to happen, but it hasn't yet: <span class='gd-jp'>空が暗い。雨が降りそうだ。</span>(The sky is dark. It looks like it's going to rain.) This one attaches differently: drop ます from the ます-form and add そうだ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降りそうだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if you're guessing from what you saw or felt yourself, use ようだ／みたい. If you heard it from someone, use らしい. If it looks like it's \"about to ~,\" use そうだ.</div>","n3-52":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「らしい」 has two meanings, almost like two different English phrases:<br>①<b>\"I hear that ~\" / \"apparently ~\"</b>: a guess based on information you heard or read, or on objective clues, not something you can confirm yourself.<br>②<b>\"typical of ~\" / \"very ~-like\"</b>: like saying \"it feels really springlike\" or \"that's just like him.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>① Hearsay / apparently</span>You pass along something you heard or saw, while also signaling \"that's just what I heard, no guarantees.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来週から気温が下がるらしい。</span>(I hear temperatures will drop starting next week.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さん、会社を辞めるらしいよ。</span>(I heard Tanaka is quitting the company.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店のラーメン、すごくおいしいらしい。</span>(Apparently the ramen at that place is really good.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>地面が濡れている。雨が降ったらしい。</span>(The ground is wet. It seems it rained.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>② Typical of ~</span>Put a noun before it to say \"it has exactly the feel that thing should have.\" It's usually a compliment.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は春らしい天気ですね。</span>(It's really springlike weather today, isn't it?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の発言はいかにも彼らしい。</span>(That comment is so typical of him.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅刻するなんて、彼らしくない。</span>(Being late? That's not like him.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Meaning ①</b><br>Verbs and い-adjectives: attach directly. Past and negative forms are fine too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くらしい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないらしい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いらしい</span></span><br>な-adjectives and nouns: <b>drop だ</b>, then attach.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>静からしい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨らしい</span></span><br>For the past, keep だった: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったらしい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Meaning ②</b><br>Noun + らしい: <span class='gd-jp'>春らしい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>学生らしい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>彼らしい</span><br>It's often strengthened with <span class='gd-jp'>いかにも</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>本当に</span>, for example <span class='gd-jp'>いかにも先生らしい話し方</span> (a way of speaking that's very much like a teacher).</div><div class='gd-sec'>「らしい」 itself conjugates like an い-adjective: <span class='gd-jp'>らしくない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>らしかった</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>らしいです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add an extra だ after な-adjectives or nouns: it's <span class='gd-jp'>静からしい</span>, not 静かだらしい.<br>・<b>When attached directly to a noun, either meaning is possible</b>, so check the context. For example, <span class='gd-jp'>先生らしい人が来た</span> is usually ①, \"someone who seems to be a teacher came,\" not \"a person with a teacherly manner.\"<br>・Put the negative in the right place: \"I hear he's not coming\" is <span class='gd-jp'>来ないらしい</span> (①, the verb before it is negated). <span class='gd-jp'>彼らしくない</span> is ②, \"not like him.\"<br>・① means \"I heard this from somewhere,\" so you can't use it for things you experience firsthand. If you yourself are going tomorrow, say <span class='gd-jp'>明日行きます</span>. Saying 「明日行くらしい」 sounds like you're talking about someone else.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>らしい (①)</b> = hearsay: <span class='gd-jp'>午後から雨が降るらしい。</span>(I hear it's going to rain from the afternoon.)<br><b>そうだ (hearsay)</b> = you clearly pass along a specific source: <span class='gd-jp'>天気予報によると、午後から雨が降るそうだ。</span>(According to the weather forecast, it's going to rain from the afternoon.)<br><b>ようだ・みたいだ</b> = you saw or sensed it yourself: <span class='gd-jp'>傘を持った人が入ってきた。雨が降っているみたい。</span>(Someone came in carrying an umbrella. It seems to be raining.)<br>Also, when you see a sign right in front of you and feel something is about to happen, use <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうだ</span> (it looks like it's going to rain).</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to simply relay what you heard as-is, use そうだ. For a \"probably so\" guess based on what you heard or on objective clues, use らしい. For a judgment based on your own feelings or experience, use ようだ・みたいだ.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>らしい (②) vs. っぽい</b> are also often confused:<br><span class='gd-jp'>子どもらしい笑顔</span> (a smile with a child's innocence): the person really is a child and fits what a child should be like, so it leans toward praise.<br><span class='gd-jp'>大人なのに子どもっぽい。</span> (They're an adult but act childish.): not a child, yet resembling one, which often carries a negative tone.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: use らしい for \"it really is that, and has that quality\"; use っぽい for \"it actually isn't, but it's a bit like it.\"</div>","n3-59":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を通じて」and「〜を通して」have two meanings. The first is <b>\"through ~\" or \"via ~\"</b>. Something happens by going through a person or a channel, as in \"met through a friend\" or \"got in touch through the internet.\" The second is <b>\"throughout ~\"</b>. You put a period of time before it to say that something holds from start to finish, like \"all year round\" in English.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's simple. Attach it directly after a noun:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>友達</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>友達を通じて</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一年</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>一年を通して</span></span><br>To use it before a noun and modify that noun, add 「の」in between: <span class='gd-jp'>ボランティアを通しての出会い</span> (a connection made through volunteering)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Through a person or channel</span>Someone acts as the go-between, or you use a channel such as the internet, the phone, or the media.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達を通じて、今の彼女と知り合った。</span> (I met my current girlfriend through a friend.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>インターネットを通じて、世界中の人と話せる。</span> (Through the internet, you can talk with people all over the world.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Through an experience</span>You went through some experience and learned or felt something from it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>アルバイトを通して、敬語の使い方を覚えた。</span> (Through my part-time job, I learned how to use honorific language.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Throughout a period</span>Put a span of time like 「一年」or 「一生」before it to say that something holds for that whole period.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この島は一年を通して暖かい。</span> (This island is warm all year round.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>祖父は一生を通じて、同じ会社で働いた。</span> (My grandfather worked at the same company his whole life.)</div><div class='gd-h'>を通じて vs を通して: what's the difference?</div><div class='gd-sec'>In most cases <b>the two are interchangeable</b> and mean about the same thing, so don't stress. If you have to split hairs:<br><b>を通じて</b> is used more for \"channels\" like the internet, the phone, or personal connections.<br><b>を通して</b> has more of a feeling of \"doing it yourself, on purpose, firsthand,\" so it's common when talking about experience, practice, or experiments.<br>When you're not sure, either choice won't be badly wrong.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't use it for vehicles or ordinary tools.</b> \"Go by bus\" is <span class='gd-jp'>バスで行く</span>, and \"cut with scissors\" is <span class='gd-jp'>はさみで切る</span>. You can't say バスを通して行く. を通じて is about \"the person or channel something passes through,\" not the tool in your hand.<br>・\"Pass an exam\" is not this grammar. Say <span class='gd-jp'>試験に合格する</span>. The English \"through\" in \"get through an exam\" doesn't map onto を通して either.<br>・The particle before it is <b>を</b>. Don't write に.<br>・For the \"throughout a period\" use, put <b>a span of time</b> before it, such as 一年, 一生, or 四季. It means \"during that whole period,\" not \"after that time has passed.\"<br>・The tone is fairly formal and very common in news, essays, and self-introductions. When chatting with friends and saying \"introduced by a friend,\" people often say <span class='gd-jp'>友達の紹介で</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take \"passing a message to the teacher\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>メールで先生に伝えた。</span> (I told the teacher by email.): <b>で</b> = the tool or method, the most ordinary everyday choice.<br><span class='gd-jp'>先輩を通じて先生に伝えた。</span> (I told the teacher through a senior student.): <b>を通じて</b> = a person or channel in the middle carried it for you.<br><br>Another easy one to mix up is <b>によって</b>, which says \"by what means something is achieved\" or \"what caused something\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>この研究によって、病気の原因が分かった。</span> (Thanks to this research, the cause of the disease was found.)<br>If you met someone through a friend's introduction, say <span class='gd-jp'>友達を通じて知り合った</span>, not 友達によって知り合った.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if a \"person, channel, or experience\" sits in the middle as a go-between, use を通じて / を通して. For a plain tool or a vehicle, use で. For a method or a cause, use によって.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other patterns in the \"noun + を + ~\" shape are worth looking at next: <span class='gd-jp'>〜を中心に</span> (centered on ~) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜をはじめ</span> (starting with ~). These often show up together in the same article.</div>","n3-60":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に基づいて」means <b>\"based on ~\" or \"in accordance with ~\"</b>. It tells you what you rely on as your basis when you do something. A film's opening credits often say \"This film is based on a true story,\" and in Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>この映画は実話に基づいている。</span><br>What goes before it is the basis for \"what you're going on,\" such as facts, data, rules, or experience.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by an action</span>noun + に基づいて + verb</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>規則に基づいて判断します。</span> (I'll judge according to the rules.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>データに基づいて計画を立てた。</span> (I made a plan based on the data.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a noun</span>noun + に基づく / に基づいた + noun. Both forms are common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>調査結果に基づく報告書</span> (a report based on the survey results)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>経験に基づいたアドバイス</span> (advice based on experience)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the end of a sentence</span>Use <span class='gd-jp'>〜に基づいている</span> to mean \"is based on ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この話は事実に基づいています。</span> (This story is based on fact.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In news and official documents you'll also see <span class='gd-jp'>に基づき</span>. It means the same as に基づいて, just more bookish.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What goes before it must be a <b>basis</b>, not a person directly. For \"what the teacher said,\" say <span class='gd-jp'>先生の話に基づいて</span>, not 「先生に基づいて」.<br>・Before a noun, use <b>基づく / 基づいた</b>. Don't write 「データに基づいて計画」.<br>・It's read <b>もとづいて</b>. The kana is 「づ」, not 「ず」, so when typing, enter motodu.<br>・The tone is fairly formal and it often appears in reports, the news, and work settings. It's less common in casual chat with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even with the same topic, \"data,\" these three phrases mean different things:<br><span class='gd-jp'>データについて説明します。</span> (I'll explain <b>the data itself</b>.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>データに基づいて説明します。</span> (I'll explain <b>using the data as my basis</b>, for example explaining a plan or a conclusion.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>データによると、売り上げが増えています。</span> (<b>According to the data</b>, sales are increasing.)<br>「によると」names the source of information, and what follows is \"what the data says.\" It can't be followed by an action you take yourself.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for \"about ~\" use について. For \"according to ~\" (it shows, I hear) use によると. For \"doing or judging something on the basis of ~\" use に基づいて.</div>","n3-62":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ものだから</span>」and「<span class='gd-jp'>〜もので</span>」work like English <b>\"It's just that…, you see, so…\"</b>. Say you're late and tell your boss, \"Sorry, the train was delayed, you see…\" That <b>slightly defensive, please-understand-me tone</b> is exactly what these carry.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Explaining when apologizing</span>When you've made a mistake or caused trouble, add your reason to show \"I didn't mean to.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、電車が止まっていたものですから。</span>(Sorry, the train had stopped, you see…)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝坊したもので、遅れてしまいました。</span>(I overslept, so I ended up being late.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The first sentence「<span class='gd-jp'>電車が止まっていたものですから。</span>」stops right at「<span class='gd-jp'>ものですから。</span>」. It gives only the reason and leaves the rest unsaid. You'll hear this all the time in apologies.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Explaining your situation</span>A soft way to explain why you can't do something, or why you did something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもがまだ小さいもので、夜はあまり出かけられないんです。</span>(My child is still little, so I can't go out much at night.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>So ~ that I couldn't help it</span>Explaining that you ended up doing something because you couldn't resist.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あまりにおいしかったものだから、全部食べちゃった。</span>(It was so delicious that I ate it all.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: plain form, attached directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝坊した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝坊したものだから</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attached directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいものだから</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add「な」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなものだから</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: also add「な」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>初めて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>初めてなもので</span></span><br>In speech it's often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>もんだから</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>もんで</span>. With superiors and customers, use <span class='gd-jp'>ものですから</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>もので</span> is already a bit polite, so it's common in formal situations too.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a な-adjective or noun it's「な」, not「だ」: say <span class='gd-jp'>静かなものだから</span>, not「静かだものだから」.<br>・The second half <b>can't be a command, request, invitation, or your own intention</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているものだから、傘を持っていってください。</span> is wrong. Change it to <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているから、傘を持っていってください。</span> (It's raining, so please take an umbrella.)<br>・It's for <b>your own situation and personal reasons</b>. For objective explanations of causes, as in news or reports, don't use it; use <span class='gd-jp'>ので</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ため</span>.<br>・If you use it constantly when you're clearly at fault, it sounds like you're making excuses. When you really are in the wrong, a sincere apology matters more.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>から／ので</b> = simply states the reason: <span class='gd-jp'>電車が遅れたので、遅刻しました。</span> (The train was delayed, so I was late.)<br><b>ものだから</b> = states the reason and adds \"it wasn't my fault, please understand\": <span class='gd-jp'>電車が遅れたものですから、遅刻してしまいました。</span> (The train was delayed, you see, so I ended up late…)<br>If the second half tells people to do something, you can only use から／ので: <span class='gd-jp'>電車が遅れているから、タクシーで行こう。</span> (The train is delayed, so let's take a taxi.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ものだ</b> (at the end of a sentence) = an exclamation, \"that's just how it is.\" It isn't giving a reason: <span class='gd-jp'>時間が経つのは早いものだ。</span> (Time really flies.)<br><b>ものだから</b> has「から」or「で」after it, so it is always giving a <b>reason</b>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you want the listener's understanding and you're explaining your own situation, use ものだから／もので. If the second half tells someone to do something or invites them, switch to から／ので. If the sentence ends at「ものだ。」, it's an exclamation, not a reason.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>In everyday conversation there's an even shorter, more childlike version that ends the sentence with <span class='gd-jp'>もん</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>だって、知らなかったんだもん。</span> (But I just didn't know!) Use it only with people you're very close to.</div>","n3-63":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span>」means <b>\"even ~\"</b>. It deliberately picks the <b>most basic, most obvious</b> example, meaning \"if even this is the case, then the rest goes without saying.\" For \"I'm so busy I don't even have time to eat,\" Japanese says: <span class='gd-jp'>忙しくて、ご飯を食べる時間さえない。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>After nouns</span>Add <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> directly after the noun. Drop the original「<span class='gd-jp'>が</span>」or「<span class='gd-jp'>を</span>」; <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> replaces it.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>時間がない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>時間さえない</span></span> (don't even have time)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>自分の名前を書けない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>自分の名前さえ書けない</span></span> (can't even write my own name)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Keep other particles</span>Don't drop「<span class='gd-jp'>に</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>で</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>と</span>」; just put <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> after them.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家族に話していない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家族にさえ話していない</span></span> (haven't even told my family)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Saying \"even a certain person\"</span>To stress \"even this person…\", you'll often hear「<span class='gd-jp'>でさえ</span>」: <span class='gd-jp'>子供でさえ知っている。</span> (Even children know.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>After verbs</span>To say \"don't even ~\", drop ます from the verb and add <span class='gd-jp'>さえしない</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>見さえしない</span></span> (doesn't even look)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は私の顔を見さえしなかった。</span>(He didn't even look at my face.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a negative</span>This is the most common use: you don't even have, or can't even do, the most basic thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しくて、水を飲む時間さえなかった。</span>(I was so busy I didn't even have time to drink water.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>足が痛くて、歩くことさえできない。</span>(My leg hurts so much I can't even walk.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by a positive</span>Even the least likely person knows it or can do it, which implies \"everyone else surely should too.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなことは、子供でさえ知っている。</span>(Even a child knows that.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この問題は、先生でさえ間違えた。</span>(Even the teacher got this question wrong.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You must drop「<span class='gd-jp'>が</span>」and「<span class='gd-jp'>を</span>」: 「時間がさえない」is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>時間さえない</span>. But keep「<span class='gd-jp'>に</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>で</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>と</span>」, so it's <span class='gd-jp'>家族にさえ</span>, not 家族さえ.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> is fairly strong and formal. When chatting with friends, many people just use「<span class='gd-jp'>も</span>」: <span class='gd-jp'>時間もない</span>. The meaning is similar, just without the heavy \"even this\" feel.<br>・On its own, <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> means \"even ~\", <b>not \"if only\"</b>. Many learners see <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> and translate it as \"if only,\" but for that meaning it must be followed by a conditional form (see the next section).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both talk about \"time,\" but what follows changes the meaning a lot:<br><b>さえ</b> = even ~: <span class='gd-jp'>最近は寝る時間さえない。</span> (Lately I don't even have time to sleep.)<br><b>さえ〜ば</b> = as long as ~: <span class='gd-jp'>時間さえあれば、旅行に行きたい。</span> (As long as I have time, I want to go traveling.)<br><b>さえ〜たら／なら</b> also means \"as long as ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>時間さえあったら、行けるのに。</span> (If only I had time, I could go.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> is followed by an \"if\" conditional, it means \"as long as ~.\" That includes「〜ば」and also「〜たら」「〜なら」, such as <span class='gd-jp'>あれば</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>あったら</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>あるなら</span>. If there's no such conditional, it means \"even ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn「<span class='gd-jp'>〜さえ〜ば</span>」(as long as ~): <span class='gd-jp'>この薬さえ飲めば、すぐ治りますよ。</span> (As long as you take this medicine, you'll get better soon.)</div>","n3-64":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜こそ」 works like a <b>highlighter</b> run over the word before it. It means 「<b>this is the one</b>」, 「<b>this is what's really ～</b>」, or 「<b>this time for sure</b>」. <span class='gd-jp'>今年頑張る</span> just means \"I'll do my best this year,\" but <span class='gd-jp'>今年こそ頑張る</span> becomes \"This year I'm giving it everything, no matter what.\" It's much stronger.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>This time, for sure</span>You failed before, and now you've made up your mind. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>今年</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>明日</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>次</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今年こそ合格するぞ。</span>(I'm passing this year, for sure!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>次こそ負けないよ。</span>(I definitely won't lose next time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I'm the one who ～</span>When someone thanks you, apologizes, or asks for your kind regard, you reply \"No, it's me who should say that.\" <span class='gd-jp'>こちらこそ</span> works as a complete reply on its own, and it's very handy.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：いつもありがとう。　B：こちらこそ。</span>(A: Thanks for everything. B: No, thank you.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：遅れてすみません。　B：いえ、こちらこそすみません。</span>(A: Sorry I'm late. B: Not at all, I'm the one who's sorry.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>This is the one</span>It stresses \"not anything else, exactly this.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今こそ挑戦するときだ。</span>(Now is exactly the time to take on the challenge.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これこそ私が探していた本だ。</span>(This is exactly the book I've been looking for.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basically it's <b>noun + こそ</b>. If the original sentence has <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>が</span>, just replace it with <span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>今年は頑張る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>今年こそ頑張る</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>これが大切だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>これこそ大切だ</span></span><br><span class='gd-jp'>を</span> is usually dropped too, with <span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span> attached directly.<br>With particles like <span class='gd-jp'>に</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>で</span>, keep them and put <span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span> after them:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あなたにこそ見てほしい。</span>(You're the one I most want to see it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>は</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> can't go before <span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span>. Forms like <span class='gd-jp'>今年はこそ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>私がこそ</span> are wrong, so remove them first. The same goes for <span class='gd-jp'>を</span>, which is normally dropped. Placing them after <span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span> is fine, though: <span class='gd-jp'>今年こそは</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>これこそが</span> are both common.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>こちらこそ</span> is very polite, but <span class='gd-jp'>あなたこそ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>お前こそ</span> are often used to snap back in an argument: <span class='gd-jp'>お前こそ何してるんだよ。</span> (What are YOU doing, then?). Don't use these with people senior to you.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span> is strong. It's there to highlight a key point. If you add it to every word, it's like highlighting the whole page, so nothing stands out.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both talk about this year's exam:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今年は合格したい。</span> (I want to pass this year.): A neutral statement with no special emotion.<br><span class='gd-jp'>今年こそ合格したい。</span> (I want to pass this year, for sure.): You didn't pass before, and this time you're determined.<br><span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span> is often studied together with <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span>, but they point in opposite directions. <span class='gd-jp'>さえ</span> means \"even ～\": it gives an extreme example to imply that everything else goes without saying. It works in both affirmative and negative sentences, for example <span class='gd-jp'>簡単な問題さえできなかった。</span> (I couldn't even do the easy questions.) and <span class='gd-jp'>子供でさえ知っている。</span> (Even children know it.) <span class='gd-jp'>こそ</span>, on the other hand, makes that word the star.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say \"this is the one,\" \"this time for sure,\" or \"I'm the one who...,\" use こそ. To say \"even ～,\" use さえ. For a plain, ordinary statement, just use は.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>からこそ</span> means \"precisely because ～,\" and it stresses the reason: <span class='gd-jp'>好きだからこそ、厳しく言うんだ。</span> (I'm only this strict with you because I care.) You'll also hear <span class='gd-jp'>だからこそ</span> a lot, meaning \"precisely because of that.\"</div>","n3-65":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜そうだ」 means <b>\"looks like ～\"</b> or <b>\"seems ～\"</b>. You haven't tasted the cake yet, but just looking at it you think \"that looks delicious,\" and in Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>おいしそう</span>. The key point is that you <b>see how something looks right now and make an intuitive guess</b>. You haven't actually confirmed it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing from appearance</span>Adjective + そう means \"looks ～.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキ、おいしそう。</span>(This cake looks so good.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さん、今日は元気そうですね。</span>(Mr. Tanaka looks energetic today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>About to happen</span>Verb + そう mostly means \"looks like it's about to ～.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>空が暗くなってきた。雨が降りそうだ。</span>(The sky's getting dark. It looks like it's going to rain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>シャツのボタンが取れそう。</span>(The button on my shirt looks like it's about to come off.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」 and add そう.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span> → drop い → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしそう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高そう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: just add そう.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気そう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暇そう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change to the ます form, drop ます, and add そう.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降ります</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>降りそう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>落ちます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>落ちそう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Two exceptions to memorize</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よさそう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なさそう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Before a noun use <b>そうな</b>, and before a verb use <b>そうに</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>高そうな時計ですね。</span>(That's an expensive-looking watch.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもたちが楽しそうに遊んでいる。</span>(The children are playing and look like they're having fun.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Saying \"doesn't look ～\"</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives: <span class='gd-jp'>おいしくなさそう</span> (doesn't look tasty), <span class='gd-jp'>暇じゃなさそう</span> (doesn't look free)<br>Verbs: use 「そうにない」 or 「そうもない」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この仕事は今日中に終わりそうにない。</span>(It doesn't look like this work will be finished today.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't say 「いそう」 for <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>よさそう</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>天気がよさそうだ</span> (The weather looks nice).<br>・<b>You can't add this そう directly to a noun</b>: ×学生そうだ. To say \"looks like a student,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>学生みたいだ</span>.<br>・Don't use そう for things that are obvious at a glance and need no guessing. A red flower is simply <span class='gd-jp'>赤い</span>, not 「赤そう」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>かわいそう</span> means \"poor thing,\" not \"looks cute.\" To say something is cute, use <span class='gd-jp'>かわいい</span>.<br>・With verbs, そう often means \"about to ～\": <span class='gd-jp'>泣きそう</span> means \"about to cry,\" not \"seems to be crying.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <b>hearsay そうだ</b> (the next grammar point) looks almost identical, but the meaning is completely different. Compare them on the same topic:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうだ。</span> (Looking at the sky, it looks like rain is coming.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降るそうだ。</span> (I heard from the weather forecast that it will rain.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>おいしそうだ。</span> (It looks tasty.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>おいしいそうだ。</span> (I heard it's tasty.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>元気そうだ。</span> (He looks energetic.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>元気だそうだ。</span> (I heard he's energetic.)<br>Nouns only have the hearsay version: <span class='gd-jp'>学生だそうだ。</span> (I heard he's a student.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tests:<br>・Verbs and い-adjectives: if the part before そう is shortened (<span class='gd-jp'>降り</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>おいし</span>), it means \"looks like.\" If the full form comes before it (<span class='gd-jp'>降る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>), it means \"I heard.\"<br>・な-adjectives and nouns: check whether there's a 「だ」. <span class='gd-jp'>元気そうだ</span> = looks like; <span class='gd-jp'>元気だそうだ</span> = I heard; <span class='gd-jp'>学生だそうだ</span> = I heard he's a student.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <b>ようだ・みたいだ</b> mean \"seems like\" too, but they're more of a judgment you make after putting several clues together. そう is a <b>first impression from a single glance</b>. You see a cake and say <span class='gd-jp'>おいしそう</span>. You say <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいみたい</span> only after seeing everyone enjoying it.</div>","n3-66":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜そうだ」means <b>“I hear that…” or “they say…”</b>. When you pick something up from the news, a friend, or the weather forecast and want to pass it on, you add そうだ at the end of the sentence. The key point: <b>you didn't see this yourself and you're not guessing. Someone else said it.</b></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Passing on what you heard</span>Relay what someone else said as it is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来月結婚するそうです。</span>(I hear Mr. Tanaka is getting married next month.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長は今日、会社に来ないそうです。</span>(I hear the department head isn't coming to the office today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Naming the source</span>You'll often see「<span class='gd-jp'>〜によると</span>」or「<span class='gd-jp'>〜の話では</span>」in front, making clear where you heard it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうだ。</span>(According to the weather forecast, it will rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生の話では、来週テストがあるそうです。</span>(The teacher says there's a test next week.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the content you're relaying into the <b>plain form</b> (the form without ます or です), then add そうだ. Tense and affirmative/negative are all handled inside the part before そうだ.<br><b>Verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降るそうだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降ったそうだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降らないそうだ</span></span><br><b>i-adjectives</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いそうだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒かった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒かったそうだ</span></span><br><b>na-adjectives</b>: you need to add だ　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだそうだ</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add だ here too　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だそうだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったそうだ</span></span><br>When talking to a teacher or a customer, change the ending to <span class='gd-jp'>そうです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Among friends</span>In casual speech people more often use「<span class='gd-jp'>〜って</span>」or「<span class='gd-jp'>〜んだって</span>」. The meaning is the same: “I hear that…”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さん、来月結婚するんだって。</span>(I heard Tanaka's getting married next month!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、雨だって。</span>(Apparently it's going to rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a noun or na-adjective, <b>you can't drop だ</b>. Writing <span class='gd-jp'>静かそうだ</span> turns it into “looks quiet,” which is a completely different meaning. “I hear it's quiet” is <span class='gd-jp'>静かだそうだ</span>.<br>・<b>そうだ itself can't be made negative or past</b>. You can't say ×<span class='gd-jp'>来るそうではない</span> or ×<span class='gd-jp'>来るそうだった</span>. Change the part before it instead: <span class='gd-jp'>来ないそうだ</span> (I hear he's not coming), <span class='gd-jp'>来たそうだ</span> (I hear he came).<br>・In English, “I hear that…” sits at the start of the sentence, but Japanese そうだ always goes at the <b>very end</b>.<br>・For hearsay with <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span>, it's <span class='gd-jp'>いいそうだ</span> (I hear it's good). <span class='gd-jp'>よさそうだ</span> means “looks good,” which is the other そうだ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Appearance そうだ (n3-65)</b>: something you see or sense yourself, “looks like… / is about to…”. For verbs, take ます off the ます-form; for adjectives, drop い; then add そうだ.<br><b>Hearsay そうだ (this lesson)</b>: something someone told you, “I hear that…”, attached to the full plain form.<br>Both about the weather:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうだ。</span>(It looks like it's about to rain: you judge from looking at the sky.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降るそうだ。</span>(I hear it's going to rain: you got it from the forecast.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both about a cake:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキはおいしそうだ。</span>(This cake looks delicious.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキはおいしいそうだ。</span>(I hear this cake is delicious.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>らしい (n3-52)</b> can also be translated as “I hear,” but with そうだ someone clearly said it and you're relaying it. らしい is more like “it seems that…”: the source is vaguer, or you're inferring from things you heard or saw.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来月結婚するそうだ。</span>(I hear Mr. Tanaka is getting married next month: someone told me so.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来月結婚するらしい。</span>(It seems Mr. Tanaka is getting married next month: I've picked up some rumors.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before is the full form like「<span class='gd-jp'>降る</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span>」, it means “I hear.” If it's the clipped form like「<span class='gd-jp'>降り</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>おいし</span>」, it means “looks like.”</div>","n3-67":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Think of「くる」and「いく」as arrows: <b>くる = moving toward “me, now”</b>, <b>いく = moving away from “me, now”</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>てくる</span> often matches English “come / start to / has been getting…”, and <span class='gd-jp'>ていく</span> often matches “go / keep on…ing from here.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in the te-form, then add くる or いく.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>増える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増えて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増えてくる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>なる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なって</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なっていく</span></span><br>The くる or いく at the end conjugates as usual: <span class='gd-jp'>てきます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>てきた</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>ていきます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>ていった</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Direction in space</span>Use てくる when something or someone comes toward you, and ていく when it leaves your side. <b>What matters is where you are when you speak.</b></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうだから、傘を持っていこう。</span>(It looks like rain, so let's take an umbrella with us.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達がケーキを持ってきてくれた。</span>(A friend brought me a cake.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Go do something and come back</span>てくる can also mean “go do something and then come back.” The <span class='gd-jp'>行ってきます</span> you say when leaving the house means “I'll go and be back.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっとコンビニで飲み物を買ってくる。</span>(I'll go grab a drink at the convenience store and be right back.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Change: up to now</span><span class='gd-jp'>てきた</span> = it has been changing from the past up to now, and you can feel it now.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、日本語が少し分かってきた。</span>(Lately I've started to understand a bit of Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここ数年、オンライン会議が増えてきた。</span>(Online meetings have become more and more common over the past few years.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Change: from now on</span><span class='gd-jp'>ていく</span> = starting now, it will keep changing or keep going.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからどんどん寒くなっていく。</span>(It's going to get colder and colder from now on.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからも日本語の勉強を続けていきたい。</span>(I want to keep studying Japanese from now on, too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Starting to happen</span><span class='gd-jp'>てきた</span> is also often used to say a state has “started to appear,” like English “it's starting to…”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、雨が降ってきた。</span>(Oh, it's started to rain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お腹がすいてきた。</span>(I'm getting hungry.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Direction depends on <b>where you are when you speak</b>, not where the listener is. If you promise your teacher in person at school, you're already at the place the thing will be delivered to, so <span class='gd-jp'>はい、明日持ってきます。</span> is perfectly natural.<br>・English “I'll go buy it” doesn't say whether you come back. If you'll return after buying, Japanese speakers often say <span class='gd-jp'>買ってくる</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>買っていく</span> means “buy it and take it somewhere else,” for example <span class='gd-jp'>お土産を買っていく</span> (buy a souvenir to take along).<br>・When talking about time, don't mix up “up to now” and “from now on”: if <span class='gd-jp'>これから</span> comes before it, you need ていく. You can't say 「これから寒くなってきた」.<br>・くる and いく here are usually written in hiragana. The past form is <span class='gd-jp'>てきた</span>, not 「てくた」; the past of ていく is <span class='gd-jp'>ていった</span>.<br>・In casual speech ていく often becomes 「てく」, as in <span class='gd-jp'>寒くなってく</span>. Don't mistake it for a different grammar point when you hear it.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Three ways to say “the weather is getting colder”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>寒くなってきた。</span>(It's already been getting colder and colder, and you can feel it now.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>寒くなっていく。</span>(It's going to get colder and colder from now on.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>寒くなる一方だ。</span>(It just keeps getting colder with no end in sight, often with a hint of helplessness.)<br>The first two differ in time direction, while <span class='gd-jp'>一方だ</span> stresses “it only keeps developing in this one direction.”</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: looking from the past up to now, use てきた. Looking from now onward, use ていく. For “it just keeps getting worse and won't stop,” use 一方だ.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add ている after てきた to get <span class='gd-jp'>てきている</span>, meaning “this change is still going on now”: <span class='gd-jp'>外国人観光客が増えてきている。</span>(The number of foreign tourists keeps growing, and it's still rising.)</div>","n3-57":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たびに」 means <b>\"every time ~, ~\"</b>. Whenever the first thing happens, the second thing follows, with almost no exceptions. Think \"every time I hear this song, I want to cry\" or \"I buy souvenirs on every business trip.\" That's this pattern.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A feeling or reaction that comes every time</span>Whenever something happens, the same feeling rises up in you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この写真を見るたびに、学生時代を思い出す。</span>（Every time I look at this photo, I remember my school days.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この歌を聞くたびに、泣いてしまう。</span>（Every time I hear this song, I end up crying.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something you do every time</span>Whenever something happens, you always do a certain action.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>出張のたびにお土産を買って帰る。</span>（I buy a souvenir and bring it home every time I go on a business trip.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A change you notice every time</span>Each time you meet, you find that something is different.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼に会うたびに、背が伸びている。</span>（Every time I see him, he's grown taller.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: dictionary form (the base form you'd look up in a dictionary) + たびに.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会うたびに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見るたびに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るたびに</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>旅行</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>旅行のたびに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>試験</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>試験のたびに</span></span><br>The noun should be <b>something that happens repeatedly</b>, like <span class='gd-jp'>出張</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>旅行</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>試験</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>引っ越し</span>. Both <span class='gd-jp'>雨のたびに</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降るたびに</span> are fine.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Even when you're talking about the past, <b>the verb before it stays in dictionary form</b>. Put the past 「た」 at the end of the sentence only: <span class='gd-jp'>子供のころ、祖母の家に行くたびに、お菓子をもらった。</span>（When I was a kid, every time I went to my grandmother's house, I got sweets.）Don't say 「行ったたびに」.<br>・What comes before it must be something that <b>can happen many times</b>. Things that happen once in a lifetime (being born, graduating from university) can't be used.<br>・A noun before it must stand for \"an event.\" Nouns that aren't events, like <span class='gd-jp'>本</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>先生</span>, don't work. Switch to a verb: <span class='gd-jp'>本を読むたびに</span>（every time I read a book）.<br>・For everyday routines that happen as a matter of course, like brushing your teeth or eating breakfast, Japanese people usually just say <span class='gd-jp'>毎朝</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>いつも</span>. Using たびに makes it sound like you're stressing something special, which feels odd.<br>・たびに is also often written with kanji as <span class='gd-jp'>度に</span>. It's read the same way, so don't mistake it for a different grammar point.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same situation, \"going to Japan\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くたびに、ラーメンを食べる。</span>（Every time I go to Japan, I eat ramen.）<br>　→ Stresses \"every single time, without missing once.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くときは、いつもラーメンを食べる。</span>（When I go to Japan, I always eat ramen.）<br>　→ Almost the same meaning, but the tone is plainer and more everyday.<br><span class='gd-jp'>二年ごとに日本へ行く。</span>（I go to Japan every two years.）<br>　→ <b>ごとに</b> can follow a number, to say \"every so often\" or \"every so many.\" たびに can't do that, so you can't say 「二年のたびに」.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if what comes before is a <b>number or a time interval</b>, use ごとに. If it's <b>an event</b> and you want to say \"every time ~, ~,\" use たびに, or the lighter 「ときはいつも」 if you want it to sound more relaxed.</div>","n3-58":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を中心に」 means <b>\"centered on ~,\" \"with ~ as the core,\" \"mainly ~\"</b>. You treat something as the core and things unfold around it, or you say where the focus or main range of something lies.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Places</span>Spreading out or distributed around a certain place.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅を中心に店が並んでいる。</span>（Shops line the streets centered on the station.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>九州を中心に大雨が降った。</span>（Heavy rain fell mainly around Kyushu.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>People</span>With a certain person as the core, everyone moves around them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんを中心に、新しいチームができた。</span>（A new team was formed with Mr. Tanaka at its core.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Main range and focus</span>\"Mainly ~,\" but it doesn't mean only that.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このアニメは子どもを中心に人気がある。</span>（This anime is popular mainly among children.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は文法を中心に復習しよう。</span>（Let's review mainly grammar today.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>What comes before it is always a <b>noun</b>, and an action or state follows:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>駅</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>駅を中心に</span></span>　You can also say <span class='gd-jp'>駅を中心にして</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>駅を中心として</span>, with the same meaning.<br>When it needs to modify a noun directly, change it to <b>を中心とした</b> (or <span class='gd-jp'>を中心とする</span>):</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>野菜を中心とした食事</span>（a diet centered on vegetables）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>東京を中心とした関東地方</span>（the Kanto region, centered on Tokyo）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The particle is <b>を</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>駅を中心に</span>, not 「駅に中心に」 or 「駅が中心に」.<br>・You can't put a verb directly before it; use a noun. To say \"focusing on studying,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>勉強を中心に</span>.<br>・「を中心に」 is different from 「<b>の</b>中心に」. <span class='gd-jp'>公園の中心に噴水がある。</span> means \"There's a fountain right in the middle of the park.\" It only describes a position, not something \"unfolding around ~.\"<br>・\"Mainly ~\" doesn't mean \"only\": <span class='gd-jp'>若者を中心に人気だ</span> means most fans are young people, but people of other ages like it too.<br>・When modifying a noun after it, don't write 「野菜を中心にの食事」. Use <span class='gd-jp'>野菜を中心とした食事</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をはじめ（として）</b> = \"starting with A, and also B, C…\" It <b>picks one representative example</b>, and the point is that there are others besides it.<br><b>を中心に</b> = \"mainly A,\" and the point is that <b>A is the core and carries the most weight</b>.<br>Compare them in the same situation:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このお菓子は東京を中心に人気がある。</span>（This sweet is popular mainly in Tokyo.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このお菓子は東京をはじめ、大阪や福岡でも人気がある。</span>（This sweet is popular in Tokyo, and also in Osaka, Fukuoka, and elsewhere.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"mainly in A, centered on A,\" use を中心に. To say \"A is just one example, and B and C are the same,\" use をはじめ.</div>","n3-68":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three are 「te-form + a little tail」. The difference is what you want to say:<br><b>ている</b> = how things are now (in the middle of doing something, or staying in a state)<br><b>てある</b> = someone set something up and it's still that way (you can tell it was done on purpose)<br><b>ておく</b> = do it ahead of time, for later (like English \"go ahead and ~\" or \"~ in advance\")<br>Picture walking into a meeting room and seeing the window open. Just describing \"the window is open\" uses ている. Saying \"someone has already opened the window\" uses てある. Before the meeting, when you say \"I'll open the window first,\" you use ておく.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>All of them attach after the verb's te-form:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>開ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>開けて</span></span> → <span class='gd-jp'>開けている</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>開けてある</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>開けておく</span></div><div class='gd-h'>ている: how things are now</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In progress</span>The action is happening right now.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、ご飯を食べている。</span>(I'm eating right now.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>State remains</span>Something happened, and the result has stayed that way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>エアコンがついている。</span>(The air conditioner is on.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>姉は結婚している。</span>(My older sister is married.)</div><div class='gd-h'>てある: someone set it up, and it stays</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Result + done by someone</span>The focus is \"it's like this now, and someone did it on purpose.\" Who did it usually goes unsaid. The thing in front is mostly marked with <b>が</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ドアに名前が書いてある。</span>(A name is written on the door.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ビールが冷やしてある。</span>(The beer has been chilled.)</div><div class='gd-h'>ておく: do it in advance</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Preparing ahead</span>You get something done first, for later.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>旅行の前に、ホテルを予約しておく。</span>(Before the trip, I'll book the hotel ahead of time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Leave as is</span>You deliberately keep it that way and don't touch it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓は開けておいてください。</span>(Please leave the window open. No need to close it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In speech it's often shortened to <b>とく</b>:<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買っておく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買っとく</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んでおく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んどく</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ジュース、買っとくね。</span>(I'll go ahead and buy the juice, okay?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・First, separate two kinds of verbs: verbs where <b>things change by themselves</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>開く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>つく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>閉まる</span>; the thing takes が), and verbs where <b>someone acts on it</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>開ける</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>つける</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>閉める</span>; the thing takes を).<br>・てある goes with the \"someone acts on it\" type: <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開けてある</span> is correct, while <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いてある</span> is not said.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓が開けている</span> is wrong; it would come out like \"the window is opening something.\" Say either <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いている</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>窓が開けてある</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>結婚している</span> means \"is married,\" not \"is getting married right now\"; <span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span> means \"know.\" Don't turn every ている into \"is ~ing.\"<br>・The casual 「てる」 is a contraction of ている, while 「とく」 is the one that means ておく. Don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>One window, four ways to say it:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いている。</span>(The window is open: just describing what you see.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓が開けてある。</span>(The window has been opened: you sense someone opened it on purpose for some reason.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けておく。</span>(I'll open the window in advance: because it'll be needed soon.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが窓を開けている。</span>(Mr. Tanaka is opening the window: the focus is on the person's action.)<br>Also, once you've done something with <b>ておいた</b>, the resulting state becomes <b>てある</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>昨日資料を準備しておいたから、もう準備してある。</span>(I prepared the materials yesterday, so they're already ready.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to describe only how something looks, use 「が＋ている」. To stress \"someone set it up and it's still that way,\" use 「が＋てある」. To say \"do it first, for later,\" use 「を＋ておく」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜っぱなし」 also leaves something as it is, but it's mostly about <b>forgetting to put it away or just leaving it</b>, with a negative feel: <span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けっぱなしにした。</span>(I left the window open and never closed it.) That's the exact opposite of the deliberate <span class='gd-jp'>開けておく</span>.</div>","n3-69":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, \"do it for me\" or \"help\" covers everything: he did it for me, I did it for him, I asked him to do it for me. Japanese adds <b>くれる、あげる、もらう</b> after the verb to show which direction the favor travels.<br><b>くれる</b>: someone else → me (someone does it for me)<br><b>あげる</b>: me → someone else (I do it for someone)<br><b>もらう</b>: I receive the favor from someone (I get someone to do it for me)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its te-form, then attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>手伝う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>手伝って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>手伝ってくれる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>教える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>教えて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>教えてもらう</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Looking at the three separately: it's all about particles</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>てくれる</span>The star is the <b>other person</b>, and the doer takes <b>が</b>. \"Me\" usually goes unsaid. My family counts as \"my side\" too, so you can use it for them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達が引っ越しを手伝ってくれた。</span>(My friend helped me move.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母がお弁当を作ってくれた。</span>(My mom made me a lunch box.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>てもらう</span>The star is <b>me</b>, and the doer takes <b>に</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅員さんに道を教えてもらった。</span>(I had the station attendant tell me the way.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生にほめてもらった。</span>(I got praised by my teacher.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>てあげる</span>The star is me, and the particle after the other person <b>depends on the original verb</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>妹に本を読んであげた。</span>(I read a book to my little sister.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達を駅まで送ってあげた。</span>(I saw my friend off to the station.)</div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most: asking a favor</div><div class='gd-sec'>Turn it into a question and it means \"could you ~ for me?\" The further down the list, the more polite.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと手伝ってくれる？</span>(Can you give me a hand? To a friend.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この書類、確認してもらえますか。</span>(Could you check this document for me?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう少しゆっくり話してもらえませんか。</span>(Could you speak a little more slowly?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Wrong direction</b>: くれる can only point toward \"my side.\" For \"I taught my friend,\" you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>友達に教えてくれた</span>; say <span class='gd-jp'>友達に教えてあげた</span>.<br>・<b>Wrong particle</b>: くれる goes with が, もらう goes with に. <span class='gd-jp'>友達に手伝ってくれた</span> is wrong.<br>・<b>Translating too plainly sounds cold</b>: for \"He bought it for me,\" saying <span class='gd-jp'>彼が買った</span> is grammatical, but it sounds like you aren't grateful. When someone does something for you, Japanese people almost always add <span class='gd-jp'>くれた</span>.<br>・<b>てあげる is rude toward superiors</b>: it feels like \"I'm doing you a favor.\" Saying <span class='gd-jp'>荷物を持ってあげます</span> to a teacher won't do; say <span class='gd-jp'>お持ちします</span> (let me carry that for you). Even with friends, saying 「～てあげる」 to their face can sound condescending, so people often say <span class='gd-jp'>手伝おうか？</span> (want me to help?) instead.<br>・<b>もらう doesn't always mean \"ask\"</b>: in <span class='gd-jp'>ほめてもらった</span>, you didn't ask the teacher for anything; you just received the benefit.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>てくれる and てもらう often describe <b>the same event</b>; the difference is who the star is.<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんが傘を貸してくれた。</span>(Mr. Tanaka lent me his umbrella: the star is Tanaka, and the focus is his kindness.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんに傘を貸してもらった。</span>(I got to borrow an umbrella from Mr. Tanaka: the star is me, and it often feels like \"I was the one who asked.\")</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if the doer takes <b>が</b>, use くれる; if it takes <b>に</b>, use もらう. To stress the other person's kindness, use くれる; to say \"I asked and got help,\" use もらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>With teachers and superiors, switch to the honorific versions: くれる → <span class='gd-jp'>くださる</span>, もらう → <span class='gd-jp'>いただく</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生が教えてくださいました。</span>(The teacher taught me.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生に教えていただきました。</span>(I had the teacher teach me.)</div>","n3-70":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜させる」 means <b>\"make someone do ~\"</b> or <b>\"let someone do ~\"</b>. A mother makes her child eat vegetables, a boss lets employees leave early: both use it. It can mean forcing someone or allowing them, and the context tells you which.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Making someone do</span>You tell someone to do something, usually from a higher position to a lower one, such as parents to a child or a manager to a subordinate.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母は子供に野菜を食べさせた。</span>(Mom made her child eat vegetables.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Letting someone do</span>The other person already wants to do it, and you allow it or step back and let them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は私を一人で旅行に行かせてくれた。</span>(My dad let me go on a trip by myself.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Causing a feeling</span>Making someone cry, making someone happy, making someone worry.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女を泣かせてしまった。</span>(I made my girlfriend cry.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親を心配させたくない。</span>(I don't want to make my parents worry.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「あ」-row sound of the same row, then add 「せる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to か → <span class='gd-jp'>書かせる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲ませる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たせる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らせる</span></span><br>Watch out for verbs ending in 「う」: the 「う」 changes to <b>わ</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → change う to わ → <span class='gd-jp'>買わせる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「させる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べさせる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見させる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>調べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>調べさせる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>させる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来させる</span>（こさせる）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once formed, 「〜させる」 is itself a Group 2 verb, so it conjugates like one: <span class='gd-jp'>書かせます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書かせた</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書かせて</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書かせない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Who makes, who does: particle placement</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basic pattern: <b>the person who makes</b> + は／が, <b>the person who does it</b> + に or を, and 〜させる at the end. Whether the doer takes に or を depends on whether the action already has its own 「を」:<br><b>Actions that already have a 「を」 object</b> (read a book, eat a meal, do homework): the doer takes <b>に</b>, because を is already used by the object.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は学生に教科書を読ませた。</span>(The teacher had the students read the textbook.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Actions with no 「を」 object</b> (go, play, cry, wait): the doer usually takes <b>を</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供を公園で遊ばせる。</span>(I let my child play in the park.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second case can also use に, as in <span class='gd-jp'>子供に自由に遊ばせる</span>, which sounds more like \"respecting the other person and letting them do whatever they like.\" When you're just starting, the basic rule above is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜させてください</span>\"Please let me ~.\" A polite way to ask permission when you want to do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>少し考えさせてください。</span>(Please let me think about it for a moment.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここで働かせてください。</span>(Please let me work here.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>待たせる</span>\"Make someone wait.\" Very common in apologies.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>待たせてごめん。</span>(Sorry to keep you waiting.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お待たせしました。</span>(Thank you for waiting. / Sorry to keep you waiting.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs ending in 「う」 change to わ: <span class='gd-jp'>買わせる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>会わせる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>使わせる</span>, not 買あせる.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来させる</span> is read <b>こさせる</b>, not きさせる.<br>・Don't add an extra 「さ」 to Group 1 verbs: it's <span class='gd-jp'>読ませる</span>, not 読まさせる. Japanese people often say it that way too, but it's generally considered incorrect.<br>・Don't use させる toward teachers, bosses, or customers, because it sounds like you're giving them orders. To ask someone above you to do something, use 「〜てもらう」 or 「〜ていただく」: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に説明していただきました。</span>(The teacher kindly explained it to me.)<br>・English happily says \"This movie moved me,\" but in Japanese it's more natural to say <span class='gd-jp'>この映画に感動した</span> (\"I was moved by this movie\"). A literal translation like この映画は私を感動させた sounds like translationese.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same event, cleaning a room, and look at who orders and who does it:<br><b>させる</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>母は私に部屋を掃除させた。</span>(Mom made me clean my room: Mom orders, I do it.)<br><b>させられる</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>私は母に部屋を掃除させられた。</span>(I was made to clean my room by Mom: I'm still the one cleaning, but it's told from my side, with a feeling of reluctance.)<br><b>てもらう</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>私は母に部屋を掃除してもらった。</span>(I had Mom clean my room for me: I asked, Mom did it.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the subject makes someone else do it, it's させる. If the subject is the one being forced, it's させられる. If the subject asks someone to do it for them, it's てもらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add the passive to させる and you get 「being forced to do ~」: <span class='gd-jp'>〜させられる</span> (causative-passive): <span class='gd-jp'>子供のころ、毎日ピアノを練習させられた。</span>(When I was a kid, I was made to practice piano every day.)</div>","n3-71":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜（ら）れる」 is the Japanese <b>passive, like English \"was/got ~ed\"</b>. It puts the focus on the person who receives the action: not \"the teacher scolded me\" but \"I was scolded by the teacher.\" Japanese also has a special use: when something happens that causes you trouble, you can use it to complain, like \"I had such bad luck.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「あ」-row sound of the same row, then add 「れる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>呼ぶ</span> → change ぶ to ば → <span class='gd-jp'>呼ばれる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>踏む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>踏まれる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>叱る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>叱られる</span></span><br><b>Watch out for verbs ending in う</b>: う doesn't become あ, it becomes <b>わ</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言われる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買われる</span></span><br>The easiest way to think of it: make the ない form, remove ない, and add れる. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言われる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「られる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>褒める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>褒められる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見られる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>される</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span>（こられる）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once formed, it is itself a Group 2 verb and keeps conjugating by the Group 2 rules:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>褒められる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>褒められた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>褒められます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>褒められない</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basic pattern: <b>(me) は + the person who does the action + に + passive form</b>. The doer takes 「に」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は先生に叱られた。</span>(I was scolded by the teacher.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>上司にアイデアを褒められた。</span>(My idea was praised by my boss.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It isn't always bad news: you can also use it for being praised or being chosen.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Body or belongings affected</span>Your foot gets stepped on, your wallet gets stolen. Japanese usually says \"I + thing + を + passive,\" so the main character is still \"I.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車で足を踏まれた。</span>(I had my foot stepped on on the train.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅で財布を盗まれた。</span>(I had my wallet stolen at the station.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Bad luck, trouble</span>Something someone else did (or even the weather) inconveniences you. English has no neat equivalent for this (you can't say \"I was cried by the baby\"), which makes it a very distinctive feature of Japanese.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰りに雨に降られた。</span>(I got caught in the rain on the way home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夜中に赤ちゃんに泣かれて、全然寝られなかった。</span>(The baby kept crying in the middle of the night, and I couldn't sleep at all.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Objective description</span>Common in news and introductions. It carries no emotion and doesn't say who did it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このお寺は500年前に建てられた。</span>(This temple was built 500 years ago.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs ending in う change it to <b>わ</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>買われる</span>, not 買あれる.<br>・The doer takes 「に」: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に叱られた</span> means \"I was scolded by the teacher\"; written as <span class='gd-jp'>先生が叱られた</span> it becomes \"The teacher was scolded.\"<br>・English says \"my foot got stepped on,\" but Japanese doesn't say 私の足が踏まれた. Say <span class='gd-jp'>足を踏まれた</span>.<br>・The passive of a Group 2 verb looks exactly like its potential form: <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span> can mean \"be eaten\" or \"can eat,\" so check the context.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span> is read <b>こられる</b>, not きられる.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>られる (passive)</b> = someone else's action lands on me, and I'm the one who \"takes\" it:<br><span class='gd-jp'>弟にケーキを食べられた。</span>(My little brother ate my cake.)<br><b>させる (causative)</b> = I make or let someone else do it:<br><span class='gd-jp'>弟にケーキを食べさせた。</span>(I let my little brother eat the cake.)<br><b>てもらう</b> = someone does it for me, and I'm grateful:<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生に作文を褒めてもらった。</span>(The teacher praised my essay, and I'm happy about it.)<br>Saying the same thing with <span class='gd-jp'>褒められた</span> just states the fact that you were praised, without any particular note of gratitude.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"was ~ed\" uses れる・られる, \"make someone ~\" uses せる・させる, and \"someone did ~ for me (thankfully)\" uses てもらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Combine the causative and the passive and you get \"being forced to do ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>母に野菜を食べさせられた。</span>(I was made to eat vegetables by my mom.)</div>","n3-76":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three of these, <b>「～てたまらない」「～てしかたがない」「～てならない」</b>, mean \"I can't stand it\" or \"it's killing me.\" Use them when a feeling gets so strong you can't hold it down: so hot you can't stand it, homesick to the point of aching, worried sick. The three mean nearly the same thing. They differ in tone and setting.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Physical sensations</span>Hot, cold, pain, sleepiness, hunger, itching.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お腹が空いてたまらない。</span>(I'm starving, I can't stand it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>足が痛くてしかたがない。</span>(My foot hurts like crazy.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Emotions</span>Happy, sad, lonely, worried, bothered.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試験の結果が心配でならない。</span>(I'm incredibly worried about the exam results.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Wanting to do something badly</span>Put 「〜たい」 in front and you get \"I want to ~ so badly I can't stand it.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族に会いたくてたまらない。</span>(I miss my family so much, I can't bear it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The key is that the word in front must first be in its 「て」 form, and each type of word changes differently:<br><b>Verbs</b>: use the て-form as is　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>気になる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>気になって</span>しかたがない</span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: replace the final 「い」 with 「くて」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暑い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暑くて</span>たまらない</span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: just add 「で」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>心配</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>心配で</span>ならない</span><br>「〜たい」 changes just like an い-adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会いたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会いたくて</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to say \"I'm dying of sleepiness\":<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>眠くてたまらない。</span>(The most direct and vivid. Common in speech.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>眠くてしかたがない。</span>(Sleepy non-stop, and you can't shake it. In speech it's often said as <span class='gd-jp'>眠くてしょうがない</span>.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>眠くてならない。</span>(Same meaning, but more formal and bookish. Rare in everyday chat.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ならない</b> has one especially common pattern: \"<b>I just can't shake the feeling that ~</b>.\" Here ならない is the usual choice, and たまらない is rarely used this way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何か忘れているような気がしてならない。</span>(I keep feeling like I've forgotten something.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の話は嘘のように思えてならない。</span>(However I look at it, his story sounds like a lie.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: when you're talking to a friend about \"~ like crazy,\" use たまらない or しょうがない. In writing, or for \"I can't help feeling that ~,\" use ならない.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't attach て directly to an い-adjective: it's <span class='gd-jp'>暑くてたまらない</span>, not 暑いてたまらない. For な-adjectives it's <span class='gd-jp'>心配で</span>, not 心配て.<br>・This describes <b>your own</b> feelings. For other people, add <span class='gd-jp'>ようだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>らしい</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>弟は寂しくてたまらないようだ。</span>(My little brother seems to be terribly lonely.)<br>・It only works with sensations and emotions, not plain descriptions. You can't say 広くてたまらない for \"this room is huge.\" Use something like <span class='gd-jp'>すごく広い</span> instead.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>てならない</span> is not the same as <span class='gd-jp'>てはならない</span>! Add one 「は」 and it means \"must not ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>ここに入ってはならない。</span>(You must not enter here.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>しかたがない</span> on its own means \"it can't be helped\": <span class='gd-jp'>雨だから、しかたがない。</span>(It's raining, so it can't be helped.) It only means \"~ unbearably\" after a て-form.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>On its own, <span class='gd-jp'>たまらない</span> often means \"so good I can't stand it\": <span class='gd-jp'>仕事の後のビール、たまらない！</span>(A beer after work, nothing beats it!)</div>","n3-77":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜どおりに／〜とおりに」 means <b>\"as ~\", \"just like ~\", \"according to ~\"</b>. Use it for cooking by the recipe, following the plan, or writing what the teacher said. The kanji is 「通り」. Picture a model that has already drawn the route, and you simply follow it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Following a model</span>You have something to follow, such as a recipe, a manual, a plan, or what someone said, and you do it that way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>説明書どおりに組み立てた。</span>(I assembled it following the instructions.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生が言ったとおりに書いてください。</span>(Please write it the way the teacher said.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just as expected</span>The result matches what you thought or predicted, a bit like \"just as I expected.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>予想どおり、試験は難しかった。</span>(Just as expected, the exam was hard.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>思ったとおり、彼は来なかった。</span>(Just as I thought, he didn't come.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>What matters is <b>whether a noun or a verb comes before it</b>, because the reading changes:<br><b>Noun directly attached</b>: read <b>どおり</b> (with the voiced mark)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>計画</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>計画どおり</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>予定</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>予定どおり</span></span><br><b>Noun with 「の」 in between</b>: read <b>とおり</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>説明書</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>説明書のとおり</span></span><br><b>Verb</b>: dictionary form or た-form, read <b>とおり</b><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言うとおり</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>教わった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>教わったとおり</span></span><br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>そのとおりです</span> (\"That's right,\" \"You're exactly right\") is a very common way to agree when the other person has it right.</div><div class='gd-sec'>What follows depends on what you want to say:<br>Before an action, use 「に」, which is often dropped in speech: <span class='gd-jp'>計画どおり進んでいる。</span>(Things are going according to plan.)<br>Before a noun, use 「の」: <span class='gd-jp'>予想どおりの結果だった。</span>(It was the result we expected.)<br>At the end of a sentence, use 「だ／です」: <span class='gd-jp'>結果は期待どおりだった。</span>(The result was just what we hoped for.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Dictionary form or た-form?</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>た-form</span>The model has <b>already happened</b>: something already taught, said, or seen. This is the most common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>見たとおりに話してください。</span>(Please tell it the way you saw it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Dictionary form</span>The model is <b>happening right now</b>, or you mean \"do what someone says\" in general.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私が言うとおりに書いてください。</span>(Please write down what I'm saying, as I say it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親の言うとおりにする。</span>(I do as my parents say.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Only a noun attached <b>directly</b> is read どおり. With の in between it becomes とおり: <span class='gd-jp'>説明書のとおり</span> is right, not 説明書のどおり; <span class='gd-jp'>計画どおり</span> is right, not 計画とおり.<br>・After a verb, read it <b>とおり</b>. Don't write the voiced どおり.<br>・In English you \"follow the plan,\" but in Japanese you <b>don't add を</b> before it: 計画をどおりに is wrong.<br>・Before a noun you must have の: <span class='gd-jp'>予想どおりの結果</span>. You can't say 予想どおり結果.<br>・In Japan you'll see street names like <span class='gd-jp'>銀座通り</span>. That 「通り」 means \"street\" and has nothing to do with this grammar.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>どおりに</b> = you do it <b>exactly</b> like the model: <span class='gd-jp'>マニュアルどおりに対応した。</span>(I handled it exactly by the manual.)<br><b>に基づいて</b> = you use it as a <b>basis or foundation</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>マニュアルに基づいて対応した。</span>(I handled it based on the manual.)<br>Also, \"the result was just as expected\" only works with どおり, as in <span class='gd-jp'>予想どおり</span>. You can't swap in に基づいて.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for following something exactly, or \"just as I expected,\" use どおり／とおり. If you're only using something as a basis or foundation, use に基づいて.</div>","n3-72":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜させられる」 means <b>\"be made to do ~\"</b> or <b>\"be forced to do ~\"</b>. <b>You're the one doing it</b>, but you don't want to; someone else is making you. It usually carries a note of resentment or complaint.</div><div class='gd-h'>Where it comes from</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's two grammar points stacked together: 「させる」 = make someone do something, and 「られる」 = passive.<br>Mom told me to clean. Said from Mom's point of view:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母は私に部屋を掃除させた。</span>(Mom made me clean my room)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Now switch to <b>my</b> point of view, and it becomes \"I was made to clean by Mom\":</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は母に部屋を掃除させられた。</span>(I was made to clean my room by Mom)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The subject becomes \"I\", and the person making you do it is marked with <b>に</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「あ」-row sound in the same row, then add 「される」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → く becomes か → <span class='gd-jp'>書かされる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まされる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たされる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買わされる</span></span><br>Note that verbs ending in 「う」 change to 「わ」, not 「あ」.<br>This is the short form commonly used in speech. The full form is <span class='gd-jp'>書かせられる</span>. Both are correct, but you'll hear the short form more in everyday talk.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>An exception within Group 1: verbs ending in 「す」</b> can't use the short form; they take 「させられる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話させられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出させられる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「させられる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べさせられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やめる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>やめさせられる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (just two verbs, memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>させられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来させられる</span> (こさせられる)</span><br>\"Noun + する\" verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>残業する</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>スピーチする</span> work the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>残業する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>残業させられる</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Forced to do</span>The basic use: complaining about something you didn't want to do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どものころ、毎日ピアノを練習させられた。</span>(As a kid, I was made to practice piano every day)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>飲み会でお酒をたくさん飲まされた。</span>(I got made to drink a lot at the drinking party)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Kept waiting</span><span class='gd-jp'>待たされる</span> is very common. It means \"I was kept waiting for ages.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>病院で一時間も待たされた。</span>(I was kept waiting at the hospital for a whole hour)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't help but</span>Used with verbs like 「think」 or 「be moved」, it means \"something makes you can't-help-but ~.\" There's no complaint in it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この映画にはいろいろ考えさせられた。</span>(This movie really made me think about a lot of things)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>話させられる</span>; you can't say 「話さされる」. All verbs ending in 「す」 work this way.<br>・Mark the person making you do it with <b>に</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に作文を書かされた。</span>(I was made to write an essay by the teacher)<br>・It carries a feeling of \"I didn't want to.\" If someone kindly lets you do something and you're grateful, using this form turns it into a complaint and sounds rude.<br>・Don't mix up <span class='gd-jp'>書かされる</span> with the ordinary passive <span class='gd-jp'>書かれる</span>: with the extra 「さ」, the meaning goes from \"be written\" to \"be forced to write.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Tell it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>られる</b> = someone <b>does something to me</b>, and the other person is the doer: <span class='gd-jp'>母に叱られた。</span>(I was scolded by Mom; Mom did the scolding)<br><b>させられる</b> = someone <b>makes me do</b> something, I'm the doer, and I don't want to: <span class='gd-jp'>母に掃除させられた。</span>(I was made to clean by Mom; I did the cleaning)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Now compare 「させてもらう」. It's also someone letting you do something, but the feeling is completely the opposite:<br><b>させてもらう</b> = I want to do it, the other person <b>allows</b> me, and I'm grateful: <span class='gd-jp'>店長に頼んで、休ませてもらった。</span>(I asked the manager, and he let me take the day off)<br><b>させられる</b> = I don't want to, the other person <b>forces</b> me: <span class='gd-jp'>熱があって、店長に無理やり休まされた。</span>(I had a fever, and the manager forced me to go home and rest)</div><div class='gd-warn'>A quick test: ask \"who does the action?\" If the other person does it, it's られる. If I do it reluctantly, it's させられる. If I do it and I wanted to, it's させてもらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>If 「させる」 and 「られる」 still feel shaky, go back and review <span class='gd-jp'>〜させる</span> (causative) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜られる</span> (passive). This grammar point is just those two stacked together.</div>","n3-73":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ても」 means <b>\"even if ~, ... anyway\"</b> or <b>\"no matter ~, ...\"</b>. Whatever happens in the first part, the result in the second part is <b>unaffected</b> and stays the same. It's like saying \"Even if it rains, I'm going\": the rain can't change the fact that you're going.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っても、行きます。</span>(Even if it rains, I'll go)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>高くても、買いたい。</span>(Even if it's expensive, I want to buy it)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change to the て-form first, then add も.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ても</span> (きても)</span><br>For verbs whose て-form ends in 「で」, adding も gives 「でも」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでも</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: replace the final 「い」 with 「くても」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高くても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しくても</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: just add 「でも」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>下手でも</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日曜日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日曜日でも</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料理が下手でも、大丈夫です。</span>(Even if you're not good at cooking, it's fine)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日曜日でも、仕事がある。</span>(Even on Sunday, I have work)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Negative \"even if not ~\"</b>: change ない to なくても. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなくても</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Question word + ても</span>Put \"what, who, where, when\" in front and it becomes \"no matter ~, ...\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何を食べても、おいしい。</span>(No matter what I eat, it's delicious)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつ行っても、混んでいる。</span>(No matter when I go, it's crowded)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度確認しても、ミスが見つからない。</span>(No matter how many times I check, I can't find the mistake)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>どんなに／いくら〜ても</span>\"No matter how much ~, ...\" It stresses that even a high degree makes no difference.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんなに忙しくても、朝ご飯は必ず食べる。</span>(No matter how busy I am, I always eat breakfast)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いくら練習しても、上手にならない。</span>(No matter how much I practice, I don't get any better)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>たとえ〜ても</span>\"Even if ~ really happens, ...\" The tone is firmer and often expresses determination.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>たとえ反対されても、留学したい。</span>(Even if people object, I still want to study abroad)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives take <b>でも</b> directly: <span class='gd-jp'>下手でも</span>, not 「下手くても」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> must become <span class='gd-jp'>よくても</span>, not 「いくても」.<br>・Verbs must go to the て-form first: <span class='gd-jp'>読んでも</span>, not 「読むでも」.<br>・Use ても only when the result goes <b>against</b> what you'd normally expect. A fever going down after taking medicine is the expected result, so you can't say 「薬を飲んでも、熱が下がった」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだら、熱が下がった。</span> (After I took medicine, my fever went down). With ても it would be <span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んでも、熱が下がらない。</span> (Even if I take medicine, my fever doesn't go down)</div><div class='gd-h'>Tell it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same rainy-day outing. Change the grammar and the meaning changes:<br><b>たら</b> = if ~, then ... (the condition holds and the result follows): <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったら、行かない。</span> (If it rains, I won't go)<br><b>ても</b> = even if ~, ... (the condition doesn't change the result): <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っても、行く。</span> (Even if it rains, I'll go)<br><b>のに</b> = even though ~, ... (something that has already happened, with a sense of surprise or dissatisfaction, like \"how come?\"): <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているのに、傘を持たないで出かけた。</span> (Even though it's raining, he went out without an umbrella)</div><div class='gd-sec'>ても can talk about hypotheticals that haven't happened yet, and can be followed by \"let's\" or \"please\": <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っても、行きましょう。</span> (Even if it rains, let's go). のに talks about something that has already happened, so it can't be followed by \"let's\" or \"please.\" That's why you can't say 「雨が降っているのに、行きましょう」.</div><div class='gd-warn'>A quick test: for \"if ~, then ...\" use たら; for \"even if / no matter ~, ...\" use ても; for \"even though ~, ...\" use のに.</div>","n3-74":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たことがある」means <b>\"have done ~ before\" / \"have the experience of ~\"</b>. What matters isn't which day you did something, but whether <b>you have this experience</b> in your life. When a friend asks \"Have you ever tried stinky tofu?\", that's exactly the question being asked.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>富士山に登ったことがある。</span>(I've climbed Mt. Fuji.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本の温泉に入ったことがありますか。</span>(Have you ever been in a Japanese hot spring?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb <b>た-form</b> (the \"〜た\" past form) + <span class='gd-jp'>ことがある</span>.<br>For a politer tone, say <span class='gd-jp'>ことがあります</span>. If you don't have the experience, swap ある for <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べたことがある</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたことがない</span></span> (have never eaten)</div><div class='gd-h'>How it changes: first, identify the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'>The た-form is just the て-form with 「て」 swapped for 「た」, so if you know the て-form, you know the た-form.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: look at the last sound.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳いだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話した</span></span><br>Watch out: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span>, not 行いた.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add 「た」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見た</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>した</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span>（きた）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking about experience</span>Great for starting a conversation. When you answer, you don't need to repeat the whole sentence; 「あります／ありません」 is enough.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>沖縄に行ったことがありますか。</span>(Have you ever been to Okinawa?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい、あります。</span>(Yes, I have.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、一度もありません。</span>(No, never, not even once.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Adding frequency</span>Often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>一度</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何度も</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>一度も〜ない</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店には何度も行ったことがある。</span>(I've been to that shop many times.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>納豆は一度も食べたことがない。</span>(I've never eaten natto, not even once.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A past state</span>Use <span class='gd-jp'>〜ていたことがある</span> to mean \"there was a period in the past when I ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どものころ、大阪に住んでいたことがある。</span>(I lived in Osaka when I was a kid.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Not every English \"I've done ~\" or \"I went\" is this pattern</b>. \"I went to the supermarket yesterday\" just reports something you did yesterday. In Japanese, <span class='gd-jp'>昨日スーパーに行きました</span> is all you need. You can't say 昨日スーパーに行ったことがあります.<br>・<b>Don't use it for things that just happened or are very recent</b>: specific, near time points like <span class='gd-jp'>さっき</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>昨日</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>先週</span> don't fit with \"life experience\". A long stretch of time like <span class='gd-jp'>子どものころ</span> is fine.<br>・<b>Everyday, taken-for-granted things sound odd too</b>: if you say \"I've eaten rice\" or \"I've ridden the subway\", a Japanese person will think, \"Why mention that?\" Use it for experiences that are somewhat special, ones not everyone has.<br>・What comes before it must be the <b>た-form</b>. If you write <span class='gd-jp'>行くことがある</span>, the meaning changes completely (see below).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both use 「ことがある」, but the form before it changes the meaning completely:<br><b>た-form + ことがある</b> = <b>have done it before</b> (past experience)<br><b>Dictionary form + ことがある</b> = <b>sometimes does it</b> (something that happens occasionally)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一人で映画を見に行ったことがある。</span>(I've been to see a movie alone before.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一人で映画を見に行くことがある。</span>(I sometimes go to see a movie alone.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The first sentence only says it has happened at least once, and it may not be something you still do. The second says that you occasionally do it now.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: if you see 「た」 before it, think \"have done ~ before\". If you see the dictionary form, think \"sometimes ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another pattern that starts with the「た-form」is <span class='gd-jp'>〜たばかり</span> (just did ~). It talks about something that happened very recently, which is the opposite of \"experience\", so it helps to learn them together.</div>","n3-75":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことができる」means <b>\"can ~\" / \"be able to ~\"</b>. The meaning is close to the potential form (like <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>行ける</span>), but it sounds fuller and more formal. You'll see it often in announcements, explanations, interviews, and formal situations.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Having a skill (ability)</span>You've learned it and have the ability to do it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はピアノを弾くことができる。</span>(He can play the piano.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Permission, being possible</span>The rules allow it, or the conditions make it possible.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真を撮ることができます。</span>(You can take photos here.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この展示は予約なしで入場することができます。</span>(You can enter this exhibition without a reservation.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use the verb's dictionary form (the original form you'd look up in a dictionary) and add 「ことができる」 right after it. The good news is that <b>you don't need to worry about the verb group</b>; you attach it the same way every time.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話すことができる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べることができる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>することができる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ることができる</span>（くる）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>To say \"can't\" or \"was able to\", change only the final 「できる」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できない</span>(can't)</span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できた</span>(was able to)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns have a pattern too</b>: nouns that stand for an \"activity\", such as a language, a sport, or a skill, can take 「ができる」 directly: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語ができる</span> (can speak Japanese), <span class='gd-jp'>テニスができる</span> (can play tennis).<br>Ordinary object nouns don't work this way. For example, \"can drive\" is <span class='gd-jp'>車の運転ができる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>車を運転することができる</span>; you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>車ができる</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>本ができる</span> means \"the book is finished\", not \"can read books\".</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことができます</span>Shops and notices tell you what you can do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>カードで払うことができます。</span>(You can pay by card.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことはできません</span>The rules say no. In the negative, 「が」 often becomes 「は」, which sounds more like \"this particular thing can't be done\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここでたばこを吸うことはできません。</span>(You can't smoke here.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことができました</span>You finally managed it after some effort.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やっと切符を買うことができました。</span>(I was finally able to buy a ticket.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことができるようになった</span>You couldn't before, but now you can.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>オンラインで申請することができるようになった。</span>(You can now apply online.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Always use the dictionary form before it, never the ます-form: say <span class='gd-jp'>行くことができる</span>, not 行きますことができる.<br>・The 「を」 before the verb stays as it is: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話すことができる</span>. It's when you switch to the potential form that 「を」 often becomes 「が」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せる</span>.<br>・When chatting with friends, the potential form sounds more natural. Asking a friend <span class='gd-jp'>明日来ることができる？</span> sounds stiff; people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>明日来られる？</span> (Can you come tomorrow?).<br>・English \"will\" doesn't always map to ことができる. \"It will rain tomorrow\" is a prediction and has nothing to do with ability, so you say <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るでしょう</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ことができる」and「ことがある」differ by only one word, but the meanings are very different. Compare them using the same action:<br><b>ことができる</b> = can, be able to: <span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べることができる。</span>(I can eat natto.)<br><b>Potential form</b> = same meaning, more casual: <span class='gd-jp'>納豆が食べられる。</span>(I can eat natto.)<br><b>た-form + ことがある</b> = have done it before: <span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べたことがある。</span>(I've eaten natto before.)<br><b>Dictionary form + ことがある</b> = sometimes does it: <span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べることがある。</span>(I sometimes eat natto.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: look at whether it ends in「できる」or「ある」. できる = can or can't; ある = have or haven't (done it before, or sometimes do it).</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adding 「ようになる」 afterward means \"come to be able to ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>日本語で電話することができるようになった。</span>(I've become able to talk on the phone in Japanese.)</div>","n3-78":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜っけ」is what you use when you <b>used to know something and can't recall it right now</b>. It's like saying in English, \"Wait, what time is tomorrow's meeting again? I forgot,\" or \"His birthday is next week, right?\" You're thinking out loud as you ask.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't remember</span>Ask someone, or mutter to yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日のミーティングは何時からだっけ？</span>(What time does tomorrow's meeting start again? I forgot.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あれ、鍵どこに置いたっけ？</span>(Huh, where did I put my keys?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Checking your memory</span>You have an answer in mind and want to make sure you've got it right.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の誕生日は来週だっけ？</span>(His birthday is next week, right?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reminiscing</span>Talking to yourself about the past. The ending doesn't rise, and it has a \"back then...\" feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どものころ、よくこの公園で遊んだっけ。</span>(I used to play in this park a lot as a kid, didn't I.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: add「だ」or「だった」first, then っけ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>明日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>明日だっけ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだったっけ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: to ask about something in the past, use the た form + っけ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言ったっけ</span></span><br>For something that hasn't happened yet,「〜んだっけ」usually sounds more natural.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るんだっけ</span></span> (He's coming, right?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: change い to かった, then add っけ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かったっけ</span></span> (Was that place expensive? I forget.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>To be more polite</b>: say「でしたっけ」or「ましたっけ」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、お名前は何でしたっけ？</span>(Excuse me, your name was... I can't recall it right now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>前に言いましたっけ？</span>(Did I mention that to you before?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't attach っけ directly to a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>明日っけ</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>明日だっけ</span>.<br>・っけ only works if you <b>knew it before</b>. If you're asking a stranger for directions for the first time, you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>駅はどこだっけ？</span>; say <span class='gd-jp'>駅はどこですか。</span><br>・You can use the past form even for something tomorrow: <span class='gd-jp'>明日の会議、何時からだったっけ？</span> The「た」here doesn't mean the meeting is already over. It points to \"that thing I heard earlier.\"<br>・「だっけ」is the casual, friends version. With a boss or customers, switch to「でしたっけ」「ましたっけ」, which you hear a lot at work. Still, skip it in job interviews and formal letters.<br>・Hold the small「っ」for a beat: <span class='gd-jp'>だっけ</span> is pronounced dakke. Without that beat it becomes <span class='gd-jp'>だけ</span>, which means \"only.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to ask a friend what time tomorrow's meeting is:<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日の会議、何時から？</span>: a plain question; you genuinely don't know.<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日の会議、何時からだっけ？</span>: you heard it, but you forgot.<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日の会議、何時からかな。</span>: you're wondering to yourself, and you don't need an answer.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use っけ for \"I used to know it but forgot\"; use a normal question for \"I never knew it.\"</div>","n3-79":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜反面」is the English <b>\"on the other hand\" or \"the flip side\"</b>. You describe one side of something, then the <b>opposite side</b> of that same thing. For example, \"This job pays well, but I'm really busy\": one thing, two opposite sides. That's when you use 反面.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>One good, one bad</span>The most common use: the same person or thing has both strengths and weaknesses.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この仕事は給料がいい反面、休みが少ない。</span>(This job pays well, but there's little time off.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一人暮らしは自由な反面、寂しいこともある。</span>(Living alone gives you freedom, but it can also get lonely.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Mixed feelings</span>You feel two opposite things at once. This is also very common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>卒業はうれしい反面、少し寂しい。</span>(I'm happy to graduate, but I'm also a little sad.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Sentence-initial</span>After finishing one sentence, start the next with「その反面」, meaning \"on the other hand.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>スマホは便利だ。その反面、使いすぎると目が疲れる。</span>(Smartphones are convenient. On the other hand, using them too much tires your eyes.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly; the past form works too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利になった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利になった反面</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>速い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>速い反面</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add「な」, or use the more formal「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利な反面</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>便利である反面</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>有名人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>有名人である反面</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は有名人である反面、自由な時間が少ない。</span>(She's a celebrity, but in exchange she has little free time.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・な-adjectives can't attach directly: say <span class='gd-jp'>便利な反面</span>, not「便利反面」or「便利だ反面」.<br>・Nouns take <span class='gd-jp'>である</span>; don't say「学生な反面」.<br>・反面 describes two sides of <b>the same thing</b>. Using it to compare two different people, like \"my older brother is tall, my younger brother is short,\" sounds odd.<br>・反面 is written and formal, common in essays, news and reports. In casual conversation with friends, plain <span class='gd-jp'>けど</span> is fine: <span class='gd-jp'>給料はいいけど、忙しい。</span> (The pay is good, but I'm busy.)<br>・It's read <b>はんめん</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>反面</b> = two sides of <b>one</b> thing: <span class='gd-jp'>兄は優しい反面、気が弱い。</span> (My older brother is gentle, but on the flip side he's timid.)<br><b>一方で</b> = can describe two sides of one thing, <b>or</b> contrast two different things: <span class='gd-jp'>兄はスポーツが好きな一方で、弟は本が好きだ。</span> (My older brother likes sports, while my younger brother likes books.)<br><b>に対して</b> = used specifically to contrast <b>two different things</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>兄は背が高いのに対して、弟は低い。</span> (My older brother is tall, whereas my younger brother is short.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: one subject, with its good and bad sides, use 反面. Two subjects compared against each other, use 一方で or に対して.<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>一方だ</span> at the end of a sentence means \"more and more...\" It's a different thing from the 「一方で」 here, so don't mix them up.</div>","n3-26":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「きり」 has two faces.<br>① After a noun it means <b>“only ~”</b>, like “just the two of us” or “just this once”.<br>② After a verb's た-form it means <b>“~ and never again”</b>, like “went out and never came back” or “haven't heard from him since”.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only ~</span>noun ＋ きり. The noun is usually a number of people, a number of times, or 「これ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>二人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>二人きり</span></span>(just the two of us)<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>一度</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>一度きり</span></span>(just once)<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>これ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>これきり</span></span>(only this much)<br>Note: <span class='gd-jp'>あれきり</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>それきり</span> usually don't mean “only that one”. They mean “nothing since that time”, which belongs to the second use below.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>二人きりで話したい。</span>(I want to talk, just the two of us.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>チャンスは一度きりだ。</span>(You only get one chance.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お金はこれきりしかない。</span>(This is all the money I have left.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Never again after ~</span>verb た-form ＋ きり. First put the verb in the た-form, then add きり.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出かける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出かけた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出かけたきり</span></span><br>What follows is something that should have happened but never did, so the sentence is often negative. You can also put 「〜たきりだ」 right at the end of a sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は朝出かけたきり、まだ帰ってこない。</span>(My dad left this morning and still hasn't come back.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女とは卒業式で会ったきりだ。</span>(I saw her at the graduation ceremony and haven't seen her since.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あれきり、彼から連絡はない。</span>(I haven't heard from him since then.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>One very common word is <span class='gd-jp'>寝たきり</span> = lay down and never got up again = <b>bedridden</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>祖母は去年から寝たきりだ。</span>(My grandmother has been bedridden since last year.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・In casual speech it's often pronounced <b>っきり</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>二人っきり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>これっきり</span>. The meaning is the same, so don't mistake it for a different word.<br>・「たきり」 can't be followed by “started doing something new”. To say “since joining the company I've been busy every day”, use てから: <span class='gd-jp'>会社に入ってから、毎日忙しい。</span> You can't say 「会社に入ったきり、毎日忙しい」.<br>・きり already means “since then”, so don't stack it with <span class='gd-jp'>以来</span>.<br>・When 「○○きり」 points to a moment in time, that moment was the <b>last time</b>. It doesn't mean it never happened before. <span class='gd-jp'>卒業式で会ったきり</span> means “the graduation ceremony was the last time we met”, not “we only met that once”.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>きり vs だけ</b>: Both can be translated as “only”, but きり attaches only to words like numbers of people, counts, and これ, and it often carries a feel of “no one else, that's all”.<br><span class='gd-jp'>一人だけ来た。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>一人きりだ。</span>(Only one person came. / It's just me alone.) Both are fine here.<br><span class='gd-jp'>りんごだけ食べた。</span>(I ate only an apple.) With ordinary things like this, you can't swap in きり.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たきり vs っぱなし</b>: Both carry a feel of “nothing more after that”, but the focus differs.<br><span class='gd-jp'>テレビをつけっぱなしで寝てしまった。</span>(I fell asleep with the TV left on.): The finishing step (turning it off) wasn't done, so the state just stays as it is.<br><span class='gd-jp'>兄は東京へ行ったきり、帰ってこない。</span>(My older brother went to Tokyo and never came back.): After that event, nothing that should have followed ever happened.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: “only these people / these few times” → noun ＋ きり; “after that time, never again ~” → た-form ＋ きり; “left on, left as it is” → っぱなし.</div>"});
